�। जहां तक \nएनआरआईज़ क� संख्या का सवाल है, हम अलग से उसक� गणना नह�ं करते। \n*105. [The questioner Shri Kalraj Mishra was absent. For answer vide \npage 23 Infra] \nमध्य प्रदे श म� �वमानपत्तन� का �वकास \n*106. श्रीमती सुषमा स्वराज: क्या नागर �वमानन मंत्री यह बताने क� कृ पा कर�गे �क: \n(क) क्या मध्य प्रदे श म� भोपाल, खजुराहो और इंदौर के �वमानपत्तन� को अंतरार्ष्ट्र�य स्तर का \n\n 41 \nबनाने के �लए अपे��त भू�म का अ�धग्रहण �कया गया है; \n(ख) य�द हां, तो भू�म अ�धग्रहण करने के बाद इन �वमानपत्तन� पर �कए जाने वाले �वकास \nसंबंधी काय� क� अद्यतन िस्थ�त क्या है; और \n\n 42 \n(ग) क्या इन �वमानपत्तन� को अंतरार्ष्ट्र�य स्तर का बनाने के �लए कोई समय-सीमा �नधार्�रत क� \nगई है? \nनागर �वमानन मंत्रालय के राज्य मंत्री (श्री प्रफु ल्ल पटे ल): (क) से (ग) एक �ववरण सदन के \nपटल पर रखा गया है। \n�ववरण \n(क) भोपाल और इंदौर हवाईअड्ड� के �वकास के �लए भू�म का अ�धग्रहण कर �लया गया है। \nखजुराहो हवाई अड्डे के �वकास के �लए, इकाओ के मापदं ड� के अनुसार बे�सक िस्ट्रप के प्रावधान के \n�लए आवश्यक अ�त�रक्त भू�म का अ�धग्रहण अभी �कया जाना है। \n(ख) और (ग) 1. भोपाल  हवाईअड्डे पर, 500 अंतद� शीय तथा 200 अंतरार्ष्ट्र�य या�त्रय� क� \nआवश्यकताओं क� पू�तर् के �लए नए एक�कृ त ट�मर्नल भवन का �नमार्ण कायर् �कया जा चुका है, िजसक े \n�दसम्बर 2009 तक पूरा होने क� संभावना है, रनवे का 2745 मीटर तक �वस्तार तथा सुदृढ़�करण से \nसंबं�धत कायर् और इससे संबं�धत \nअन्य कायर् भी �दए जा चुके ह�, िजनक� जुलाई 2009 तक पूरा होने \nक� संभावना है तथा नए एप्रन के �नमार्ण तथा संबं�धत काय� को भी �दसम्बर 2009 तक पूरा करने क� \nयोजना बनाई गई है। \n2. खजुराहो हवाई अड्डे पर 500 अंतद� शीय तथा 100 अंतरार्ष्ट्र�य या�त्रय� क� आवश्यकताओं क� \nपू�तर् के �लए एक एक�कृ त ट�मर्नल भवन के �नमार्ण का कायर् शेड्यूल के अनुसार चल रहा है, रनवे का \n2286 मीटर तक �वस्तार �कए जाने से संबं�धत कायर् और नए एप्रन का �नमार्ण कायर् पूरा कर �लया \nगया है। \n3. इन्दौर हवाईअड्डे पर, 500 अंतद� शीय तथा 200 अंतरार्ष्ट्र�य या�त्रय� क� आवश्यकताओं क� \nपू�तर् के �लए एक एक�कृ त ट�मर्नल भवन के �नमार्ण का कायर् �दया जा चुका है, िजसक े �दसम्बर 2009 \nतक पूरा होने क� संभावना है, मौजूदा रनवे का 2750 मीटर का �वस्तार �कए जाने से संबं�धत कायर् \nप्रग�त पर है िजसक े अगस्त 2009 तक पूरा होने क� संभावना है तथा नए एप्रन का �नमार्ण भी प्रग�त \nपर है िजसक े माचर् 2009 तक पूरा होने क� संभावना है। \nDevelopment of airports in Madhya Pradesh \n†*106. SHRIMATI SUSHMA SWARAJ: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the land required for the airports of Bhopal, Khajuraho \nand Indore in Madhya Pradesh has been acquired to make them airports of \ninternational standard; \n(b) if so, the latest status of development work at these airports \nafter acquiring the land; and \n(c) whether any time limit has been fixed to make these airports of \ninternational standard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \n\n 43 \nPATEL): (a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \nStatement  \n(a) Land has been acquired for development of Bhopal and Indore \nairports. For development of Khajuraho airport, additional land required \nfor provision of basic strip as per ICAO norms is yet to be acquired, \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 44 \n(b) and (c) 1. At Bhopal airport, the work of construction of new \nintegrated terminal building to cater to 500 domestic and 200 \ninternational pax. has been awarded with projected date of completion by \nDecember, 2009; the work related to extension and strengthening of runway \nto 2745m and associated work has also been awarded with projected date of \ncompletion by July, 2009 and the work related to construction of new \napron and associated works has also been planned by December, 2009. \n2. At Khajuraho airport, the progress of work related to construction \nof new integrated terminal building to cater to 500 domestic and 100 \ninternational pax is as per schedule; the work related to extension of \nrunway upto 2286m and new apron has been completed. \n3. At Indore airport, the work related to construction of new \nintegrated terminal building to cater to 500 domestic and 200 \ninternational pax. has been awarded with projected date of completion by \nDecember, 2009; the work related to extension of existing runway to 2750m \nis underway with projected date of completion by August, 2009 and \nconstruction of new apron is also underway with projected date of \ncompletion by March, 2009. \nश्रीमती सुषमा स्वराज: सभाप�त जी, अगर म� मंत्री महोदय का �ल�खत उत्तर पढ़ती हूं तो \nसंतोषजनक लगता है। ले�कन म� मंत्री जी को बताना चाहूंगी �क जमीनी हक�कत इससे बहुत अलग है। \nआपने कहा �क जुलाई तक भोपाल के रन-वे का �वस्तार हो जाएगा और बाक� सारा काम �दसम्बर, \n2009 तक पूरा करने क� योजना बनाई है। मेरा भोपाल अकसर आना-जाना\n होता है। \n...(व्यवधान)... नीचे क� जो ग�त है, वह बहुत धीमी है। इस�लए म� आपसे जानना चाहती हूं �क \nक्या आप कोई ऐसी स�म�त बनाते ह�, जो यह दे खे �क वहां जो काम हो रहा है, वह �कस रफ्तार पर \nहो रहा है? अगर वहां समयाव�ध के बीच म� काम नह�ं हो पाता तो उ स के �लए आप क्या करते ह�? क्या \nआपने ऐसी कोई स�म�त बनाई है? \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Question Hour is getting over. \nश्रीमती सुषमा स्वराज: सर, म� के व ल सवाल ह� पूछवाना चाहती हूं। \nश्री प्रफु ल्ल पटे ल: सर, म� सुषमा जी को के व ल यह कहना चाहता हूं �क म� परस� ह� भोपाल म� था \nऔर वहां िजस पैमाने पर काम हाथ म� �लया गया है, वह काफ� व्यापक है। जहां तक रन-वे के बाक� \nकाम क� बात है, इसम� इस�लए �वलंब हो रहा था �क इ स के �लए जो जमीन हस्तांत�रत होनी थी, \nउसम� �वलंब हुआ था। उसम� राज्य सरकार ने भी सहयोग प्रदान �कया। अब वह जमीन हमारे अधीन आ \nगई है। म� आपको �वश्वास �दलाता हूं �क इस काम को हम समय-सीमा के भीतर पूरा कर�गे। \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Question Hour is over. \n______ \nWRITTEN ANSWERS TO STARRED QUESTIONS \nPNG connection \n\n 45 \n*105. SHRI KALRAJ MISHRA: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether consequent upon installation of Piped Natural Gas (PNG) \nconnection, a consumer is required to surrender LPG connection to the \nformer supplier alongwith both LPG cylinders; \n\n 46 \n(b) whether there had been instances when the PNG is not available \nround the clock, with the result that one has to make alternative \narrangement for cooking; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that the LPG suppliers do not accept the \nsurrender of one cylinder by the consumer; \n(d) if so, the reasons therefor; and \n(e) what measures Government proposes to remedy the situation? \nTHE MINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI MURLI DEORA): (a) \nGovernment has conveyed approval to the public sector Oil Marketing \nCompanies (OMCs) to formulate a scheme for surrendering LPG connection by \na customer who is being supplied Piped Natural Gas (PNG) whereby the LPG \ncustomer can keep the LPG connection in safe custody, that is, in case, \nthe customer changes residence to an area not covered by PNG, the \ncustomer can avail a new LPG connection at the surrendered security \ndeposit rate on priority basis. OMCs are in the process of finalizing the \nscheme. \n(b) PNG suppliers have reported some instances of PNG not being \navailable for short durations on account of damage to pipeline network by \nthird parties on account of excavation activities. Efforts are made to \nresume supplies of PNG in such cases at the earliest. \n(c) to (e) Does not arise in view of (a) above. \nTourism development projects in Bihar and Uttar Pradesh \n*107. SHRI NAND KISHORE YADAV: \n SHRI KAMAL AKHTAR: \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the tourism development projects sanctioned for \nUttar Pradesh and Bihar during the last two years, till date and the \nfunds sanctioned or released for each of these projects, district-wise; \n(b) the details of the funds spent, year-wise and State-wise; \n(c) whether any proposal from Uttar Pradesh and Bihar is pending for \nclearance; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) and (b) The \ndetails of the tourism development projects received and sanctioned for \nUttar Pradesh and Bihar during the last two years (2006- 07 & 2007- 08) \ntill date are enclosed as S tatement (See below). The State Government of \n\n 47 \nUttar Pradesh was sanctioned Rs.91.45 crore during the years 2006- 07 to \n2008-09 (till January, 09) against which the State has spent Rs. 13.66 \ncrore. \nThe State Government of Bihar was sanctioned Rs. 64.05 crore during \nthe years 2006- 07 to 2008- 09 (till January, 2009) against which \nutilization has been Rs. 4.96 crore. \n\n 48 \n(c) and (d) The Ministry of Tourism, Government of India, extends \nCentral Finance Assistance under its various Plan Schemes, to the \nproposals received from the State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations, which are complete in all respects as per the scheme \nguidelines subject to inter - se priority and availability of funds. \nStatement \nDetails of the tourism development projects received and sanctioned for \nUttar  \nPradesh and Bihar during last two years till date \n(Rs. in lakh) \nS.No. Name of projects Amount \n  Sanctioned \n1           2 3 \n Bihar \n 2006-07 \n1 Development of Tourist circuit Bodhgaya-Rajgir-Nalanda  1922.42 \n under major project. \n2 Information Technology – Desktop computer, HP Laser Jet 14.87 \n 5200+ \n 2007-08 \n1 Development of Tourist spot Vishnupad Temple and Pilgrim 321.81 \n Centre in Gaya \n2 Development of Ravelganj, Chhapra 464.01 \n3 Construction of Huen Tsang Gram/Sanskritik Gram for Nav 408.93 \n Nalanda Mahavir at Nalanda \n4 State IHM at Bodh-Gaya 1000.00 \n 2008-09 \n1 Development of Vaishali (Kolhua) 388.97 \n2 Development of Lauriya Nandan Garh, Areraj & Rampurwa as 134.87 \n a Tourist destination. \n3 Development of infrastructure at Pragbodhi (Dungeswari) Hills,\n 156.50 \n Bodh-Gaya. \n4 Development of Tourism infrastructure at Sujata Kuti, Bakror, 158.40 \n Bodh-Gaya \n5 Development of Antichak at Vikramshila, Bhagalpur 173.71 \n6 Riverfront Development and Beautification work at Sonepur 297.66 \n7 Development of Shershah Fort, Sasaram Bihar Received during  \n  January, 2009 \n8 Development of Rohtas Fort Distt. Rohtas, Bihar Received during  \n  January, 2009 \n9 Development of Niranjana River front and Parikrama Path from268.27 \n Mahabodhi Temple to Pragbodhi, Bodh-Gaya \n\n 49 \n1           2 3 \n10 Development of Tourism infrastructure at Bhaluni Dham 106.63 \n Temple at Rohtas. \n11 Development of Tourism infrastructure at Ular Sun Temple at113.44 \n Patna. \n12. FCI Muzzafarpur 475.00 \n Uttar Pradesh \n 2006-07 \n1 Development of Brij Chaurasi kos Parikrama Tourism Circuit-441.53 \n (Madhurvan Maholi-Jatipura Goverdhan-Vrindadavan-Maha  \n Vidya) in Mathura \n2 Integrated Development of Mahabharat Tourist circuit-Bagpat-445.63 \n Bawana-Garhmukteshwar \n3 Budget for revitalization of Varanasi as a special Tourism786.00 \n Destination in Varanasi. \n4 Integrated Development of Agra-Revitalization and visitor 758.00 \n management for Taj Mahal in Agra. \n5 Development of Various places in Aligarh District. 497.04 \n6 Development of Simoni Tourist Complex, Banda. 397.86 \n7 Celebration of Deo Deepawali Festival at Varanasi 3.00 \n 2007-08 \n1 Dev. of Barsana in Distt. Mathura 364.08 \n2 Dev. of Chitrakoot as a Destination 444.49 \n3 Dev. of Jaunpur destination 444.10 \n4 Dev. of Sidheshwar Nath Dham, Distt. Sant Kabirnagar 141.96 \n5 Organization of Taj Mahotasav at Agra-2008 5.00 \n6 FCI Rae Bareli 475.00 \n7 FCI Garhmukteshwar 475.00 \n8 IHM Fursatganj 1000.00 \n9 Development of Rural Tourism at village Bhaguwala 50.00 \n 2008-09 \n1 Dev. of Varanasi -Sarnath-Ramnagar Ph-II 1416.31 \n2. Development of Amethi as a destination. 576.71 \n3 Dev. of Sultanpur as a Tourist destination. 423.13 \n4 Development of Pratapgarh (Ghuisarnathdham) Received in  \n  Feb., 2009 \n5. Development of Rai Bareli Received in  \n  Feb., 2009. \n\n 50 \nDevelopment of Tourism in Karnataka \n*108. SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR: Will the Minister of TOURISM be \npleased to state: \n(a) the number of proposals submitted by the State Government of \nKarnataka for the development of tourism in that State and pending at \npresent for clearance by the Centre; \n(b) the details of each proposal; and \n(c) the number, out of them, likely to be cleared by the end of 2009-\n10 with details of the Central assistance to be provided in each case? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) to (c) Development \nand promotion of places of tourist interests is primarily the \nresponsibility of State Governments/Union Territory Administrations \nincluding Karnataka. However, the Ministry of Tourism provides central \nfinancial assistance for the project proposals submitted by the State \nGovernments/UT Administrations under various schemes. \nDuring 2008-09, the following project proposals have been received in \nthe Ministry of Tourism from the Govt. of Karnataka: \nSl.No. Name of the project \n1. Celebration of Bangalooru Habba-2008 \n2. Development of tourism infrastructure at Hampi World Heritage \nsite as a mega project \n3. Tourist infrastructure and development works at Sangama \n(Mekedatu) Ramanagara District \n4. Development of infrastructure facilities at Shanthisagar Lake, \nChennagiri Taluk Davanagere District \n5. Development of Eco tourism Park at Kaginele, Haveri District \n6. Destination development works under Restoration of Magadi \nFort, Magadi Town, Bangalore \nProject proposals that are complete, are appraised as per scheme \nguidelines on inter- se-priority and funds sanctioned, subject to \navailability under the respective head. The following proposals, which \nwere complete as per guidelines, have been sanctioned in the current \nfinancial year for the Government of Karnataka: \nSl. No. Name of the project Amount sanctioned  \n  (Rs. in lakh) \n1. Celebration of Bangalooru Habba-2008 15.00 \n2. Development of tourism infrastructure at Hampi 3283.58 \n World Heritage site as a mega project \n3. Tourist infrastructure and development works at 474.63 \n Sangama (Mekedatu) Ramanagara District \n4. Development of infrastructure facilities at Shanthisagar 500.00 \n Lake, Chennagiri Taluk Davanagere District \n TOTAL 4273.21 \n\n 51 \nScam in Satyam Computers \n†*109. SHRI MOTILAL VORA: Will the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be \npleased to state: \n(a) the date on which the scam in Satyam computers had come to the \nnotice of Government; \n(b) the loss incurred by the company due to manipulation of records by \nthe managers; \n(c) whether the auditors of the company have been found responsible \nfor this; \n(d) the action taken by Government against the culprits; \n(e) the steps taken by Government to protect the interests of the \ninvestors; and \n(f) the steps taken by Government to check this kind of cheating with \nthe investors by other companies in future? \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): (a) January \n7, 2009. \n(b) On the statement by Shri B. Ramalinga Raju, the former chairman of \nSatyam Computer Services Limited, as to the presence of misstatements and \nfalsification in the published financial statements of the Company, the \nCompany suffered in terms of loss of credibility before its stakeholders, \nincluding its shareholders, employees and customers in a manner which \njeopardized the future continuity of its business and operations and its \nvery survival. In view of complex nature of the issues involved, \nfinancial losses incurred due to the said event and its consequences \ncannot be quantified. \n(c) to (e) The Ministry has ordered investigation into the affairs of \nthe Company under the Companies Act, 1956, by officers drawn from the \nSFIO, to ascertain factual position including the role of its statutory \nauditors and the investigation is under progress. The Ministry has also \nasked the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI) to look into \nthe matter with regard to the financial reporting by the company, the \nrole and manner of discharge of the statutory functions by its auditors, \ncompliance with the accounting and auditing standards as well as the \nprovisions of the Companies Act, 1956. In addition, investigations are \nalso being carried out on by CBI, SEBI, Income Tax Authorities, etc. The \nGovernment has also suspended the previous Board of Directors and \n\n 52 \nappointed six new Directors on the Board of the Company with the due \napproval of Company Law Board given vide its order dated 9.1.2009. The \nnew Board is taking all possible steps to continue the operations and the \nbusiness of the Company and to safeguard the interests of all its \nstakeholders. \n(f) Disclosures about the affairs of companies intended to inform the \nstakeholder the truth about the state of affairs of companies, have been \nmandated under the Companies Act, 1956. To facilitate making of such \ndisclosures by companies, and for stakeholders and regulatory agencies to \neasily  access  and  view  them, Government has set up an electronic \nregistry with  round  the  clock  \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 53 \naccess through internet. The Government has powers of inspection of the \nbooks of accounts of companies and also to investigate their affairs, if \nneed be, under the Companies Act, 1956. In addition, the Act provides for \nappointment of independent, statutory auditors to audit the accounts and \nreport to the shareholders. Such audited accounts are also displayed on \nthe electronic registry for general viewing. While the reporting \nrequirements are regulated under the Companies Act, 1956, the conduct of \nauditors is regulated under the Chartered Accountants Act, 1949. In \naddition, for listed companies, compliance with these statutory \nrequirements is required to be certified by a company secretary in \npractice, who in turn is regulated under the Company Secretaries Act, \n1980. Government has amended the Chartered Accountants Act, 1949 and the \nCompany Secretaries Act, 1980 in 2006 to provide for a more effective \ndisciplinary mechanism to deal with cases of misconduct by Chartered \nAccountants, Company Secretaries respectively. In 2006, the Government \nhas notified Accounting Standards to enable accounts of companies to be \ndrawn up and disclosed on the basis of fair, transparent and \ninternationally accepted principles. The Government has also introduced \nCompanies Bill, 2008 in the Lok Sabha to provide for a comprehensive \nrevision of the Companies Act, 1956 including, inter alia, provisions \nthat would enable more effective checks against irregularities/frauds by \ncompanies. \nInvestment by oil companies \n†*110. SHRI RAM JETHMALANI: \n   SHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Planning Commission has expressed \nits serious concern over the investment of public assets in other sectors \nand investment to create special capacities by the public sector oil \ncompanies of the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that there is neither any monitoring \nmechanism nor any directive or guidelines of Government on investments \nbeing made by these oil companies; \n(d) if so, the detail thereof; and \n(e) whether Government has taken any perspective action keeping in \nview the concerns of the Planning Commission? \nTHE MINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI MURLI DEORA): (a) and \n\n 54 \n(b) During finalization of Annual Plan proposals of public sector oil \ncompanies for 2009-10, the Planning Commission had, inter alia, observed \nthat with global demand projected to fall, India's private sector \nrefineries are seeking entry into the domestic market. As a result, \ndomestic public sector refineries will be made to compete, resulting in \nconsiderable under utilization of domestic public sector refining  \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 55 \ncapacity. They also stressed on the need for developing pipelines and \nrelated facilities at ports, tank farms, storages, marketing depots, etc. \nas common carriers with regulated usage charges. \n(c) and (d) Investment decisions are taken by the public sector oil \ncompanies at the level of their Board of Directors under the powers \ndelegated by the Government to Navaratna/Miniratna companies. Public \nsector oil companies do not receive any Budgetary support from the \nGovernment and finance their projects entirely from their own resources. \nHowever, Government monitors the implementation of various projects of \noil companies on a regular basis and a Ministry Monitoring Cell has also \nbeen set up for this purpose. \n(e) Refining Industry in India was delicensed in June, 1998. Capacity \nexpansions of public sector refineries are undertaken based on techno-\neconomic feasibility and projections for future growth in domestic \nconsumption. The existing public sector refining capacity at 105.47 MMTPA \nis still below the estimated domestic consumption of petroleum products \nof about 133 MMTPA during 2008- 09. Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) are \nresorting to imports, in addition to purchases from refineries in the \nprivate sector, to meet the demand gap. Regarding development of \npipelines and other infrastructure as common carriers, the public sector \noil companies are already attaching utmost importance to sharing their \nindividual infrastructures with each other to optimise costs as well as \nto ensure supplies throughout the country. \nImpact of climate change on water resources \n*111. SHRIMATI MOHSINA KIDWAI: Will the Minister of WATER \nRESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government has established cells and resource centres to \nstudy the impact of climate change on water resources; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether Government has conducted any study on impact of climate \nchange on water management in the country; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES (SHRI JAY \nPRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) to (d) Cells have been created in Central \nWater Commission (CWC), Brahmaputra Board (BB) and National Institute of \nHydrology (NIH) to study and examine the impact of climate change on \nwater resources. Reputed academic institutions have also been actively \nassociated with the studies. Ministry of Water Resources has established \nprofessorial chairs at Indian Institute of Technology (IIT) Guwahati, IIT \n\n 56 \nKanpur, IIT Kharagpur, IIT Roorkee, National Institute of Technology \n(NIT) Patna and NIT Srinagar. \nPreliminary studies related to impact of climate change on water \nresources have been conducted by NIH in respect of specific areas, which \nincludes (a) Impact Assessment of Climate Change on the Hydrological \nResponse of a Snow and Glacier Melt Runoff dominated Himalayan River \n(Spiti River) (b)  Hydrological Sensitivity of a Large Himalayan Basin to \nClimate Change (Sutlej Basin) (c) Effect  \n\n 57 \nof Climate Change on Runoff of a Glacierized Himalayan Basin (Dokriani \nGlacier Basin) (d) Effect of Climatic Variation on Runoff Regime of River \nChenab (e) Changes in Rainfall and relative humidity in different river \nbasins in the Northwest and Central India. \nUnder recoveries of OMC \n*112. SHRI MOINUL HASSAN: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) are to recover their \nso-called under recoveries; \n(b) whether the term 'under recoveries' is shown in profit and loss \naccount of petroleum companies; and \n(c) the details of the figure of Rs.2,45,000 crore shown as losses for \npublic consumption and under recoveries in official records? \nTHE MINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI MURLI DEORA): (a) to \n(c) The Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) pay Trade Parity \nprice to refineries for buying Petrol/Diesel and Import Parity Price for \nPDS Kerosene/ Domestic LPG. The actual retail selling prices (RSPs) of \nthese products are, however, modulated by the Government in order to \nprotect the interests of the consumers. The difference between the \nrequired price based on Trade Parity / Import Parity and the actual RSPs \nrealized represents the under-recoveries of the OMCs. \nThe under-recoveries, as computed above are different from the actual \nprofits and losses of the OMCs as per their published results. The latter \ntake into account other income streams like dividend income, pipeline \nincome, profits from freely priced products and refining margins in the \ncase of integrated companies. \nIn June 2008, the under -recoveries of the OMCs on the sale of the \nabovesaid four sensitive petroleum products were estimated at Rs.2,45,305 \ncrore for 2008-09 based on the then international oil prices and the RSPs \nprevailing before 5th June, 2008, as per the details given below: \n(Rs. crore) \nPetroleum Products Under-Recoveries \nPetrol 25,000 \nDiesel 1,55,264 \nPDS Kerosene 39,026 \nDomestic LPG 26,015 \nTOTAL : 2,45,305 \n\n 58 \nThe fall in the international oil prices has, however, provided some \nrelief to the OMCs. The gross under- recoveries for 2008- 09, which were \nprojected at Rs.2,45,000 crore in June 2008, are now estimated to come \ndown to around Rs. 1,03,908 crore. \nIn the revised Burden Sharing Mechanism for 2008- 09, Government has \ndecided that after accounting for contributions from the Public Sector \nUpstream Oil Companies of Rs.32,000 crore, the  \n\n 59 \nremaining under- recovery during the year 2008- 09 would be compensated \nthrough issue of Oil Bonds by the Government. \nProduction and marketing of coal bed methane \n*113. SHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether certain coal blocks have been awarded to various agencies \nviz. Great Eastern Energy Corporation (GEEC), Essar Exploration & \nProducts Ltd. (EEPL) Oil and Natural Gas Corporation (ONGC) Gas Authority \nof India Ltd. (GAIL) etc. to explore the availability of Coal Bed Methane \n(CBM) in various coal-fields under Coal India Ltd. (CIL); and \n(b) if so, the details thereof, the CBM potential of each of the \nblocks and the present status with regard to production and marketing of \nCBM? \nTHE MINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI MURLI DEORA): (a) and \n(b) Government of India has awarded 26 Coal Bed Methane (CBM) blocks so \nfar to different companies. Details of CBM blocks awarded along with \ntheir present status is given in the S tatement (S ee below). The \nCommercial Production of Coal Bed Methane commenced in Raniganj block \nsince July 2007. The current production of CBM is about 68000 Standard \nCubic Meter per Day (SCMD). The Gas is being sold to local Industries and \nalso being utilized as Compressed Natural Gas (CNG). \nStatement \nDetails of CBM blocks awarded by the Government to different companies \nCompany-wise Status of CBM Blocks as on January, 2009 \nSl.  Coal  Block  Consortium Date Area Resource\n Remarks  \nNo. Field Name (Participating signing (Sq. Billion  \n   of Interest)   KM) Cubic  \n   PI %   Meter  \n      (BCM) \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 \nOn Nomination Basis \n1 Raniganj  Raniganj  Oil & Natural 6.2.2003350 43\n Exploration  \n North North Gas Corporation     Stage \n   Ltd. (74) &  \n   Coal India  \n   Ltd. (26) \n2 Jharia Jharia Oil & Natural 6.2.2003 85 85 Exploration  \n   Gas Corporation    Stage \n   Ltd. (90) &  \n   Coal India  \n   Ltd. (10) \n\n 60 \nAwarded through Foreign Investment Promotion Board (FIPB) route \n3 Raniganj  Raniganj  Great Eastern 31.5.2001 210 28\n Reserves \n South South Energy    \n established: \n   Corporation Ltd.     38.78 \n  \n   (100)    BCM \n\n 61 \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 \n       CBM  \n       Production \n@  \n       68000 Cubic  \n       Meters per \n       Day \nCBM-I Round \n4 Raniganj  RG (East)- Essar Oil 26.7.2002500 42\n Exploration  \n East CBM-