 \n   Ltd.(40)-GAIL \n   (India) Ltd. (40)- \n   EIG Energy  \n   Infrastructure Group  \n   AB(10)- Tata Power  \n   Company Ltd.(l0) \n18 Birbhum BB-CBM- BP Exploration  16.11.2006 248 50 Exploration  \n  2005/III Alpha Ltd. (100)    Stage \n   \n19 Tatapani  TR-CBM- Arrow Energy 7.11.2006 458 53.78\n Exploration  \n Ramkola 2005/III India Pvt.    Stage \n   Ltd.(35)-GAIL \n   (India) Ltd(35)- \n   EIG Energy  \n   Infrastructure Group  \n   AB(15)- Tata Power  \n   Company Ltd.(15) \n20 Mand  MR-CBM- Arrow Energy  7.11.2006 634 19.9 Exploration  \n Raigarh 2005/III India Pvt. Ltd.(40)-    Stage \n   GAIL(India) Ltd. (45)- \n   EIG Energy  \n   Infrastructure  \n   Group AB (15) \n21 Sohagpur SP(N)- Reliance Energy 7.11.2006 609 16.72 Exploration  \n  CBM- Ltd.(45).-    Stage \n  2005/III Reliance Natural  \n   Resources Ltd. (45).-  \n   Geopetrol International  \n   Inc.(10). \n\n 64 \n22 Singrauli SR(N)- Coalgas Mart 7.11.2006 330 31\n Exploration  \n  CBM- LLC.(10)-Deep    Stage \n  2005/III Industries Ltd.(90) \n\n 65 \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 \n23 Kotha- KG(E)- Reliance Energy 7.11.2006 750 57.2 Exploration  \n gudem CBM- Ltd.(45).-    Stage \n  2005/III Reliance Natural  \n   Resources Ltd.(45)-  \n   Geopetrol International  \n   Inc.(10) \n24 Barmer BS(4)- Reliance Energy 7.11.2006 1168 82 Exploration  \n  CBM- Ltd.(45)-    Stage \n  2005/III Reliance Natural  \n   Resources Ltd. (45).-  \n   Geopetrol International   \n   Inc.(10) \n25 Barmer BS(5)- Reliance Energy Ltd 7.11.2006 739 38\n Exploration  \n  CBM- (45).-    Stage \n  2005/111 Reliance Natural  \n   Resources Ltd.(45)-  \n   Geopetrol International  \n   Inc.(10) \n26 Godavari GV(N)- Coalgas Mart  7.11.2006 386 29.65 Exploration  \n  CBM- LLC(10).-Deep    Stage \n  2005/III Industries Ltd.(40)- \n   Adinath Exim  \n   Resources Ltd.(50) \n TOTAL : 26 Blocks   13600 1354.45 Reserves  \n      \n established:  \n       174.58 BCM  \n       CBM  \n       Production \n@  \n       68000 Cubic  \n       Meters per  \n       Day \nEthanol from Brazil \n†114. SHRI SHREEGOPAL VYAS: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Indian Oil Companies are looking to \nBrazil for ethanol; \n(b) whether sugar mills in the country are in a bad shape due to the \nabove policy; and \n(c) the details of measures being adopted to tackle this situation? \n\n 66 \nTHE MINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI MURLI DEORA): (a) Oil \nMarketing Companies (OMCs) had explored the possibilities of acquiring \nsugarcane acreage and putting up ethanol manufacturing units in Brazil. \nThe Project was found to be feasible and strategic for Indian Oil  \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 67 \nIndustry. However, in view of economic slow down & resource crunch, OMCs \nare not contemplating any investment in Brazil to set up an ethanol \nproject at present. Further, the OMCs are not looking at import of \nethanol from Brazil into India. \n(b) and (c) No, Sir. Since the scheme is not implemented, the question \nof sugar mills in the country being in a bad shape due to the above \npolicy does not arise. \nImpact of use of branded petrol \n*115. SHRI KAMAL AKHTAR: \n SHRI NAND KISHORE YADAV: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to refer to \nanswer to Unstarred Question 2432 given in the Rajya Sabha on 15th April, \n2008 and state: \n(a) whether his Ministry has conducted any study on the rate of \nprotection, the branded fuels are providing to the vehicles and the \nextent of reduction in pollution level by these branded fuels; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any study report regarding the non- wastage of fuel based \non these advertisements is with him; and \n(d) if not, the basis of his claim thereon? \nTHE MINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI MURLI DEORA): (a) and \n(d) Branded fuel is manufactured by addition of multifunctional additives \nto normal fuel. The data on these branded fuels has been evaluated \nextensively. As per test report, the branded fuel reduces carbon monoxide \nand hydrocarbon emissions considerably, improves engine performance and \nalso improves fuel economy. Branded fuel substantially reduces the \ndeposits on intake valve in the engine, leading to lower maintenance cost \nand better drivability and pick-up. \nFAA'S Notice regarding safety norms \n*116. SHRI DHARAM PAL SABHARWAL: \n SHRI AMAR SINGH: \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Directorate General of Civil Aviation have received a \nnotice from USA's Federation Aviation Administration (FAA) to reassess \nthe precarious staff strength to ensure flight safety norms; \n(b) if so, the details of the FAA notice and the action taken by the \nDGCA; \n\n 68 \n(c) whether FAA is likely to downgrade India from Level- 1 to Level-3 \nresulting in curtailment of flights to and from USA; and \n(d) if so, what steps Government have taken to strengthen the DGCA as \nper FAA norms? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) and  (b)  The Director General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) has \nreceived communication from USA's  \n\n 69 \nFederal Aviation Administration (FAA) regarding India's Aviation Safety \nOversight System under the International Aviation Safety Assessment \n(IASA) programme of FAA. International Civil Aviation Organisation (ICAO) \nhad conducted the audit of India in October 2006 wherein they observed \nshortage of manpower, which affects its performance. DGCA has started \npreparing for the reassessment with the available resources and has also \ninitiated interim short term, medium term and long term steps to overcome \nthe shortage of manpower by hiring/appointing experts on secondment to \naugment the strength of DGCA. \n(c) and (d) Presently, India is in Category I. However, various short \nterm and long term measures have been initiated with ICAO assistance to \nstrengthen DGCA to avoid any downgrading in FAA reassessment. A plan \noutlay of Rs.2.00 crore has been estimated during the year 2009- 10 for \nthese National Projects of ICAO. \nDispute of completion of Kusaha embankment \n*117. SHRI MAHENDRA MOHAN: \n SHRIMATI SHOBHANA BHARTIA: \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government is aware that the breach in the 1.7 km long \nKusaha embankment in Nepal caused widespread floods in Bihar during the \nlast year; \n(b) if so, whether the Central Government and the State Government of \nBihar are disputing each other over completion of work to plug the 1.7 km \nlong breach of the Kusaha embankment in Nepal; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the steps taken by Government to ensure that in future such breach \nin embankments does not occur? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES (SHRI JAY \nPRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) Yes Sir. \n(b) No, Sir. The State Government of Bihar constituted a team under \nShri N. Sanyal, the retired Engineer- in-Chief and former Chairman, GFCC \nto advise the Government on Kosi Breach closure in which Member \n(Coordination), Ganga Flood Control Commission, is a Member from the \nGovernment of India. The work of breach closure is going on as per the \nadvice of Kosi Breach Closure Advisory Team (KBCAT). The Central \nGovernment is providing all technical assistance and total cost of the \nclosure of breach and related protection works would be borne by the \nGovernment of India. \n(c) Does not arise. \n\n 70 \n(d) A High Level Expert Team (HLET) was constituted by Ministry of \nWater Resources under the C hairmanship of Chairman, Central Water \nCommission (CWC) to suggest remedial measures to prevent recurrence of \nsuch incidents in future. HLET has already submitted its report to the \nGovernment for consideration. Remedial measures are aimed at preventing \nrecurrence of breach in the embankment. \n\n 71 \nDevelopment of defunct airports \n*118. SHRI B.J. PANDA: \n MS. MABEL REBELLO: \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is proposed to engage private hands to develop defunct \nairports in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Centre has prepared a supportive regulatory framework \nto address the key concerns of private players in the matter; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether Government has already started work for the development of \nsuch a defunct airport Jharsuguda in Orissa under the above plan; and \n(f) if so, the details of progress made in the matter? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) Airports Authority of India (AAI) has not declared any of its \nairport as defunct Airport. Further, presently, there is no proposal for \nengaging private hands for development of non- operational Airports of \nAAI. \n(b) to (d) Do not arise. \n(e) and (f) Jharsuguda Airport is one of the non- operational airports. \nAAI has engaged M/s RITES, to carry out a feasibility study to consider \noperationalisation of this airport. Accordingly, a Master Plan has been \nprepared and land requirement of 815 acres has been projected to the \nState Government of Orissa under a Memorandum of Understanding (MOU). \nInitiation of work is, inter- alia, subject to response of the State \nGovernment. \nDecision of Gujarat Contract Labour Law \n*119. SHRI PRAVEEN RASHTRAPAL: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM \nAND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether his Ministry is aware about the State Government of \nGujarat Contract Labour Board's decision taken in the year 2001 in the \nmatter of General Motors who were authorized to employ contract labour \nwith a condition that workers will be paid wages i.e. basic, DA and other \nallowances as well as other facilities such as uniform, shoes, socks, \ncanteen, bonus, leave benefits, etc. similar to permanent workers; and \n(b) whether it is a fact that the contractual workers at Mehsana, \nAhmedabad, Ankleshwar and Vadodara Assets are not paid wages and other \n\n 72 \nfacilities as mentioned above in spite of clear-cut order of the Labour & \nEmployment Department, Government of Gujarat, dated 16th July 2002? \nTHE MINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI MURLI DEORA): (a) and \n(b) Oil and  Natural  Gas  Corporation  Limited  (ONGC)  does not engage \ncontract workers directly for its  \n\n 73 \nactivities. However, it awards certain categories of works/jobs to \nContractors through Job/Service contracts. The contractors of ONGC are \nrequired to pay not less than minimum wages, as notified by the Regional \nLabour Commissioner (RLC), Ahmedabad, for the State of Gujarat. In \naddition to minimum wages and additional wages, the labourers deployed by \ncontractors are provided coverage under Employees Provident Fund and \nMiscellaneous Payments Act, 1952, Bonus under the payments of Bonus Act, \n1965, Leave with wages, Kits and Liveries and Insurance for workmens' \ncompensation liability. \nVisit of domestic and foreign tourists in India \n†*120. SHRIMATI MAYA SINGH: Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the number of domestic and foreign tourists during January, 2007 \nto January, 2008 and January, 2008 to January, 2009, separately; \n(b) whether the number of tourists has declined during 2008-09; and \n(c) the measures taken by Government to encourage tourism? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) and (b) The number \nof Foreign Tourist Arrivals (FTAs) during January 2007 to January 2008 \nand January 2008 to January 2009 were 5.67 million and 5.85 million \nrespectively. The number of domestic tourist visits during January to \nDecember 2007 was 527 million. The data on domestic tourist visits for \n2008 and January 2009 are not available. \nThough the growth rate in FTAs in 2008 over 2007 was lower (5.6%) as \ncompared to that of 14.3% in 2007 over 2006, the total number of FTAs in \n2008 was 5.37 million which is higher than the FTAs of 5.08 million in \n2007. The lower growth rate in 2008, particularly in November and \nDecember, may be due to various reasons including global financial \nmeltdown and terrorist activities. \n(c) Development and promotion of tourism is primarily undertaken by \nthe State Governments/ Union Territory Administrations. However, to \nsupplement the efforts of the State Governments/ Union Territory \nAdministrations, and to encourage tourism, the Ministry of Tourism inter-\nalia takes the following steps: \n• Development of tourism infrastructure at tourist sites with the \ncooperation of other Ministries/ Departments and State/ UT \nGovernments; \n• Focusing on growth of hotel infrastructure, particularly that for \nbudget hotels; \n• Enhancing connectivity through augmentation of air capacity and \n\n 74 \nimproving road infrastructure to major tourist attractions; \n• Direct approach to the consumers through electr onic and print media \nthrough the “Incredible India” Campaign; \n• Production and distribution of publicity material; \n• Direct co-operative marketing with the airlines, tour operators and \nwholesalers in overseas markets; \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 75 \n• According greater focus to the emerging markets, particularly of \nChina, North East Asia and South East Asia; \n• Participation in Trade Fairs and Exhibitions; \n• Use of internet and web marketing; and \n• Re-inforced hospitality programmes including grant of air passages to \ninvite media personnel, tour operators on familiarization tours to \nIndia to get first hand knowledge on various tourism products. \n______ \nWRITTEN ANSWERS TO UNSTARRED QUESTIONS \nMerger of Jet and Kingfisher \n707. SHRI RAMA CHANDRA KHUNTIA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Jet Airways and Kingfisher are going for \na merger; \n(b) whether there would be any impact on Air India's market if such \nmerger takes place; and \n(c) if so, what would the action plan of Air India to address such a \nsituation? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) No such proposal has been received. \n(b) and (c) Do not arise. \nFlights from Dabolim airport \n708. SHRI SHANTARAM LAXMAN NAIK: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) the number of flights operating from Dabolim Airport in Goa to \nforeign countries, sector-wise and country-wise; \n(b) whether Air India or other airlines propose to operate any new \nflight from Goa to any of the foreign countries; and \n(c) if so, the route of these flights and by which date these flights \nare going to be operational? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) Presently, Sri Lankan Airlines, Condor Airlines and Transaero \nAirlines provide 3, 2 and 5 services a week from Colombo, Frankfurt and \nMoscow/St Petersburg to Goa respectively. Air India has 5 and 3 services \na week on the Bangalore - Goa - Dubai and Chennai - Goa - Kuwait sectors \nrespectively. Apart from these, any authorised carrier can provide \n\n 76 \nconnectivity to tourists by international charter flights to/from Goa. \n(b) and (c) Goa is available as a point of call to the designated \nairline(s) of 14 countries apart from the Indian carriers operating to \ninternational destinations. However, actual operation by airlines is \nguided by their commercial judgment. \n\n 77 \nUtilisation of redundant airports \n709. SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that earlier airports at Bangalore and \nHyderabad have become redundant or are underutilized; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the rational of making viable airports put to such \nunderutilization; \n(d) whether Government has worked out the cost and impact analysis of \nunderutilization of these airports; \n(e) how Government proposes to use these airports to its full \ncapacity; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (f) The existing Hyderabad Airport at Begumpet and the \ncivil enclave of Airports Authority of India (AAI) at HAL Airport in \nBangalore are operational and are being utilised for general aviation \npurposes. AAI have plans to utilise the existing airports at Hyderabad \nand Banglore for the following further purposes: \nBegumpet Airport:- (i) Integrated Aviation Academy (ii) Maintenance, \nRepair and Overhaul (MRO) facilities (iii) Aviation Expo and Convention \nCentre (iv) General Aviation Aircraft (v) High Speed Rail Link to Rajiv \nGandhi International Airport at Shamashabad with remote check in facility \n(vi) other services and miscellaneous purposes. \nHAL Airport:-  (i) General Aviation Aircraft Operation (ii) \nEstablishment of Convention Centre (iii) Other Services and Miscellaneous \npurposes. \nUpgradation of airport \n710. DR. JANARDHAN WAGHMARE: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government is keen in developing certain airports as \ninternational airports, and \n(b) if so, how many of them are being developed and what will be their \nestimated cost? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Airports Authority of India (AAI) has taken up the task of \n\n 78 \ndevelopment and modernisation of non- metro airports where provision for \nconstruction of New Integrated Terminal Building has been made to cater \nfor International and domestic operations. These airports alongwith \nestimated cost (in Rs. crores) are as under: \nConstruction of I nternational Terminal Building at existing \nInternational airports: Ahmedabad (291), Amritsar (145), Goa (330), \nJaipur (95), Nagpur (79.03), Srinagar (101.33) and Trivandrum (245.57). \nConstruction of International Terminal Building at Port Blair airport has \nalso been planned subject to acquisition of land. \n\n 79 \nConstruction of New Integrated Terminal Building at existing Non- metro \nairports: Aurangabad (99.67), Bhopal (135.04), Bhubaneswar (145.54), \nChandigarh (77.97), Gaya (13.26), Indore (135.60), Khajuraho (75.32), \nLucknow (129.38), Madurai (128.76), Mangalore (147.01), Pune (96.30), \nRaipur (135.72), Ranchi (137.79), Trichy (74.7), Udaipur (77.44), \nVaranasi (94.11) and Vishakhapatnam (94.94). Construction of new \nintegrated terminal building at Coimbatore airport has also been planned \nsubject to acquisition of land. Additionally, there is also plan for \nconstruction of new integrated terminal building at Vadodara airport \nsubject to finalization of estimates. Above projects are besides the \nmodernisation work presently underway at the major metro airports viz IGI \nairport, Delhi (8975), CIS airport, Mumbai (9802), NSCBI airport, Kolkata \n(1942.51), Chennai airport (1808). \nAviation projects in Uttar Pradesh \n711. SHRI MOHAMMED ADEEB: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) the details of ongoing works and projects of his Ministry in Uttar \nPradesh; \n(b) those which are likely to be completed on time and those running \nbehind schedule; \n(c) whether it is a fact that most of the works are not completed in \ntime; and \n(d) if so, the reasons for the same and the remedial measures taken in \nthe matter? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (d) Details of ongoing works at the airports in Uttar \nPradesh are as under:  \nLucknow Airport: \n1. 67% works of construction of new apron and taxiway has been completed \nwith expected date of completion by March, 2009. The work has \ninitially been delayed due to slow mobilisation of resources by the \nAgency for which letter has been issued to the Agency to increase the \nresources for early completion of the work. \n2. 36% works of the construction of Integrated terminal building has been \ncompleted. The work has been delayed due to change in layout of \nbuilding, late receipt of structural drawings and local disturbance by \nthe villagers. The Agency has been asked to expedite the progress of \n\n 80 \nwork. \n3. Construction of Fire Station and Emergency Medical Centre has just \nbeen started. The delay occurred due to land dispute with the \nvillagers. The matter has been taken up with the State Government to \nresolve the issue. \nFurstganj (Rae Bareli): \n85% works of construction of hostel building, class room, quarters at \nIndira Gandhi Rashtrya Uran Academy has been completed with expected date \nof completion by March, 2009. The delay has occurred due to late receipt \nof structural drawings and slow mobilization of resources by the Agency. \nIn this regard, letter has been issued to the Agency to increase the \nresources. \n\n 81 \nVaranasi airport: \n1. 56% works of construction of new terminal building has been completed \nwith expected date of completion by March, 2009. The delay occurred \ndue to change in location and layout of the terminal building, late \nreceipt of structural drawings and rains from June to September, 2008. \nLayout was revised due to site requirement. Letter has already been \nissued to the Agency to expedite the progress of work. \n2. 25% works of expansion of apron, construction of taxiway, extension of \nrunway and allied works with expected date of completion by June, \n2009. \nDelayed air operations at Delhi Airport \n712. SHRI NANDI YELLAIAH: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether his Ministry is seized of the data collected by Indira \nGandhi International (IGI) Airport's Operation Control Centre (OCC) which \nreveal that about 110 departures and 140 arrivals are delayed at IGI each \nday on an average due to operational and technical delays by airlines, \nsuch as untimely loading of baggage, delayed repairing and replacement of \naircraft parts, cabin crew reporting late, catering trucks and fuel \ntrucks arriving late and step ladders going missing; and \n(b) if so, the remedial measures taken to run flights as per schedule \nat IGI Airport? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) and (b) Average number of flights delayed due to operational \nand technical delay by airlines during last one month are about 15- 20 \ndeparture and 15- 20 arrivals per day. Other delays are due to various \nreasons like late arrival of flights, bad weather etc. However, for \nefficient and smooth process of airlines, Delhi International Airport \nLimited has done various improvement on infrastructure facilities at IGI \nairport like, at airside- commissioning of 3rd runway and associated \ntaxiway, commissioning of additional rapid exit taxiways and fillets for \nimproving the aircraft movement, increased CAT-III bays, introduced Power \nin Push Back for optimal use of airside bring operation and safety \nimprovement, introduced three tier parking in Satellite Apron to \naccommodate more parking bays, commissioning of Rubber removal machine \nfor upkeep of the runways. At international terminal, increased check in \ncounters, increased X- ray machines, increased DFMD gates, increased \n\n 82 \nimmigration counters, implemented in-line X-ray baggage screening system, \n4 single entry gates have been replaced by 4 double entry gates etc. \nSimilarly, improved infrastructure facilities are provided at domestic \ndeparture and arrivals terminals and also at city side domestic and city \nside international. \nUpgradation of Bhubneshwar Airport \n713. SHRI RAMDAS AGARWAL: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government has decided for development and upgradation of \nBiju Patnaik Airport at Bhubaneshwar to International standards; \n\n 83 \n(b) if so, how much time new terminal building, aerobridges and \nexpansion of apron is likely to take; and \n(c) what budgetary allocations have been made to meet development, \nexpansion and facilities of aerobridges etc. during the current year? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Construction of new Terminal Building at a cost of Rs. 145.54 \ncrores to handle 800 passengers, at a time, with all modern facilities \nincluding two aerobridges have been planned at this airport for \ncompletion in March, 2010. Expansion of apron to accommodate additional \naircraft parking stands has already been completed in June, 2008 at a \ncost of Rs. 14.97 crores. \n(c) Total capital budget provision for development of Bhubaneswar \nairport in the current Financial Year RE 2008-09 is Rs. 14.24 crores. \nConstruction of helipads and runways in Uttarakhand \n†714. SHRI BHAGAT SINGH KOSHYARI: Will the Minister of CIVIL \nAVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) the places in Uttarakhand where his Ministry is planning to \nconstruct helipads and runways which can be used by private airlines and \nchartered planes for the tourists and by the ambulance helicopters for \nextending relief during emergency alongwith the time by when the same \nwould be completed; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (c) No request has been received, in the recent past, \neither from the State Government of Uttarakhand or airline operators for \nconstructing new airstrip/Helipads or upgrading the existing airstrips in \nthe State. \nFacilities for the passengers with special needs \n715. SHRIMATI VIPLOVE THAKUR: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a 'contact zone' has been set up at \nIndira Gandhi International Airport (IGIA) Delhi for passengers with \nspecial needs; \n(b) if so, whether the facilities like wheelchairs for disabled and \nassistance for unaccompanied minors will be provided free of charge; \n(c) if so, whether Government proposes to set up such 'contact zone' \n\n 84 \nat various Airports in the country especially in Himachal Pradesh where \ntourists are often found complaining about these facilities; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 85 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. \n(c) and (d) At airports including small airports managed by Airports \nAuthority of India (AAI), facilities like wheel chair for physically \nchallenged passengers and assistance for unaccompanied minors have been \nprovided. However, there has not been any demand for setting up of \n'Contact Zone' at other airports in the country including Himachal \nPradesh. \nFog related problems at Delhi Airport \n716. SHRI DHARAM PAL SABHARWAL: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a large number of flights at Indira \nGandhi International Airport (IGIA) at Delhi were delayed or cancelled \ndue to fog during the last three months; \n(b) if so, what are the total number of flights that were cancelled or \ndelayed due to fog during the above period; \n(c) whether it is a fact that Runway Visual Range measuring machines \nand lights of taxiways Z- 6 and Z- 7 occasionally stop working and \nsecondary runway is not CAT-III complaint; and \n(d) if so, the details in this regard and the steps being taken to \nimprove the situation at Delhi airport? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) Indira Gandhi International Airport Delhi has CAT- III B \nInstrument Landing System (ILS) on Runways 29/11 and 28/10 which enables \naircraft landing and takeoff upto runway visibility of 50 mtrs. Usually \nthere are some delays/cancellations of flights during fog due to low \nvisibility if the airlines do not have CAT- III trained pilots and their \naircraft are not equipped with the same for operating during fog periods. \n(b) The total number of flights delayed/cancelled due to fog during \nthe period were 376 and 82 respectively. \n(c) and (d) Seven equipments installed by the India Meteorological \nDepartment (IMD) at the three runways are CAT-III B compliant. During the \nfog season the RVR systems at Runway 28, Mid of 28- 10, Runway 10, Runway \n27 and Runway 29 worked uninterruptedly 7 x 24 hrs. The RVR systems at \nRunway 11 failed on one occasion and the RVR system of Mid point of \nRunway 29-11 failed on four occasions mainly due to failure of power \nsupply. Installation of Uninterrupted Power Supply(UPS) at New Runway \n\n 86 \nlocations is being planned. \nIn-flight services of Air India \n717. SHRI RAMACHANDRA KHUNTIA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to  \nstate: \n\n 87 \n(a) whether his Ministry is aware of the fact that the air passengers \nare unhappy because of poor inflight service and food served in Air India \nincluding the take off and landing of private Airlines are much better; \nand \n(b) whether Government is also planning to improve the inflight \nservice, food including comfortable flying in Air India? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) and (b) The Ministry of Civil Aviation has not received \ncomplaints about landing and take-offs of Air India Flights. \nAlthough, on board services of the airline are at par with full \nservice international carriers, the few complaints received are forwarded \nfor appropriate action. The process of improvement in all areas of \naircraft airworthiness, cabin service, safety drills etc. is continuous \nand is regulated appropriately. \nJob loss in aviation sector \n718. SHRI MOHD. ALI KHAN: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether employees of domestic airlines in the country are facing \nsituation of loss of their jobs; \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor; and \n(c) the steps taken to protect the employees of airlines including \nthose of private airlines? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (c) Employees management is a human resource matter of \nairlines. Ministry does not regulate such matters. However, Government \ndoes not foresee any situation of major loss of jobs in airlines. \nShortage of pilots \n719. SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) the details of steps being taken by Government to meet the acute \nshortage of trained pilots in the country; and \n(b) whether it is a fact that due to crunch of pilots the aviation \nIndustry will not be able to grow as per requirement? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) In order to have sufficient quality pilots over longer period \nof time, the Indira Gandhi Rashtriya Uran Akademi (IGRIA) is being \n\n 88 \nmodernised through upgradation of civil and electrical infrastructure and \nacquisition of additional trainer aircraft. IGRUA has also entered into a \nmanagement contract with M/s. CAE, a reputed international organisation \nin this field, to professionalise its management. The Government has also \ndecided to set up a world- class institute at Gondia in Maharashtra as \nJoint Venture  Enterprise under the aegis of Airports Authority of India. \nThe other flying clubs in the country  \n\n 89 \nare also being supported by the Central Government by way of providing \nfunds for acquisition of trainer aircraft through Directorate General o f \nCivil Aviation and Aero Club of India. \n(b) At present there is no general shortage of pilots. However, there \nis a shortage of aircraft type endorsed pilots, particularly in the \nCommander category, which are met by the airlines by engaging foreign \npilots. Operators are required to train type rated Indian pilots for \nphasing out Foreign pilots. \nLoss due to fog \n†720. SHRI PRABHAT JHA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the flights were badly affected due to \nfog and mist this year; \n(b) if so, the details of cancelled and delayed flights; \n(c) the total loss of revenue to Government due to this; \n(d) the steps taken by Government to solve the problems of the \npassengers; and \n(e) the steps being taken by Government to tackle the problem of fog \nand to prevent the loss of revenue? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. During December, 2008 and January, 2009, 71 \nflights were cancelled, 752 flights were rescheduled and 145 flights were \ndiverted due to fog. \n(c) No such assessment is made. \n(d) and (e) Pilots of various domestic Scheduled Airlines are trained \nfor CAT-II/III operations during low visibility conditions as per the \nrequirements laid down in Civil Aviation Requirements (CAR) Section- 7, \nSeries 'X', Part- 1. Although there is no mandatory requirement for \nDomestic Airlines to get their pilots trained to operate during low \nvisibility conditions, these airlines are persuaded to train their pilots \nto operate in low visibility conditions. Only such pilots, who are CAT-\nII/III qualified, are approved as Instructor/Examiner so as to ensure, \nthat more trained pilots are available for CAT- II/III operations during \nlow visibility conditions to improve the operational efficiency. Further, \nflights schedules of Domestic Scheduled Operators to/from fog bound \nairports are approved on the basis of number of pilots trained by the \noperators for low visibility operations and the suitability of the planes \nfor such operations. \nPaucity of secondary airports \n\n 90 \n721. SHRI B.K. HARIPRASAD: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government has examined the recent study on Civil Aviation \nin India by an aerospace expert Hormoz P Mama, observing that India is \nnot suitable for low cost airline model due to paucity of secondary \nairports; \n(b) whether out of 127 airports under the operational control of \nAirports Authority of India (AAI) only 80 are operational; \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 91 \n(c) what are Government's plan to develop more secondary airports to \nsustain the growth of low-cost operators; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) Airports Authority of India (AAI) manages 124 airports and civil \nenclaves and at 11 other airports (including 5 international airports), \nAAI provides CNS/ATM services. At present 98 airports are operational in \nthe country. \n(c) and (d) AAI has undertaken an ambitious programme to \nupgrade/modernise airport infrastructure at 48 smaller airports in the \ncountry. Besides, AAI proposes to upgrade some of its non- operational \nsmall airports subject to availability of land from the concerned State \nGovernments free of cost and free from encumbrances, either through its \nown resources or in joint venture with the State Governments. \nService tax arrears of British Airways \n722. SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the British Airways has been permitted \nto accumulate Rs. 133 crore as service tax arrears for the period 2006-\n07; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) Does not arise. \nCAT-III-B trained pilots \n723. SHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) has \nissued instructions to airlines to train their pilots or equip their \naircrafts to make use CAT- III- B for smooth movement during dense foggy \nweather; \n(b) if so, how many pilots have been thus trained or aircrafts \nequipped during the 2006 and 2007; and \n(c) what is the break - up of such trained pilots, airlines - wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) There is no mandatory requirement for Domestic Airlines to \n\n 92 \nget their pilots trained to operate during low visibility conditions. \nThese airlines are persuaded to train their pilots to operate in low \nvisibility conditions. \n(b) and (c) No specific aircraft was equipped specially for CAT IIIB \noperation in 2006 and 2007 but aircraft are imported with the capability \nof CAT-II/III A or B operation by airlines. \n\n 93 \nIn 2006 and 2007, 650 pilots were trained by various airlines. These \ninclude 543 by National Aviation Company of India, 38 by Jet Airways, 27 \nby Kingfisher Airlines, 32 by Indigo. \nRemoval of fuel surcharge \n724. SHRI B.K. HARIPRASAD: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the aviation fuel prices are falling and are now at levels \nseen in September 2005; \n(b) if so, whether Government would prevail upon the domestic airlines \ncompanies to eliminate fuel surcharge on tickets to pass on the benefit \nto travellers; and \n(c) whether Government has convened a meeting of the CEOs of all \ndomestic airlines to discuss removal of fuel surcharge? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) Yes, Sir. In January 2009, average Aviation Turbine Fuel \n(ATF) price is almost at par with average ATF price of financial year \n2005-06. \n(b) Domestic Airlines have reduced the fuel surcharge in the range of \nRs.200/- to 400/-. Further, JetLite, Spicejet, Go Air and IndiGo have \nabolished the congestion charge. \n(c) No, Sir. \nCargo handling at Chennai Airport \n725. SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Chennai Airport is facing the risk of \nlosing its cargo business for lack of infrastructure at its air cargo \ncomplex; \n(b) whether cargo import at Chennai airport now touches around 12000 \ntones, where as it has the infrastructure to handle only 4000 tones which \nwas the earlier monthly average; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) and (c) The import cargo touched 10,000 metric tons per month \nduring April to November, 2008. Chennai airport has adequate \n\n 94 \ninfrastructure to handle 1,25,000 metric tons of import cargo per annum. \nIn the XIth Plan, Airports Authority of India has worked out capacity \nexpansion to handle 2,50,000 metric tons of import cargo against the \ndemand of 1,86,000 metric tons. \nFDI in domestic carriers \n726. SHRI N.R. GOVINDARAJAR: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government has decided to permit foreign airlines to \ninvest upto 25 per cent in domestic airlines; \n\n 95 \n(b) if so, the reasons for increasing Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) \nin aviation sector when the \"Vision 2020\" document is already under the \nconsideration of the Group of Ministers (GoM); \n(c) whether it is a fact that after 26/11 Mumbai terror attack, \nbringing FDI in domestic carriers would be a security threat or risk; \n(d) if so, whether Ministry of Home Affairs has cleared the proposal; \nand \n(e) if not, the reasons for bringing such a proposal without having \nbeen considered by GoM and Home Affairs? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (e) The proposal to allow foreign airlines to invest in \ndomestic airline is under examination. \nRelaxation in FDI norms \n727. PROF. ALKA BALRAM KSHATRIYA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government had relaxed the Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) \nnorms for civil aviation in the recent past; \n(b) if so, the benefits accrued to Government as a result thereof; \n(c) whether the private airlines have been benefited more than the \nnational airlines; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by Government to \nensure that national airlines are not at loss? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) to (d) Do not arise. \nRail and road connectivity to metro airports \n728. SHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI: \n DR. T. SUBBARAMI REDDY: \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether hue and cry raised over the connectivity of new Bangalore \nand Hyderabad airports has set to have positive spin- offs for 10 major \nairports across the country including those of the two silicon cities; \n(b) if so, whether the Centre has got into the act to ensure proper \nroad and rail based connectivity to the new and existing airports at the \nfour metros and also at Bangalore, Hyderabad, Ahmedabad, Kochi, \nCoimbatore and Pune; \n(c) if so, what extent the project has been completed; and \n\n 96 \n(d) to what extent it has been helpful to these States? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (d)  Issue  of  city  side  connectivity  of  airport  \npertains  to  State Governments/ local authorities.  \n\n 97 \nHowever, the Government is making all- out efforts to coordinate with \nthese agencies for providing improved city connectivity to the airports. \nFor this purpose, Government of India have constituted a Task Force under \nthe Chairmanship of Member, Planning Commission to finalise a plan for \nimproved city connectivity of selected airports in consultation with the \nrespective State Governments. The respective State Governments, from time \nto time, undertake/plan various measures such as widening of Highways, \nconstruction of High Speed Express Rail link from City Centre to the \nairports, etc., as per requirement. \nAssistance of foreign aviation authorities \n729. SHRIMATI SHOBHANA BHARTIA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government has decided to take the assistance of foreign \naviation authorities to ensure its airline and airports to meet all \nsafety norms; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the existing safety and security measures taken by \nGovernment at airports are neither sufficient nor as per international \nsafety norms; and \n(d) if so, how Government is going to implement international safety \nnorms at airports in the country? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. International Civil Aviation Organisation \n(ICAO) has offered to run National Projects in the areas of flight \nsafety, aerodromes and air navigation services in order to address the \nfindings of the audit conducted by ICAO in October 2006 under their \nUniversal Safety Oversight Audit Programme. These National Projects will \naugment the existing strength of DGCA by hiring experts \n(international/national) to assist DGCA in discharging its regulatory and \nsafety oversight functions, till regular technical staff are recruited \nand trained by the Government. A plan outlay of Rs. 2.00 crore has been \nestimated during the year 2009-10 for the National Projects of ICAO. \n(c) and (d) The existing security measures at the airports are \nsufficient and are as per standards of Annex- 17 of ICAO. However, ICAO \nhas offered to run a National Project for enhancement of Aerodrome Safety \nOversight Capability to assist in implementing remaining corrective \n\n 98 \nactions based on ICAO Audit recommendations and strengthen the DGCA by \nproviding experts to carry out aerodrome safety oversight functions. \nSeat upgradation in domestic flights \n†730. SHRI RAJNITI PRASAD: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the procedure for class upgradation in domestic flights; \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 99 \n(b) the type of passengers for whom the upgradation facility is \navailable and whether recommendation of Member of Parliament or any other \nofficer is required for this; \n(c) the officers entitled for class upgradation; \n(d) whether they take decision on their own discretion or there is any \ncriterion for this; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (e) Class upgradation in domestic flights is a commercial \nmatter of airlines. Government does not regulate commercial matters of \nthe airlines. \nFuel expenditure and operation cost of flights \n†731. DR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \n SHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there has been huge fall in consumer \nsale price of aviation turbine fuel used in aviation sector from August \n2008 to January 2009; \n(b) if so, the percentage of such reduction; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that the fuel expenditure contributes \nmore than 50 per cent in the operational cost of flights; \n(d) if not, the details thereof; and \n(e) the reasons for not passing the benefit being earned due to \nreduction in operational cost to general passenger yet? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) Yes, Sir. There has been a fall in the prices of Aviation \nTurbine Fuel (ATF) used in aviation sector from August 2008 to January \n2009. \n(b) There is a reduction of about 55% in the price of fuel from August \n2008 to January 2009. \n(c) and (d) The percentage of cost attributable to ATF would be \ndifferent for different airlines based on their business model. As per \nindustry estimates, ATF accounted for 45 to 50% of the operative cost of \nIndian Carriers when ATF price was at its peak. \n(e) Domestic Airlines have reduced the fuel surcharge in the range of \nRs.200/- to 400/-. Further,, JetLite, Spicejet, Go Air and IndiGo have \nabolished the congestion charge. \n\n 100 \nCatering facility at airports \n†732. SHRI PRABHAT JHA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 101 \n(a) whether Government is aware that there is no proper arrangement of \ncatering facility and cleanliness at several airports; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether it is a fact that the prices of eatables and beverages \nsold at airports are much higher than that of such items sold at Railway \nstations and at other places; \n(d) if so, the reasons therefor; and \n(e) the steps being taken by Government for selling the food and \ndrinking items at reasonable rates at the aerodromes so that people from \nevery class of society can afford them? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) and (b) Proper arrangement of catering facilities in hygienic \ncondition are made at all the operational airports by way of Restaurant, \nSnack Bars, Tea/ Coffee/ Cold Drink Vending machines. The airports are \nmaintained in neat and clean conditions through regular employees / \nmaintenance contracts. The periodical customer satisfaction surveys have \nbeen carried out to maintain proper standards. \n(c) to (e) Airports Authority of India (AAI) has been ensuring \nproviding reasonably priced food and drinking items at Snack Bars and \nKiosks to all categories of users at all airports. The price of the food \nitems at all outlets have been approved by AAI and subsequently \nmonitored. \nBlending in Jet fuel \n733. SHRI VIJAY JAWAHARLAL DARDA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government is aware that Air New Zealand has successfully \ncompleted a two-hour flight of passenger jet powered in part by vegetable \noil; \n(b) if so, whether such technology would be imported or indigenously \ndeveloped; \n(c) whether initially one engine of the aeroplane can powered by a 50-\n50 blend of oil from jatropha plants and standard jet fuel; and \n(d) whether the factor of environment sustainability would also be \ntaken in account while evaluating the viability of such a fuel? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) A report on the matter come to the notice through media. \nHowever, no authentic report has been received from International Civil \nAviation Organization (ICAO) or International Air Travellers Association \n\n 102 \n(IATA). \n(b) to (d) No such proposal is under consideration with the Ministry. \nAir services from Gwalior \n†734. SHRI PRABHAT JHA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government is aware that adequate air services are not \navailable in Madhya Pradesh which is an important State from tourism and \nhistorical point of view; \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 103 \n(b) if so, the details thereof alongwith the reasons therefor; \n(c) the steps being taken by Government to interlink several places of \nMadhya Pradesh such as Gwalior, Bhopal, Indore etc., with air services; \nand \n(d) the steps being taken by Government to link Gwalior with air \nservices which is a place of tourism and historical importance? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (d) At present, scheduled air services are available \nto/from Bhopal, Gwalior, Indore, Jabalpur and Khajuraho in the State of \nMadhya Pradesh. NACIL, Alliance Air, Jet Airways, JetLite and Kingfisher \nAirlines are operating to/from Madhya Pradesh. Government has laid down \nroute dispersal guidelines with a view to achieve better regulation of \nair transport services taking into account the need for air transport \nservices of different regions of the country. It is, however, up to the \nairlines to provide air services to specific places depending upon the \ntraffic demand and commercial viability. As such, the airlines are free \nto operate anywhere in the country subject to compliance of route \ndispersal guidelines issued by Government. \nConstruction work at airports \n735. SHRI RAMA CHANDRA KHUNTIA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) by when the construction works of New Delhi and Mumbai Airports is \nto be completed; \n(b) whether it is a fact that it has already been abnormally delayed; \nand \n(c) if so, the final date of completion? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (c) Construction of airport development projects at Mumbai \nand New Delhi airports are as per schedule. The Phase- I of the \nconstruction work is scheduled to be completed by March 2010 in the case \nof IGI Airport, New Delhi and by December 2012 in the case of CSI \nAirport, Mumbai. \nAirport at Guntur \n736. SHRI MOHD. ALI KHAN: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n\n 104 \n(a) whether Government is setting up an airport in the vicinity of \nGuntur district especially in Chilakaluri Peta in Andhra Pradesh; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, by when such airport will be set up? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) Does not arise. \n\n 105 \n(c) Setting up of new airports/upgradation of airports is a continuous \nprocess depending upon the traffic potential, demands from airlines \noperators, commercial viability etc. Airports Au thority of India (AAI), \nfrom time to time, takes initiative in this regard, keeping in view the \nabove factors, either with its own resources or with the participation of \nthe concerned State Governments through signing of Memorandum of \nUnderstanding (MoU) with them. \nAirport in BTAD \n737. SHRI BISWAJIT DAIMARY: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether all the formalities has been completed by the authority \nfor early completion of airport in Bodoland Territorial Autonomous \nDistrict (BTAD); \n(b) if so, tentative period for its completion; and \n(c) if not, the reasons for not to make any arrangement for the \nairport in the region? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) to (c) Pre- feasibility study conducted by AAI on a site \nidentified by Bodoland Territorial Council (BTC) did not find the site \nsuitable for construction of an airport. BTC would identify the alternate \nsite. \nWorking of DGCA \n738. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Directorate General of Civil \nAviation (DGCA) is under staffed, under paid and over worked which has \nled to many incidents of imbroglio in air journeys and risk to lives of \npassengers; \n(b) if so, the details of air journeys disrupted during last one year \nfor man made reasons; and \n(c)  the steps taken to improve the working of DGCA? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) and (b) Though Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) \nis facing manpower constraints, it is not true that this has led to any \nincidents or risk to lives of passengers. \n(c) DGCA continues to effectively enforce safety and regulatory \n\n 106 \nstandards on all airlines/operators without compromising passenger \nsafety. While Government takes action on continuous basis for filling up \nthe vacant posts, proposal for revival of lapsed vacancies are taken up \non need basis. \nStaff strength of DGCA \n739. SHRI B.K. HARIPRASAD: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the US Federal Aviation Administration has proposed to \nre-assess the precarious staff strength at the Directorate General of \nCivil Aviation (DGCA); \n\n 107 \n(b) if so, whether such assessment are a part of Federal Aviation \nAdministration's routine exercise to ensure that India complies to \nstrengthen its regulatory mechanism and ensure flight safety in \naccordance with the manifold air traffic growth in recent years; and \n(c) whether the proposed assessment at a time when DGCA's score on \ninfrastructure and manpower is low will push India from the present level \n1 to the lower third world levels? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI PRAFUL \nPATEL): (a) and (b) DGCA has received communication from USA's Federal \nAviation Administration (FAA) regarding India's Aviation Safety Oversight \nSystem under the International Aviation Safety Assessment (IASA) \nprogramme of FAA. International Civil Aviation Organisation (ICAO) had \nconducted the audit of India in October 2006 wherein they observed \nshortage of manpower, which affects its performance. DGCA has started \npreparing for the reassessment with the available resources and has also \ninitiated interim short term, medium term and long term steps to overcome \nthe shortage of manpower by hiring/appointing experts on secondment to \naugment the strength of DGCA. \n(c) Presently, India is in Category I. However, various short term and \nlong term measures have been initiated to strengthen DGCA to avoid any \ndowngrading in FAA reassessment. A plan outlay of Rs. 2.00 crore has been \nestimated during the year 2009-10 for the National Projects of ICAO. \nGovernments' position on bailing out Satyam Computers \n740. SHRI MOINUL HASSAN: Will the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIR be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Minister of Commerce and Industry has made a statement \nthat the Centre may provide financial support to Satyam to help it come \nout of its crisis; \n(b) if so, why should the taxpayers' money be used to bail out a \ncompany which is in crisis because its chairman committed deliberate \nfraud over years; and \n(c) whether Government is hesitant to confiscate the properties of \nSatyam Computer and maytas? \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): (a) and (b) \nThe Company Satyam Computer Services Ltd. is working for and mobilizing \nfinancial resources to meet its requirements under the guidance and \nsupervisions of the new Directors appointed by the Government on the \nBoard of the company, at its own strength, and there is no proposal to \n\n 108 \nprovide a financial bail out to the Company. \n(c) The Government is taking action as appropriate and necessary under \nthe law as warranted. \nSteps to prevent corporate fraud \n741. SHRI VIJAY JAWAHARLAL DARDA: Will the Minister of CORPORATE \nAFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n\n 109 \n(a) whether Satyam Episode is due to negligence on the part of \nregulatory authorities of Government system failure, or it is just a case \nof criminality; \n(b) if so, whether the contributory negligence of independent \nDirectors or willing connivance of auditors, be main focus of \ninvestigations; and \n(c) whether Government will immediately initiate positive steps to \navoid such reccurrences in Corporate sector, or will it wait for the \nfinal outcome of long-drawn investigations and other legal implications? \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): (a) The \nevents relating to Satyam Computer Services Ltd. relate to a particular \ncompany and do not represent a failure of the regulatory system and \nrelevant Regulatory authorities. The events have been caused by an \napprehension of fraud and possible falsification of the financial \nstatements of the company having implications under the Companies Act, \n1956, Indian Penal Code, SEBI Act and other allied statutes. \n(b) The Ministry has ordered investigation under the Companies Act, \n1956, to be carried out by Inspectors drawn from the Serious Fraud \nInvestigation Office, into the affairs of Satyam Computer Services Ltd. \nincluding, inter-alia, into the involvement and role, if any, of \nindependent directors, internal and statutory auditors of the company and \nexercise of due diligence by the statutory auditors to ensure true and \nfair financial reporting. \n(c) There is already an elaborate regulatory framework in place to \ndeal with such incidents. This framework, under the Companies Act, 1956 \n(the “Act”), provides for disclosures by companies for information of the \nstakeholders about the state of affairs of such companies, have been \nmandated. To facilitate making of such disclosures by companies, and for \nstakeholders and regulatory agencies to easily access and view them, \nGovernment has set up an electronic registry with round -  the clock - \naccess through internet. The Government has powers of inspection of the \nbooks of accounts of companies and also to investigate their affairs, if \nneed be, under the Act. In addition, the Act provides for appointment of \nindependent, statutory auditors to audit the accounts and report to the \nshareholders. Such audited accounts are also displayed on the electronic \nregistry for general viewing. While the reporting requirements are \nregulated under the Companies Act, 1956, the conduct of auditors is \nregulated under the Chartered Accountants Act, 1949. In addition, for \nlisted companies, compliance with these statutory requirements is \n\n 110 \nrequired to be certified by a company secretary in practice, who in turn \nis regulated under the Company Secretaries Act, 1980. Government has \namended the Chartered Accountants Act, 1949 and the Company Secretaries \nAct, 1980 in 2006 to provide for a more effective disciplinary mechanism \nto deal with cases of misconduct by Chartered Accountants, Company \nSecretaries respectively. In 2006, the Government has notified Accounting \nStandards to enable accounts of companies to be drawn up and disclosed on \nthe basis of fair, transparent and internationally accepted principles. \nThe Government has also introduced Companies Bill, 2008 in the Lok Sabha \nto provide for a comprehensive revision of the Companies Act, 1956 \nincluding, inter alia, provisions that would enable more effective checks \nagainst irregularities/frauds by companies. \n\n 111 \nIrregularities in companies \n†742. DR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \n SHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that 325 companies had been established by \none Ramalingam Raju of Andhra Pradesh; \n(b) if so, the number of the companies alongwith the names of the \nStates where he had established these companies; \n(c) whether there are allegations of irregularities in these \ncompanies; \n(d) if so, the details of such allegations and the amount of money \nlikely to be involved in these companies; and \n(e) for how many years these companies have been running? \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): (a) There \nis no record available with the Government, of one Ramalingam Raju of \nAndhra Pradesh having established 325 companies as promoter subscriber \nunder the Companies Act, 1956. \n(b) to (e) Do not arise. \nCases pending under Companies Act, 1956 \n743. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Will the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be \nplease to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that as on 31st December, 2007, 301 cases \nunder Companies Act, 1956 regarding appointment/re- appointment schedule \nof Managing Directors or whole time Director/Managers were pending; \n(b) if so, the details of cases disposed off out of the pending list \nand new applications received after 31st December, 2007 and disposed off; \nand \n(c) the steps taken to expedite disposal of the cases pending over a \nyear? \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): (a) and (b) \nYes, Sir. The details of cases disposed off till 12.02.2009 out of the \npending list as on 31.12.2007 is available at S tatement (See below). The \nnew applications received after 31st December, 2007 and the applications \ndisposed off out of those applications till 12.02.2009 is available at \nStatement-II (See below). \n (c) (i) After the implementation of MCA 21, on- line submission and \nprocessing of applications is being done to reduce delays. \n  (ii) In the on- line processing of cases, any deficiency in an \n\n 112 \napplication is being communicated on- line to reduce the time \nof processing. \n  (iii) The companies are advised to provide complete information \nin the application alongwith all supporting documents. \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 113 \nStatement-I \nThe details of coses disposed off till 12.2.09 \nPending applications Number of cases disposed Number of cases  \nregarding appointment/ off out of pending list till pending as on \nre-appointment schedule  12.02.2009 13.02.2009 \nof Managing Director or  \nWhole-time Directors/ \nManagers as on 31.12.2007 \n              301                  183              \n118* \n* It includes a number of cases which have been processed, and are pending due to \nnon-furnishing of required information/documents from the companies, about which \nthey have already been informed.  \nStatement-II \nThe details of new applications alongwith those disposed off from  \nJanuary, 2002 to 12.02.09 \nApplications regarding Number of cases disposed Number of cases  \nappointment/re-appointment  off out of col. I during the pending as on  \nschedule of Managing  period 01.01.2008 to 13.02.2009 \nDirector or Whole-time  12.02.2009 \nDirectors/Managers  \nreceived during the  \nperiod 01.01.2008 to  \n12.02.2009 \n         1112                688           424* \n* It includes a number of cases which have been processed, and are pending due to \nnon-furnishing of required information/documents from the companies, about which \nthey have already been informed. \nIrregularities in accounts of Satyam Computer Services \n744. PROF. ALKA BALRAM KSHATRIYA: \n DR. JANARDHAN WAGHMARE: \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Registrar of Companies has found serious \nirregularities in the accounts of Satyam Computer Services; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether Government has ordered Serious Fraud Investigation Office \n(SFIO) to look into the entire gamut of irregularities and other related \nanomalies in the books of accounts and other acts of commission and \nomission; and \n(d) if so, the steps taken by Government to check such frauds by \ncompanies? \n\n 114 \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): (a) and (b) \nThe Registrar  of  Companies,  after  ascertaining  information  in  \nexercise  of  his  powers  under  the  \n\n 115 \nCompanies Act, 1956 and after taking into account the statement made by \nthe then Chairman of Satyam Computer Services Limited (the “Company”) on \n7.1.2009, reported that the financial statements, statutory returns and \nother records of M/s Satyam Computer Services Limited, prima facie, \nappeared to contain serious misstatements as a result of which the \nfinancial statements and statutory returns a nd documents filed by the \nCompany may have been rendered as not reflecting a true and fair view of \nthe state of affairs of the Company, that certain decisions were taken by \nthe Board of Directors of the Company to utilize the stated cash reserves \nof the Company to acquire controlling interest in two other companies \nrelated to the promoter director of the Company and that on account of \nsuch misrepresentation and manipulation of financial information, there \nis a possibility of the affairs of the Company having been conducted in a \nfraudulent manner. \n(c) Yes, Sir. \n(d) Disclosures about the affairs of companies intended to inform the \nstakeholder the truth about the state of affairs of companies, have been \nmandated under the Companies Act, 1956. To facilitate making of such \ndisclosures by companies, and for stakeholders and regulatory agencies to \neasily access and view them, Government has set up an electronic registry \nwith round the clock access through internet. The Government has powers \nof inspection of the books of accounts of companies and also to \ninvestigate their affairs, if need be, under the Companies Act, 1956. In \naddition, the Act provides for appointment of independent, statutory \nauditors to audit the accounts and report to the shareholders. Such \naudited accounts are also displayed on the electronic registry for \ngeneral viewing. While the reporting requirements are regulated under the \nCompanies Act, 1956, the conduct of auditors is regulated under the \nChartered Accountants Act, 1949. In addition, for listed companies, \ncompliance with these statutory requirements is required to be certified \nby a company secretary in practice, who in turn is regulated under the \nCompany Secretaries Act, 1980. Government has amended the Chartered \nAccountants Act, 1949 and the Company Secretaries Act, 1980 in 2006 to \nprovide for a more effective disciplinary mechanism to deal with cases of \nmisconduct by Chartered Accountants, Company Secretaries respectively. In \n2006, the Government has notified Accounting Standards to enable accounts \nof companies to be drawn up and disclosed on the basis of fair, \ntransparent and internationally accepted principles. The Government has \nalso introduced Companies Bill, 2008 in the Lok Sabha to provide for a \n\n 116 \ncomprehensive revision of the Companies Act, 1956 including, inter alia, \nprovisions that would enable more effective checks against \nirregularities/frauds by companies. \nFinancial crisis at Satyam \n745. SHRI SHANTARAM LAXMAN NAIK: Will the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether a company known as Satyam based in Hyderabad has come into \nfinancial crisis; \n(b) if so, the nature of the crisis; \n\n 117 \n(c) whether Government has intervened to prevent the collapse of the \ncompany; \n(d) if so, the nature of the steps taken in this regard so far; \n(e) whether Government proposes to bail-out the company; \n(f) what are the offences prima- facie committed by the Directors and \nother persons connected with the company; and \n(g) the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): (a) and (b) \nOn 7.01.2009 the then Chairman of Satyam Computer Services Ltd., \nHyderabad (the “Company”), made a statement informing about mis-\nstatements in and falsification of the published accounts of the Company. \nThis statement led to loss of credibility of the Company before its \nstakeholders, including its shareholders, customers and employees, as \nalso with regard to future continuity of its business, impacting \nadversely on the value and operations of the company. \n(c) and (d) The previous Board of Directors of the Company has been \nsuspended and six new Directors appointed on the Board of the Company \nwith the due approval of Company Law Board given vide its order dated \n9.1.2009. The new Board has the requisite powers to carry on the work and \nbusiness of the company and is taking all possible steps to continue the \noperations and the business of the company and to safeguard the interests \nof its shareholders, employees and all other stakeholders. \n(e) There is no such proposal. \n(f) and (g) The Government has also ordered investigation into the \naffairs of the Company, under the Companies Act, 1956 by officers drawn \nfrom the SFIO to ascertain the nature of offences committed by directors \nand other persons connected with the Company. The matter is also being \ninvestigated by the CBI, Income Tax authorities, SEBI, ICAI etc. \nFindings of investigations into the Satyam Fraud \n746. SHRI R.C. SINGH: Will the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state: \n(a) the details of the findings of various investigations being made \ninto the Satyam fraud; \n(b) what action has been taken against the persons responsible for \nthis fraud; \n(c) whether the failure of various regulatory agencies such as \nRegistrar of Companies (ROC), Securities and Exchange Board of Indian \n(SEBI), statutory Auditors, etc. has been the main reasons for this \n\n 118 \nfraud; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and what remedial measures have been \ntaken to see that such frauds do not take place in the Corporate Sector? \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): (a) and (b) \nAn investigation into the affairs of Satyam Computer Services Limited is \nbeing carried out by officers drawn  from the Serious Fraud Investigation \nOffice (SFIO) following orders by the Government under  \n\n 119 \nthe Companies Act, 1956. In addition, investigations are also being \ncarried on with regard to the Company by Central Bureau of Investigation \n(CBI), Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI), Institute of \nChartered Accountants of India (ICAI), Income Tax Authorities, etc. On \nthe basis of orders of the Company Law Board on a Petition moved by the \nGovernment, the previous Board of Directors of the Company has been \nsuspended and six new directors appointed by the Government. \n(c) and (d) The events relating to Satyam Computer Services Ltd. \nrelate to a particular company and do not represent a failure of the \nregulatory system and relevant regulatory authorities. \nAn elaborate regulatory framework is already in place to deal with \nsuch incidents. This framework provides for statutory disclosures about \nthe affairs of companies intended to inform the stakeholder the truth \nabout the state of affairs of companies. To facilitate making of such \ndisclosures by companies, and for stakeholders and regulatory agencies to \neasily access and view them, Government has set up an electronic registry \nwith round the clock access through internet. The Government has powers \nof inspection of the books of accounts of companies and also to \ninvestigate their affairs, if need be, under the Companies Act, 1956. In \naddition, the Act provides for appointment of independent, statutory \nauditors to audit the accounts and report to the shareholders. Such \naudited accounts are also displayed on the electronic registry for \ngeneral viewing. While the reporting requirements are regulated under the \nCompanies Act, 1956, the conduct of auditors is regulated under the \nChartered Accountants Act, 1949. In addition, for listed companies, \ncompliance with these statutory requirements is required to be certified \nby a company secretary in practice, who in turn is regulated under the \nCompany Secretaries Act, 1980. Government has amended the Chartered \nAccountants Act, 1949 and the Company Secretaries Act, 1980 in 2006 to \nprovide for a more effective disciplinary mechanism to deal with cases of \nmisconduct by Chartered Accountants and Company Secretaries respectively. \nIn 2006, the Government has also notified Accounting Standards to enable \naccounts of companies to be drawn up and disclosed on the basis of fair, \ntransparent and internationally accepted principles. The Government has \nalso introduced Companies Bill, 2008 in the Lok Sabha to provide for a \ncomprehensive revision of the Companies Act, 1956 including, inter alia, \nprovisions that would  enable more effective checks against \n\n 120 \nirregularities/frauds by companies. \nStricter norms for auditing \n747. SHRI T.T.V. DHINAKARAN: Will the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is proposed to provide for stricter norms of auditing \nin large companies; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n\n 121 \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): (a) to (c) \nThe Companies Act 1956, (the “Act”) provides the framework for \nappointment of independent statutory auditors to audit the accounts of \nthe companies and for reporting to shareholders. Such audited accounts \nare also required by law to be displayed on the electronic registry of \ncompanies for general viewing. The Act also provides for \npunishment/penalty for non-compliance/default of the above requirements. \nThe Indian Auditing and Assurance Standards are formulated under the law \non the lines of the International Auditing and Assurance Standards and, \nas such, contain the best global practices for compliance by auditors. \nThe conduct of auditors in complying with the technical and legal \nframework governing audit of companies is also regulated under the \nChartered Accountants Act, 1949. Where any misc onduct on the part of \nstatutory auditors comes to notice, the Institute of Chartered \nAccountants of India, on a complaint or otherwise, ascertains the actual \nrole and discharge of functions by the statutory auditors with regard to \nthe accounting and auditing standards, the provisions of the Act etc. for \naction under the provisions of the Chartered Accountants Act, 1949, in \naddition to legal liability under the Companies Act, 1956. As there is \nalready a comprehensive regulatory framework in existence, there is no \nproposal to specifically provide for stricter norms for auditing in large \ncompanies. \nRestoration of monuments in Delhi \n748. SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI: Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that due to improper restoration method by \nArchaeological Survey of India (ASI) most prominent monuments in the \ncapital including 'Khirki Masjid' and 'Safdarjung Tomb' are slowly \nturning pink; \n(b) if so, whether Government is taking any steps to restore their \noriginal colour; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof and when the original colour of these \nmonuments is expected to be restored? \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) No, Sir. Every \ncare is taken for proper conservation of protected monuments by the \nArchaeological Survey of India. \n(b) and (c) Do not arise. \n\n 122 \nProtection of monuments in Rajasthan \n749. DR. GYAN PRAKASH PILANIA: Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased \nto state: \n(a) the norms for declaring any structure as protected monument; \n(b) the details of protected monuments located in Rajasthan; \n(c) the amount spent on the maintenance of the said monuments during \nthe last three years; \n\n 123 \n(d) the average amount annually spent on each monument and whether it \nis adequate; \n(e) the revenue earned by Government from the said monuments during \nthe above period; and \n(f) whether there are any World Heritage Sites in Rajasthan? \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) Under Section 4 of \nthe Ancient Monuments and Archaeological Sites and Remains Act, 1958, \nmonuments/sites which are of historical, archaeological or artistic \ninterest and which have been in existence for not less than 100 years can \nbe declared as monuments of national importance. \n(b) There are 160 centrally protected monuments/sites in Rajasthan \nunder the jurisdiction of Archaeological Survey of India. The details are \nat the Statement (See below). \n(c) The expenditure incurred on conservation, preservation and \nenvironmental development on these monuments during the last three years \nis as under: \n 2005-06   Rs.285.00 lakhs \n 2006-07   Rs.302.00 lakhs \n 2007-08   Rs.285.00 lakhs \n(d) Average amount spent on a monument during the last 3 years was: \n 2005-06   Rs. 1.78 lakhs \n 2006-07   Rs. 1.88 lakhs \n 2007-08   Rs. 1.78 lakhs \nThe above amount is considered to be adequate for conservation, \npreservation and environmental development of the monument. \n(e) The revenue earned from these monuments during the last 3 years \nwas: \n 2005-06   Rs.55.81 lakhs \n 2006-07   Rs.61.80 lakhs \n 2007-08   Rs.66.38 lakhs \n(f) Keoladeo National Park (1985) is a World Heritage Site in \nRajasthan. \nStatement \nThe list of centrally protected monuments/sites under the jurisdiction of \nArchaeological Survey of India in Rajasthan \n\n 124 \nSl.No. Name of monument/site\n Locality District \n1            2 3 4 \n1. Adhai Din-ka-Jhonpra Ajmer Ajmer \n2. Baori on the Ajmer-Jaipur Road Ajmer Ajmer \n\n 125 \n1            2 3 4 \n3. Badshahi Haveli Ajmer Ajmer \n4. Delhi Gate consisting of one archway Ajmer Ajmer \n5. Gateway of Taragarh Hill Ajmer Ajmer \n6. Marble Pavilions and Balustrade on the Ana  Sagar Ajmer Ajmer \n Bandh and the Ruins of the Marble Hamman behind  \n the Ana Sagar Bandh \n7. Saheli Bazar Buildings in Daulat Bagh Ajmer Ajmer \n8. Tomb of Allauddin Khan known as \"Sola Thumba\" Ajmer Ajmer \n9. Tomb of Abdulla Khan and his wife Ajmer Ajmer \n10. Tripolia Gate Ajmer Ajmer \n11. Magazine Building in Akbar Fort Ajmer Ajmer \n12. Kos Minar erected by Emperor Akbar  Ajmer-Jaipur Road Ajmer \n13. Kos Minar erected by Emperor Akbar Ajmer-Jaipur  Road Ajmer \n14. Kos Minar erected by Emperor Akbar Chatri Ajmer \n15. Sarai Chatri Ajmer \n16. Kos Minar erected by Emperor Akbar. Chugra Ajmer \n17. Kos Minar erected by Emperor Akbar Hoshiara Ajmer \n18. Kos Minar erected by Emperor Akbar Hoshiara Ajmer \n19. Kos Minar erected by Emperor Akbar Kair Ajmer \n20. Kos Minar erected by Emperor Akbar Khanpura Ajmer \n21. Mahal Badshahi Pushkar Ajmer \n22. Bhandasar Jain Temple Bikaner Bikaner \n23. Jain Temple of Susani Goddess Morkhana Bikaner \n24. Siva Temple and ruins Arthuna Banswara \n25. Mahakal and two other temples Bijolia Bhilwara \n26. Rock Inscriptions (12 century) Bijolia Bhilwara \n27. Rock Inscriptions within the Paraswanath Temple Bijolia Bhilwara \n compound (12 century) \n28. Ancient Temple known as Kaneriki Putali Khadipur Bhilwara \n  village \n29. Wall Paintings of Hardoti School in the palace Bundi Bundi \n30. Ancient Mound Nainwa Bundi \n31. Ancient Mound  Keshwarai Patan  Bundi \n\n 126 \n1            2 3 4 \n32. Ghateshwar Temple Badoli Chittaurgarh \n33. Kund Badoli Chittaurgarh \n34. Shrinagar Chawri Badoli Chittaurgarh \n35. Temple of Ashtamata Badoli Chittaurgarh \n36. Temple of Ganesh Badoli Chittaurgarh \n37. Temple of Sheshashayan Badoli Chittaurgarh \n38. Temple of Shiv and Kund Bad