6700 \n3. Hanumata C Medium 0.4827 0.0000 \n TOTAL  1.5177 1.7100 \nCLA: Central Loan Assistance.  \n\n 278 \nC: Completed. \n\n 279 \nAssessment of ground water level \n841. SHRI T.T.V. DHINAKARAN: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether any survey has been conducted to assess ground water level \nin the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES (SHRIMATI JAY \nPRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) to (c) The Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) \nconducts survey of ground water levels in the country through a network \nof 15640 observation wells throughout the country. Water levels are \nmonitored four times in a year during the months of January, April/May, \nAugust and November with a view to observe the trend of water level \nfluctuations. \nNational water policy \n842. SHRI N.R. GOVINDARAJAR: Will the Minister of WA TER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether there is any proposal to review National Water Policy as \nthe existing National Water Policy is inadequate to resolve the various \nwater issues in this country; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the time by which a comprehensive \nnew National Water Policy is likely to be announced? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES (SHRIMATI JAY \nPRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) and (b) The National Water Policy, 2002 (NWP) \nwas adopted by the National Water Resources Council on 1st April, 2002. \nThe NWP addresses the various issues related to development and \nmanagement of both surface and ground water resources. The NWP states \nthat it may be revised periodically as and when need arises. Observations \n/ views of a number of people were received in respect of NWP from time \nto time and they were duly examined by the Ministry of Water Resources. \nThe examination of these views did not call for immediate need for \nreviewing the NWP. The National Action Plan on Climate Change envisages \nrevisiting the National Water Policy in consultation with States to \nensure basin level management strategies to deal with variability in \nrainfall and river flow due to climate change. \nCriteria for declaring project as national project \n\n 280 \n843. SHRI PARIMAL NATHWANI: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government has declared 14 projects on different rivers as \nNational Project on 7 February, 2008; \n(b) the reasons for not including Narmada Project which is one of the \nbiggest three projects in the world; and \n\n 281 \n(c) what are the basic criteria for declaring project as National \nProject? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES (SHRIMATI JAY \nPRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) Yes Sir. \n(b) Sardar Sarovar Project is already eligible for 90% central grant \nfor the irrigation component of the project falling under drought prone \nand tribal area. The Power Houses of the project had already been \ncompleted. Till date, the Sardar Sarovar Project has been provided with \nCentral Assistance of Rs.5375.3585 crore under Accelerated Irrigation \nBenefits Programme (AIBP) which is highest Central assistance provided to \nany project in the country. \n(c) The criteria for selection of National Project is as under: \n  (a) International projects where usage of water in India is \nrequired by a treaty or where planning and early completion of \nthe project is necessary in the interest of the country. \n  (b) Inter-State projects which are dragging on due to non-\nresolution of Inter-State issues relating to sharing of costs, \nrehabilitation, aspects of power production etc., including \nriver interlinking projects. \n  (c) Intra-State projects with additional potential of more than \n2,00,000 hectare (ha.) and with no dispute regarding sharing \nof water and where hydrology is established. \nPercentage of irrigated cultivable land \n†844. SHRI JAI PARKASH AGGARWAL: Will the Minister of WATER \nRESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) the percentage of cultivable land irrigated in the country as on \ndate; \n(b) the increase in the percentage of irrigation during the last three \nyears, year-wise and State-wise; and \n(c) the details of increase irrigation in against in production during \nabove mentioned period, State-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES (SHRI JAY \nPRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) As per the Land Use Statistics of Ministry of \nAgriculture, the net irrigated area is 33.4% of the total cultivable land \nduring 2006-07. \n(b) As per information provided by the State Governments, during the \nlast three years i.e. 2005-06 to 2007-08, an irrigation potential of 5.32 \n\n 282 \nmillion hactares has been created. The state- wise details is at the \nStatement-I (See below). \n(c) State-wise estimates of foodgrains production by Ministry of \nAgriculture during the last three years i.e. 2005- 06 to 2007- 08, is as \nthe statement-II. \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi. \n\n 283 \nStatement-I \nStatewise status of irrigation potential created during last three years \nSl. No. Name of State                   Irrigation Potential Created (in \nthousand hectares) \n  2005-06 2006-07 2007-08 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 70.790 231.275 271.433 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 4.377 3.324 7.000 \n3 Assam 3.314 4.747 15.212 \n4 Bihar 279.451 199.600 31.750 \n5 Chhattisgarh 53.261 40.955 36.273 \n6 Goa 1.224 1.233 6.384 \n7 Gujarat 184.993 153.370 119.632 \n8 Haryana 21.890 12.564 10.356 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 7.557 4.423 5.845 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 15.559 25.355 19.443 \n11 Jharkhand 14.847 23.710 8.482 \n12 Karnataka 74.563 135.325 51.735 \n13 Kerala 12.382 5.996 7.064 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 81.350 103.550 126.200 \n15 Maharashtra 128.200 210.000 179.000 \n16 Manipur NR 0.000 12.000 \n17 Meghalaya 1.727 2.554 0.932 \n18 Mizoram 0.628 0.003 3.031 \n19 Nagaland 2.590 2.058 4.195 \n20 Orissa 24.590 43.750 63.427 \n21 Punjab 49.665 36.439 26.202 \n22 Rajasthan 164.580 99.590 93.590 \n23 Sikkim 0.800 1.214 1.080 \n24 Tamil Nadu 5.917 23.877 16.730 \n25 Tripura 4.788 3.985 2.706 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 432.236 533.707 544.503 \n27 Uttaranchal 32.177 35.310 29.506 \n28 West Bengal 3.460 5.432 7.683 \n TOTAL : 1676.916 1943.346 1701.394 \nNR - Not Reported \n\n 284 \nStatement-II \nState-wise Estimates of Foodgrains production from 2005-06 to 2007-08 \nState   Production ('000 Tonnes) \n  2005-06 2006-07 2007-08 \nAndhra Pradesh 16951.0 16229.0 19303.0 \nAssam  3677.8 3060.0 3470.0 \nBihar  8586.8 11098.6 10864.1 \nChhattisgarh 5714.8 5805.0 6291.9 \nGujarat 6154.0 6499.0 8206.0 \nHaryana 12998.1 14763.0 15307.8 \nHimachal Pradesh 1381.4 1382.2 1558.1 \nJammu & Kashmir 1482.0 1572.7 1572.1 \nJharkhand 2067.6 3686.8 4164.5 \nKarnataka 13489.0 9599.0 12186.0 \nKerala  638.3 640.5\n 539.7 \nMadhya Pradesh 13195.0 13747.0 12070.5 \nMaharashtra 12087.0 12645.1 15191.7 \nOrissa  7359.7 7344.7\n 8143.3 \nPunjab  25184.2 25313.1\n 26815.1 \nRajasthan 11445.0 14208.8 16058.7 \nTamil Nadu 6127.2 8263.0 6582.3 \nUttar Pradesh 40410.2 41214.5 42094.8 \nUttarakhand 1594.0 1735.0 1796.0 \nWest Bengal 15608.9 15974.5 16050.2 \nOthers  2449.6 2500.6\n 2509.2 \nALL INDIA 208601.6 217282.1 230775.0 \nUtilization of rain water \n845. DR. GYAN PRAKASH PILANIA: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether India receives about 4000 Billion Cubic Metre (BCM) \nrainfall every year, out of which only 690 Billion Cubic Metre (BCM) as \n\n 285 \nsurface water and 433 BCM as replenishable ground water resources are \navailable, and rest 2877 BCM are lost by evaporation or by flowing down \nto sea; \n(b) if so, what measures have been taken to check such gigantic loss; \n\n 286 \n(c) how much 'Storage Capacity' would be required to hold water \nflowing down to sea; \n(d) how much has been created so far; and \n(e) whether management of annual rainfall would be termed \n'satisfactory'? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES (SHRI JAY \nPRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) and (b) The average annual precipitation is \nestimated as 4000 billion cubic Meters (BCM) in the country. After \naccounting for the natural process of evaporation etc., the average \nannual water availability in the country is assessed as 1869 BCM. It is \nestimated that owing to topographic, hydrological and other constraints, \nthe utilizable water is 1123 BCM which comprises of 690 BCM of surface \nwater and 433 BCM of replenishable ground water resources. Rest of the \nwater could be considered to be flowing down to sea. National Water \nPolicy states that \"water resources available to the country should be \nbrought within the category of utilizable resources to the maximum \npossible extent\". Due emphasis has been laid on non- conventional methods \nfor utilization of water such as through inter-basin transfer, artificial \nrecharge to ground water and desalination of brackish or sea water as \nwell as traditional water conservation practices like rainwater \nharvesting including roof top rainwater harvesting with a view to further \nincrease the utilizable water resources. \n(c) and (d) Storage capacity of about 225 BCM has been created so far. \nAs per present assessment, the total estimated storage capacity of \nvarious projects under construction is about 64 BCM. Further, the State \nGovernments have identified various other schemes for investigation and \nplanning and the estimated storage for such schemes is about 108 BCM. The \nreservoir capacity for storing water is generally governed by \ntopographical and hydrological features. Similarly, the potential for \nartificial recharge to ground water is considerably influenced by the \naquifer  \ncharacteristics. The National Commission for Integrated Water Resources \nDevelopment in its report has projected the total storage capacity of \nabout 458 BCM by the year 2050. The potential capacity for artificial \nrecharge to ground water has been assessed as about 36 BCM. \n(e) Due emphasis has been laid on rainwater harvesting including \nrooftop rainwater harvesting, artificial recharge to ground water etc. by \n\n 287 \nthe respective State Governments and Government of India. \nEmbankment on Kosi river in Bihar \n846. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the building of embankments on the Kosi in Bihar in recent \nyears added to the problems; and \n(b) what is the amount of money spent on building those embankments \nsince the year  \n2000? \n\n 288 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES (SHRI JAY \nPRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) and (b) No, Sir. The embankments on river \nKosi were constructed before 1980. No further embankment was built on \nriver Kosi in recent years. As such, no money was spent on any embankment \non river Kosi since the year 2000. \nIrrigated land in the country \n847. SHRI RAMA CHANDRA KHUNTIA: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state the total irrigated land in the country including minor \nirrigation, lift irrigation, water harvesting and renewal water and total \nbudget allocation on different projects in various States from 2000 to \n2008, State-wise and year-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES (SHRI JAY \nPRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): As per the Land Use Statistics of the Ministry of \nAgriculture, the total Gross Irrigated Area and Net Irrigated Area in the \ncountry during 2006- 07 are 85,783 and 60,857 thousand hectares \nrespectively. As per the information provided by the Planning Commission, \nState-wise total expenditure/budget allocation for major & medium \nirrigation, minor irrigation; command area development (CAD) and flood \ncontrol in various States for the period from 2000- 01 to 2007- 08 are \ngiven in the Statement. \nStatement \nState-wise, Year-wise and Project-wise details of total expenditure for \nmajor, medi