eggade have \nparticipated. In order to impress upon the public, the Government and the \ndeplorable State of affairs, a noble method was adopted in which \nSubramanyaji played cricket on the National Highway because the road is \nnot motorable, and that has been widely published. It is reported that \nthe contract is again given to the same contractor who has done the sub-\nstandard work. Public is protesting and people are saying that a lot of \ncorruption and negligence has been involved in this. No action has been \ntaken. Therefore, it is absolutely necessary in the public interest that \nan enquiry should be held why it has happened. Shiradi Ghat was \nconsidered as one of the best routes in the NH- 48. So, action has to be \ntaken. I am bringing this urgent matter to the notice of the concerned \nDepartment of the Government and demand that immediate steps should be \ntaken not only to enquire into the matter but also to see that the road \nis restored. Thank you. \nApprehension expressed by naval chief regarding smuggling  \nof nuclear weapon through containers \nश्री प्रभात झा (मध्य प्रदे श): उपसभाप�त जी, म� सरकार का ध्यान �फर से एक बार 26 नवंबर, \n2008 को मुंबई म� जो घटना घट�, क� ओर �दलाना चाहता हूं। मुझे लगता है �क सरकार �कसी बड़ी \nघटना के बाद भी �चं�तत नह�ं होती और कोई कारगर कदम नह�ं उठाती है। अभी हाल ह� म� नौसेना \nप्रमुख सुरे श मेहता ने कहा, कहा \nह� नह�ं, बिल्क पूरे दे श के लोग� को चेताया, आगाह �कया �क माल \nढोने के �लए इस्तेमाल �कए जाने वाले क ं टेनर� का इस्तेमाल भारत �वरोध आतंक� संगठन द्वारा परमाणु \nह�थयार� क� तस्कर� के �लए समुद्र� मागर् से �कया जा सकता है। महोदय, यह दे श और दे श क� सुर�ा \nसे जुड़ा हुआ मामला है। यह कोई सामान्य व्यिक्त नह�ं कह रहा है, यह नौसेना प्रमुख कह रहे ह�। \nउस \nपर वे कहते ह� �क उन्ह� इस आशय क� सूचना �मल� है और उन्ह�ने सरकार से सुर�ा मांगी है। उन्ह�ने \nसाथ म� कहा है �क उन्ह� यह भी आशंका है �क य�द इस �दशा म� कोई पहल नह�ं हुई तो दे श �हत म� \nदे श के सामने इस आशय क� अनेक आशंकाएं ह�गी। सुर�ा मंत्रालय को इस �दशा म� कारगर कदम \nउठाना चा�हए, क्य��क यह दे श क� सुर�ा से जुड़ा मामला है। इस संबंध म� राष्ट्र�य सुर�ा सलाहकार \nक्या कर रहे ह�, मुझे इसक� जानकार� नह�ं है। अभी मुंबई क� घटना आ प के सामने है, महोदय, हम \nजानना चाहते ह� �क हमार� जो 7,517 �क.मी. लंबी तट�य सीमा है, िजससे गुजरात, महाराष्ट्र, \nपिश्चम बंगाल के तट�य इलाक� म� ह�थयार� और मादक पदाथ� क� तस्कर� बहुत जोर� पर जार� है, ये \n\n 352 \nसभी बात� आने के बाद भी यह दे श इतने गंभीर मसले पर �चं�तत क्य� नह�ं होता है, कोई कारगर \nकदम क्य� नह�ं उठाता है, यह आज तक समझ म� नह�ं आया है। सरकार इ स के �लए क्या कर रह� है? \nक्या इस दे श पर दुबारा मुंबई क� घटना होगी? मुझे लगता है �क वह ताज पर हमला नह�ं हुआ था, \nवह भारत माता के ताज पर हमला हुआ था। हम सभी इन बात� क� और �चं�तत नह�ं होते ह�। नौसेना \nप्रमुख ने �निश्चत तौर पर कोई न कोई नोट भेजा होगा, उ स के बाद उन्ह�ने जा�हर सभा म� या �कसी \nसे�मनार म� कहा है। नौसेना प्रमुख का बार-बार यह कहना दे श को ह� �चं�तत नह�ं करता, बिल्क दे श के \n115 करोड़ लोग� को भी �चं�तत  \n\n 353 \nकरता है। मुझे लगता है �क इस �च ंत ा से यह सदन भी �चं�तत होगा, सरकार भी �चं�तत होगी, आपको \nकोई न कोई कारगर कदम उठाना चा�हए। आपको एक्स रे मशीन लगानी चा�हए, उन क ं टेनर� म� क्या \nहै, क्या नह�ं है, इसक� जांच होनी चा�हए, ये सार� व्यवस्थाएं करनी चा�हए। य�द ऐसा नह�ं होता है तो \nइससे इंकार नह�ं �कया जा सकता है �क कोई बड़ी घटना दे श म� न घटे । \nश्री श्रीगोपाल व्यास (छत्तीसगढ़): उपसभाप�त जी, म� स्वयं को इससे संबद्ध करता हूं। \nNeed to protect the dialects of the country \nDR. (SHRIMATI) KAPILA VATSYAYAN (Nominated): Thank you, Mr. Deputy \nChairman, Sir. I am instituting a bronze medal for anyone who can \npronounce my second name properly. \nSir, through you, I wish to draw the attention of this House, not only \nthe attention of this House but the people of this country, to the fact \nthat this country does not have only the 22 languages that have been \nrecognised in Schedule VIII, but it has 500 other languages and dialects. \nThe UNESCO report has brought to our attention that India. \n...(Interruptions).... India heads the list of countries where languages \nand dialects are going to become extinct. We have 194 languages which are \non the verge of extinction. We pride ourselves in the cultural heritage \nof this country. Who are the repositories of this heritage? It is the \ntribal India. And, these languages belong to many linguistic streams the \nTibetan-Burman, the Austro-Asiatic, the Nicobari and the Andamanese, and \na most important linguistic stream, namely, the Mundari, known to the \nSanthals, the Hoe and other tribes. All these are going to become \nextinct. Are we at all concerned with the fact that we shall be losing \nwhatever we termed as 'intangible heritage' because that 'intangible \nheritage' is through oral communication of over 3000 years in this \ncountry? These languages and dialects are not recognised in the school \nsystem. The Ministry of HRD has taken a step, but a very small but an \nimportant step and a welcome step, to recognise some of these languages \nin the three-language formula of the Ministry of HRD as mother tongue at \nthe elementary school level. But, this is not enough. \nA concerted effort is required because it concerns all that we are \ndoing in the field of environment and biodiversity. It is relevant to our \nfostering of cultural diversity. It is relevant to all that we call 'folk \nmedicine' because who are the holders of the knowledge of this 'folk \nmedicine'? Therefore, I would suggest that a Coordinating Committee \nshould be formed at the Central level comprising of the Ministry of \nTourism and Culture, the Ministry of HRD, the Ministry of Tribal Affairs, \nand the Ministry of Environment. And, this has to be done at a very quick \n\n 354 \npace because otherwise these language will become extinct before we \nrealise. Also, the Ministry of Communication should be brought into it. \nThank you, Sir. \nExpenditure from Government treasury on advertisements \nSHRI BHARATKUMAR RAUT (Maharashtra): Mr. Deputy Chairman, Sir, I \nbelong to media, as I am a practising journalist and I am raising an \nissue which relates to newspapers and television. I am talking about the \nwasteful expenditure that is being done by the Government – not only this \nGovernment,  but also various State Governments, State Government \ncorporations and Government  \n\n 355 \nagencies – on the various advertisements, which have been appearing since \nthe last fortnight every day not only in newspapers, but also on \ntelevision channels. Sir, in the name of Bharat Nirman, the \nadvertisements are appearing boasting about the work that has been done \nby various departments. I think, it is the timing of these advertisements \nwhich is very important and that is a matter of concern for me.  \nSir, when the elections are round the corner and when the Code of \nConduct is likely to be implemented in a few days' time, suddenly, there \nis a crop of advertisements. Today, while I was going through the \nnewspapers, I noticed that two English language newspapers published from \nDelhi – I would not mention the names –  carried twelve full -page \nadvertisements of the Government; twelve full- page, Sir. Going by the \nrate-card, see, how much has been spent. This is in respect of only one \ncity and only two newspapers. Imagine, how many newspapers are there in \nthis country and how much money is being spent on these advertisements, \njust to advertise about the Government, the ruling party. I think, it is \na * the funds of the public. Sir, we pay for this; the taxpayer pays for \nthis. Sir, I would have agreed to it, had the Government been just and \nimpartial in giving the advertisements. Sir, newspapers and channels \nwhich blow the trumpet of the Government are getting huge advertisements, \nwhile those which are just and logical and which scrutinise the job of \nthe Government, they are being sidelined. I think, this is not the way \nthe Government should function. \nSo, Mr. Deputy Chairman, Sir, I request you to use your good office \nand tell the Government that this is not right. These things do not work, \nand, at the end of the day, people know who has done what, and, \ntherefore, this will not work. After all, we are a poor country. We \nshould not spend like this. This is my request to the House. Thank you. \nSHRI ARUN JAITLEY (Gujarat): Sir, the hon. Member has used the word * \n...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: We will examine it. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI ARUN JAITLEY: We should actually say, it is a stimulus package by \nthe Government for the friendly media. \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS AND THE \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING (SHRI V. NARAYANASAMY): I \nwill make a reference on 'India Shining'. That will be better. \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: Mr. Thirunavukkarasar, today, the hon. Chairman \nhas already made a reference from the Chair regarding the Oscar awards. \nSHRI SU. THIRUNAVUKKARASAR (Madhya Pradesh): Sir, I will mention only \n\n 356 \none sentence. ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: Again, mentioning it in the Zero Hour is not in. \n...(Interruptions)... I will give you time but the Chairman. \n...(Interruptions)... \n*Expunged as ordered by the Chair. \n\n 357 \nSHRI SU. THIRUNAVUKKARASAR: Sir, I would like to join the hon. \nChairman in congratulating. ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: The entire House has already joined. \n...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI SU. THIRUNAVUKKARASAR: Especially, because he is from Tamil Nadu. \n...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: He is from India. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI SU. THIRUNAVUKKARASAR: Sir, first, he is from Tamil Nadu, and, \nthen, India. He has got global acceptance. ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: The Chair has already made a reference and \ncongratulated him. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI SU. THIRUNAVUKKARASAR: He is the first Indian to receive two \nOscar awards. So, I congratulate A.R. Rahman. ...(Interruptions)... \nPROF. P.J. KURIEN (Kerala): Sir, this is not. ...(Interruptions)... \nSomeone from Kerala or Tamil Nadu. ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: Okay. Let us not talk about the region. He \nbelongs to India. ...(Interruptions)... \nप्रो. पी.जे. कु �रयन: वह भारत का है। \nश्री उपसभाप�त: जी हां, भारत का है। \nSHRI SU. THIRUNAVUKKARASAR: No problem. No problem. \n...(Interruptions)... Sir, he has added glory to Tamil Nadu, to India and \nto the Indian film industry. Sir, I congratulate A.R. Rahman and also \nPookutty, Gulzar and the Director of Best Documentary Film, Smile Pinky. \nSir, a small girl from U.P. has acted in the film, 'Smile Pinky'. She has \ngot some popularity but she hails from a family with poor economic \ncondition. I request the Central Government to give something as award to \nthat girl to appreciate her. \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: Please. ...(Interruptions)... The Chairman has \nalready made a reference. \nSHRI SU. THIRUNAVUKKARASAR: So, I congratulate all of them, especially \nA.R. Rahman. Thank you. \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: Now, Special Mentions. Only to be laid on the \nTable. \n______ \nSPECIAL MENTIONS \nDemand to stablize rate of interest levied by NABARD on agricultural \nloans \n\n 358 \nश्री ल�लत �कशोर चतुव�द� (राजस्थान): महोदय, के न् द्र सरकार द्वारा कृ षक� को 7 प्र�तशत दर पर \nफसल� ऋण उपलब्ध करवाने क� नी�तगत घोषणा के अन्तगर्त नाबाडर् (NABARD) ने वषर् 2006-07 हेतु \n2.50 प्र�तशत क� �रयायती दर से 40 प्र�तशत पुन�वर्त्त हेतु सहम�त द� थी। उनक े साथ-साथ यह भी \nतय �कया गया था �क इस दर म�  \n\n 359 \n0.50 प्र�तशत प्र�तवषर् बढ़ोतर� होती रहेगी। इस प्रकार यह दर वषर् 2007-08 म� 3 प्र�तशत 2008-09 \nम� 3.50 प्र�तशत होते हुए आगे बढ़ती जाएगी। इस 0.50 प्र�तशत क� वा�षर्क वृ�द्ध से अल्पकाल�न \nसहकार� ऋण ढांचे को प्राप्त होने वाला मािजर्न उसी प�रणाम म� घटता जाएगा और कालान्तर म� समस्त \nअल्पकाल�न सहकार� ऋण ढांचा शनै:-शनै: आ�थर्क रूप से अ�म हो जाएगा। \nवतर्मान नी�त के तहत बढ़ती हुई ब्याज-दर से अल्पकाल�न सहकार� ऋण ढांचे के वे लाभ भी \nप्रभा�वत होते ह�, जो वैद्यनाथन स�म�त क� �सफा�रश� के �क्रयान्वयन से अल्पकाल�न सहकार� ऋण ढांचे \nको �मलने वाले ह�। \nअत: मेर� मांग है �क नाबाडर् द्वारा ल� जाने वाल� ब्याज-दर को, जो आधार वषर् 2006-07 हेतु \n�नधार्�रत क� गई है, िस्थर कर �दया जाए एवं नाबाडर् द्वारा 40 प्र�तशत पुन�वर्त्तीयन इसी िस्थर दर \nपर �कया जाता रहे।  \nConcern over malnutrition prevailing amongst children in the country \nDR. GYAN PRAKASH PILANIA (Rajasthan): Sir, the Third National Family \nHealth Survey shows that 45.9 per cent of children are under-weight, \nwhich indicates that Child malnourishment in India is much higher than \nsub-Saharan Africa, where malnutrition averages 35 per cent. In China, \nonly 8 per cent are underweight. Malnourishment causes 50 per cent of all \nchild deaths. Child malnourishment has fallen only less than one per cent \nin the past seven years - from 46.7 per cent in 1998-99 to 45.9 per cent \nin 2005-06. 38.4 per cent of children up to the age of three are stunted \nand 19.1 per cent wasted, too thin for their age. \nThe figures for anaemia are even more alarming. Aged between six and \n35 months, four of five children 79.1 per cent in India, are anaemic, \nwhich is almost 5 per cent more than the last count of 74.2 per cent. \nAnaemia in women has also gone up from 51.8 per cent in 1998- 99 to 56.2 \nper cent in 2005-06. \nWhat further alarms experts is the poor performance in ORS and breast-\nfeeding practices that directly affected the newborn. Only 26.2 per cent \nkids with diarrhoea received ORS and only 23.4 per cent of mothers \ninitiated breastfeeding in the first hour. Exclusive breastfeeding was a \ndismal 46.3 per cent, when exclusive breastfeeding alone can reduce \nmortality by 16 per cent. Even the improved infant mortality rate - down \nto 57 per 1,000 births from 68 in 1998- 99, remains dramatically higher \nthan that of western nations. \nFrom above, it is obvious that India has clearly failed its children. \nHence, I would urge the hon. Minister for Women and Child Development to \n\n 360 \ntake urgent steps to save Indian Childhood from the curse of \nmalnutrition. \nNeed for immediate approval of financial restructuring proposal of \nHindustan Shipyard Limited, Visakhapatnam, Andhra Pradesh \nSHRI M.V. MYSURA REDDY (Andhra Pradesh): Sir, the premier shipbuilding \ncompany of the country, Hindustan Shipyard Limited, which is in the \nbusiness of shipbuilding and repairs for Defence,  Coast  Guard,  Ports  \nand the oil industry is on the verge of collapse due to failure of the  \n\n 361 \nGovernment to approve financial restructuring package, which is gathering \ndust in the Ministry for nearly two decades. \nOnly last July, it delivered its 700th passenger vessel Kavaratti to \nLakshwadweep administration. Sir, twenty committees were appointed, which \nhave given important recommendations for its financial restructuring, but \nnot even a single recommendation has been implemented by the Government. \nThe Group of Ministers also recommended transfer of this ISO 9001- 2000 \norganisation to the Ministry of Defence. But the Cabinet has not approved \nthis recommendation so far, which plays a vital role in the revival and \nsurvival of Hindustan Shipyard. The Group of Ministers also imposed \nrestrictions in securing fresh orders. This is also hampering the company \nin a big way for the last two years. If things go on like this, there is \nevery possibility that the Shipyard would have to be closed, and as a \nresult, nearly 10,000 workers and their families would come on the roads. \nIn view of the above, I request the Government of India to i) \nimmediately approve the recommendation of the Group of Ministers and \ntransfer HSL from Ministry of Shipping to Ministry of Defence, ii) to \npermit Hindustan Shipyard to firm up its fresh orders, and, iii) to \nimmediately approve the financial restructuring package. \nConcern over pathetic condition of Bhopal gas tragedy survivors \nSHRI D. RAJA (Tamil Nadu): It has been over a year since the Bhopal \ngas survivors have marched on foot from Bhopal to Delhi for the second \ntime, sat on a 22 days' hunger strike and slept on the footpaths of Delhi \nfor over five months. They came to Delhi with their longstanding demand \nfor setting up of an Empowered Commission on Bhopal, health care and \nclean drinking water for people which got contaminated with Union \nCarbide's poison, to make Dow Chemical liable for the clean up of the \ntoxic site in Bhopal, for action against Dow Chemical, withdrawing the \npermission given to Reliance for purchasing of a confiscated property of \nUnion Carbide and to expedite the extradition process to produce the then \nCEO of Union Carbide in the Bhopal Chief Judicial Magistrate's court. \nOn 29th May 2008, Shri Prithviraj Chavan met the survivors at Jantar \nMantar on behalf of the Prime Minister and announ ced the Government's in \nprinciple agreement of setting up of the Empowered Commission on Bhopal, \nprovide clean water supply and resume medical research by the ICMR. \nSubsequently, on 8th August 2008, Shri Ram Vilas Paswan met the \nsurvivors at Jantar Mantar and stated that Department of Chemicals and \nPetrochemicals will prepare the Terms of Reference for the Empowered \nCommission and also take legal action on the civil and criminal \n\n 362 \nliabilities of Union Carbide and Dow Chemical. \nIt has been over 8 months since these promises have been made to the \nsurvivors, but nothing has been delivered yet. Meanwhile, even today, \nevery month 15-20 Bhopalis are dying due to Union Carbide's  poison  and  \nchildren  are   born   deformed   in  Bhopal. It is a matter of great \ndistress and  \n\n 363 \nshame that in this 25th year of the disaster, the Government has not been \nable to provide basic care, justice and a life with dignity to the \nsurvivors of the biggest industrial disaster of the world. The Government \nhas even failed to deliver on its own promises. Will the Government \nrespond positively? \nSHRI D. RAJA: Sir, I would like ...(Interruptions)...  \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: You just lay it, Mr. Raja. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI D. RAJA: Sir, the House should know what I am raising. \n...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: Mr. Raja, you please mention only the subject. \n...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI D. RAJA: I will do that. But please allow me to read it. It has \nbeen over a year since the ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: No, no, we have not allowed anybody. Please lay \nit. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI D. RAJA: At least the House should know the Government is so \ncallous ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: Mr. Raja, you are going to get the reply of the \nSpecial Mention from the Government. If you do not get a reply, you can \nbring ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI D. RAJA: That I would have written separately also. Why should we \nraise it in the House? The Government should take note of what we are \nraising. That is the point I am making. \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: No, no. All other Members would complain then. \n...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI D. RAJA: I will obey what you say but my point is, this House \nshould know that there is a problem with regard to Bhopal Gas Tragedy and \nthe Government is so callous. The Government has not done anything for \nit. That is the issue I am trying to raise. ...(Interruptions)... \n\n 364 \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: The House is adjourned to meet at 2.00 p.m. \nThe House then adjourned for lunch at one minute past one of the \nclock. \n______ \n\n 365 \n2.00 P.M. \n The House re-assembled after lunch at three minutes past two of the \nclock, \nMR. CHAIRMAN in the Chair \nGOVERNMENT BILLS \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: Now we shall take up the High Court and Supreme \nCourt Judges (Salaries and Conditions of Service) Amendment Bill, 2009. \nThe High Court and Supreme Court Judges (Salaries and Conditions  \nof Service) Amendment Bill, 2009 \nTHE MINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE (SHRI H. R. BHARDWAJ): Sir, I beg to \nmove: \nThat the Bill further to amend the High Court Judges (Salaries and \nConditions of  \n Service) Act, 1954 and the Supreme Court Judges (Salaries and \nConditions of  \n Service) Act, 1958, as passed by Lok Sabha, be taken into \nconsideration. \nSir, I wish to submit that the Sixth Pay Commission made new \nprovisions for the salaries and allowances of the Civil Services. \nConsequently, salaries and perks of the judiciary were also to be \nreviewed. This Bill reals with the salaries of the judges of the High \nCourts and the Supreme Court, their sumptuary allowances, and pension \nmatters. Sir, this is a  token increase because of the recommendation of \nthe Sixth Pay Commission. Salary of the High Court judges is on a par \nwith the Secretaries to the Government. Similarly, the judge of the \nSupreme Court is paid a salary equivalent to the Cabinet Secretary. And \nthe Chief Justice of India's salary is Rs. one lakh. This is a nominal \nincrease consistent with the dignity of office. This was debated and \npassed unanimously in the Lok Sabha. Members expressed their views with \nfreedom. I wish the same gesture is ensured in this august House, because \njudges don't speak for their own salaries. Parliament always decides it. \nAnd, this should be kept in mind when discussing it. Other matters \nrelating to judiciary may come in during the debate. I would like to be \nenlightened on the problems and Members are free to express their views. \nBut, this particular matter of the Judges' salaries and allowances is \nprotected by the Constitution and it cannot be derogated to their \ndisadvantage. Sir, these days, judiciary is doing a very important task \nof protecting civil rights, human rights and disputes between the \n\n 366 \ncitizens and the State. Having regard to the importance of the \ninstitution, I submit, the House should be generous in dealing with this. \nI, therefore, Sir, request that this Bill may be considered. \nThe question was proposed. \nSHRI ARUN JAITLEY (Gujarat): Mr. Deputy Chairman, Sir, the hon. Law \nMinister has moved the High Court and Supreme Court Judges (Salaries and \nConditions of Service) Amendment Bill, 2009. Sir, I rise to support this \nBill. And, I support this Bill primarily on the ground that in these days \nof inflation, the cost of living having gone up, salary structures, first \nin the private sector and now in the Government sector, have \nsubstantially changed. One of the objects in altering the salary \nstructures really  is,  as  the  economy  also grows in order to give to \nall those who are the recipients of those  \n\n 367 \nsalaries and service conditions, the benefit of an improved quality of \nlife which they can get on those salary structures. In the case of \nJudges, when we are improving their salary structures through this \nlegislation, it's also an occasion for us to ponder over the situation as \nfar as the institution is concerned. I am, Sir, conscious of the fact \nthat the privileges we enjoy as a Member to speak on the subject also \nrequires an in-built restraint with regard to certain inter-institutional \ncourtesies that various organs of the State have to maintain when they \ndiscuss the performance and functioning of each other. \nSir, in this country, we function on one of the basic tenets which is \nthe separation of powers. The concept of separation of powers essentially \nhas conferred upon the judiciary in India the power to adjudicate \ndisputes and also a very important power of judicial review is concerned. \nThere has been a serious concern in the society that when all our \ninstitutions function, none of them tries to encroach upon the \nfunctioning of the other and, therefore, there occasionally has been a \ncomment and suggestion when we find that the limits of activism at times \ncross the lakshman rekha. But, then, there is no division which is \npossible. The only division which is possible is by the institutions \nthemselves acting in restraint and by acting in restraint, some element \nof statesmanship is also required that is fully conscious of what the \nfunctions of the Executive and Legislature are, the courts also take \nadequate care to make sure that they don't overstep. \nSir, the argument is normally given to the extent that what does a \njudicial institution do at a time when other organs of the State don't \nperform their functions. And this argument has been used to support this \ncontention that an overreach in such cases has to take place, otherwise, \nthere would be a void as far as the constitutional set- up of the society \nis concerned. But, this argument has also certain dangers. Nobody can be \nallowed to contend that arrears are mounting up and therefore, there has \nbeen some inadequate performance at some levels even by our judicial \ninstitutions and somebody else can start performing those functions. If \nany of the organs of the State Government do not perform their functions, \nthen they have to be pressed into action to perform their functions. Any \nof the other constitutional agencies do not take over the functions of \nthe other organ and start performing them, and therefore, when we discuss \nthe performance of our own judicial institution, this is one restraint in \n\n 368 \nterms of statesmanship, avoidance of any form of overreach, at the same \ntime, making sure that the judicial power of review is adequately \nexercised which the court will have to really bear in mind. Sir, when we \nare particularly increasing their salaries and increasing the salaries \nsignificantly, I consider it a welcome step, a welcome step for the \nreason that judicial offices have to be made to appear to be attractive, \npeople have to be incentivised to go and get elevated and get into those \nattractive offices. If those adequate incentives are not provided, the \nbest within the system will not be incentivised enough to go and occupy \nthose offices, and this is already one problem which the judicial \ninstitution in this country is facing. We have a judicial set up which is \nbroadly independent;  \n\n 369 \nwe have a judicial set up which is broadly fair; we have a judicial set \nup which is powerful, but, at the same time, if we consider some of the \nproblems which confront the institution, one of the serious problems is \nwith regard to the quality of persons who occupy these offices. \nHistorically, the best within the system were incentivised to occupy \nthose offices. As the system proceeded, we have now found a situation \nwhere that incentive is lacking, and even from amongst members of the \nBar, the best within the system are unwilling to get elevated. The most \ncompetent amongst them are unwilling to get elevated, and therefore, the \nincentive to occupy those offices is lacking, and when this incentive is \nlacking, how do we re-think about the system within which we are able to \nappoint the very best because ultimately, the quality of the institution \nwill have bearing on the kind of faith people have within the system \nitself? It is extremely necessary that institutions like the judicial \ninstitution, institutions like the Parliament, remain objects in which \nthere is a popular faith in democracy. If people start losing faith in \nthe judicial institutions or in the Parliamentary system, the very \nfundamentals of our democracy get weakened, and this country cannot \nafford to fall into that sort of a situation, and therefore, we need to \nimprove the system wherever we feel a problem has arisen. \nSir, today, when I speak in terms of quality, there has been a great \ndebate going on as to what the procedure of appointments should be as far \nas the judges are concerned. Conventionally, we followed a system where \nthe Chief Justice of India would send his recommendations to the \nGovernment, and after certain element of consultation, the Government \nwould decide whether to appoint them or not. But inevitably, the \nGovernment followed the advice of the Chief Justice. Till 1982, when this \nwas crystallised and a sanction was given judicially to an opinion by a \nnarrow margin of a divided court that the last word is really of \nGovernment, this was then re- considered and post 1993, we are now living \nin a situation where effectively in India, it is the judges who appoint \njudges. In India, it is not the President, acting through the elected \nGovernment, who appoints judges. They may be formally involved in some \nprocedure of consultation; they may be the executive appointing \nauthorities but the functions performed by the Government now in matters \n\n 370 \nof judicial appointments have become most minimal and the predominant \nword is of the judicial institution. In 1998, we corrected it further \nthrough judicial pronouncement again, and it was not only the Chief \nJustice of India who is appointing the judges, but the Collegium came up. \nNow, two observations have to be made. On the basis of this Collegium \nfunctioning in the last 10 to 11 years, the entire process of \nconsultation has become extremely slow, and while it has become slow, you \nwill always find that about 25 to 30 per cent of judicial posts in the \nhigher judiciary of this country, particularly at the level of the High \nCourts, are vacant. \nEach High Court would have five to seven to eight vacancies. The \nnormal strength of the vacancies,  at  any  given  point  of  time,  in  \nthe national High Courts, taken together, is anything  \n\n 371 \nbetween 150 and 200. The process of consultations has become very slow. \nYou know the date when a judge is going to retire. His retirement date is \nknown on the date of his appointment itself, and, therefore, the \nrequirement, really, is that six months before he retires, the process of \nappointing a successor should be set into motion. But, inevitably, this \ndoes not happen. Therefore, it is collectively that you appoint. And, one \nof the reasons why collectively you appoint is that the Collegiums, now, \nrepresent the collective wisdom. And because Collegiums recommend the \ncollective wisdom, and too many minds go into this whole process, \nindividual preferences, objective views, and also subjective views, about \npersons also get into it. The question that we have to pose and ask \nourselves, after ten years of the Collegiums having functioned, is: have \nwe been able to improve the quality of judges we are appointing in this \ncountry? And I have no hesitation in saying 'there is no improvement in \nthe entire process of judicial appointments that we are making in this \ncountry!' How do we, then, improve upon the quality? That is a \nfundamental question which arises before us. To the question 'who has the \nlast word', I think, earlier, the Executive had the last word; then, the \nChief Justice of India had the last world; now, the collegium has the \nlast word. The real issue has now altered from 'what was the debate', \nwhich has been conventionally going on, from 'who must have the last \nword', to the real issue 'as to what is the criterion on the basis of \nwhich you appoint judges'. Is the criterion going to be entirely \nsubjective? Because the Collegium's decisions cannot be called into \nquestion by the Government, the Government can only refer it back once, \nand if the Collegium, again, says, \"No. We insist that he be appointed\", \nhe would be appointed! My suggestion, therefore, is that we seriously \nreconsider, now, can we think in terms of a criterion by which the \nquality of judicial appointments can be improved? In all the systems we \nhave followed till date, systematically, the quality has been coming \ndown. And the quality has been coming down because subjective opinions \nabout individuals have taken precedence over the objective criterion on \nwhich the judges must be appointed. There can well be situations where \nfive judicial appointments are to be made in a high court; instead of \nappointing the best five who are available, you may pick up somebody who \ndoesn't fall in the first one thousand, and it is not whether under this \n\n 372 \nGovernment or under the previous Government. I am personally aware of \ncases where the selected ones are chosen, and once a person is chosen, he \nremains in office till he retires. Removal, in this country, has almost \nbecome unknown. And, therefore, the damage that can be done is, the \noffice can either mature you; the office can also provide you immunity \nfrom any form of criticism. If a person with inadequate abilities is \nelevated, then he is capable of becoming even a burden on the system. So, \nmy suggestion would be that the hon. Law Minister, at some stage, should \nconsider a debate as to whether the discretion available to the \nCollegiums can be restricted by legislative changes. And when I say that \nthe discretion available to the Collegiums must be restricted by \nlegislative changes, what I, specifically,  have  in  mind  is  that  \nnow,  with the entire records and the functioning of our judicial  \n\n 373 \nsystem having become computerised, the objective details of a person who \nis considered, must be available what are his academic qualifications; \nwhat is the kind of experience he has at the Bar; how many cases he has \nargued and how many cases that he has argued, are, actually reported; in \nhow many cases he has got the law altered or laid down. \nWhat is the number of youngsters that he has trained under him? What \nis his income, here is a potential available. In Government, every time \nwhen a number of vacancies come up there is some threshold of \nqualification. Today we have a situation before the Collegium where there \nis no threshold of qualification. If five people are to be appointed, the \ncandidate on merit at No. 1000 can be chosen and appointed. It can't be \ncalled into question before any court. The Government can, at best, \nreturn it once. It can't be criticised. That is the system under which we \nfunction. Therefore, the real question, if we look at the declining \nquality of the appointees, as far as judicial officers are concerned, is \nthat we must now seriously start a debate whether the discretion of the \nCollegium must be regulated by objective criteria with regard to the kind \nof persons that the Collegium select. Amongst the short- listed people, \nthe Judges certainly are best equipped to know as to what is the \nintegrity level of a candidate, how he will particularly perform as a \nJudge. Some element of discretion would always be available to them. But \nwhen they enter into the zone of consideration, there must be some \nthreshold that they have to cross. Otherwise, I am afraid, we will not be \nable to improve the quality of judicial appointments and the quality \ncan't be improved by merely altering the question. So far –  I include \nmyself as part of that responsibility –  we have, in the last 25 years, \ndebated the principal question: Who should appoint Judges? The real \nquestion is: What are the criteria on which a person is appointed as a \nJudge? I think, even under the present system, it is these criteria which \nnow require to be seriously brought in. \nSecondly, when we speak in terms of improving the quality of Judges, \nthe issue of accountability also is extremely important. Normally, courts \nare not criticised within our kind of system. Public debate on the \nfunctioning of individuals is discouraged. Contempt laws are there. \nUnless you are pushed to an extreme situation, normally, you don't bring \nout these issues for a public debate because you don't want to bring down \n\n 374 \nthe popular faith, as far as the institution is concerned. I read the \nstatement of the hon. Law Minister. I think he made it yesterday. He said \nit earlier also. There is an extreme position which is the impeachment \nprocess. Now, the impeachment process is exercised in the rarest of the \nrare cases. It can't be utilised in every case. I do hope that such \nsituations don't arise that we have to use it frequently. But then short \nof that, certain checks and safeguards are required. There are issues of \ncomplaints. There are other issues. After all, institutions are manned by \nmen. It is not that they are always infallible. Some men are bound to \nmake mistakes and let us not forget the fact that the entry into the \njudicial institution brings out a sense of fairness and sense of \nresponsibility in any entrant into that institution, in a very large \nnumber of cases. In some cases the immunity  which  a Judge enjoys can \nactually work adversely, if his individual moorings, integrity and  \n\n 375 \nfairness are not of the best quality. Now, in such cases the Judges, so \nfar, have followed an in -house mechanism in resolving these issues. The \nin-house mechanism has worked in some cases and not worked in other \ncases, and the tendency also does exist that unless pushed to an extreme \nthe in-house mechanism doesn't work. Too many cases need not be brought \ninto the mechanism because that will again bring a very bad name, as far \nas the institution is concerned. So, the hon. Law Minister is quite right \nwhen he says that the in -house mechanism is where he has not  succeeded. \nTherefore, you require some kind of an institutionalised mechanism which \nwill go in these questions of serious misdemeanours when they arise. The \nextreme situation is impeachment and we have the Judges' inquiry \nlegislation. But what happens in all other cases? We have an issue of the \nPunjab High Court which has been hanging fire and it has almost become a \nmatter of public debate. We have another issue where some hon. Members of \nthis House have submitted a memorandum to the Chairman. Do we have a  \nmechanism which exists today which commands popular confidence? I humbly \nsubmit that such a mechanism really does not exist. I have one area of \ndifference with the hon. Law Minister that when such mechanism is created \nby the legislation that he has proposed, this must essentially be a \njudge's own mechanism. If it is a mechanism of only the judge's, then the \nonly change that comes about in such a mechanism is that the present \ninformal mechanism which is functioning becomes a statutory mechanism. \nThe structure of that does not change. It acquires a little bit of a \nstatutory form. What do standards and norms of public accountability \nrequire? When there are serious complaints and the matter needs to be \ninquired into, can it only be left to the in- house mechanism, whether it \nis statutory or non- statutory? Therefore, some element of participation \nis required; it may be minority participation, but it has to be in the \nnature of a public participation. The law will have to be structured. The \nNational Judicial Commission, which was suggested earlier, had \npredominantly a larger number of judges; the majority was judges. Then \ntwo of the members suggested were, one nominee of the Government; the Law \nMinister would be best suited for that purpose and the second person, an \neminent citizen could be appointed by a Collegium comprising the Prime \nMinister, the Leader of the Opposition and the Speaker, Three of them \nappoint a very eminent public man who enjoys very high credibility level \nto be a party to it. It is this participation which will ensure that \n\n 376 \nfairness is done to the person against whom the complaint has come, and \nfairness is also done in the matter of looking into the allegation of the \ncomplaint. Such a mechanism even from the point of view of judges will \ncarry a much higher credibility level. Today, when we are having a debate \nin the House with regard to improving the quality of the judicial system, \nand this Bill which seeks to increase the salary by two- and-a-half to \nthree times, in fact, it is one of the good steps in the right direction \nbecause it incentivises and makes the office more attractive so that the \nbest talent within the system gets attracted to it. These are some \nchanges and reforms which are required in this particular matter. There \nare a large number of  issues such as arrears; how the courts must  \nfunction; what changes the Government can bring about, etc. Since they \nhave been debated, I  \n\n 377 \nam not referring to them. But I have these two suggestions to make with \nregard to the appointment process and with regard to the inquiry into the \nmisdemeanours. With these words, I support the Bill which the hon. \nMinister has brought as a step in the right direction. Thank you. \nSHRI SHANTARAM LAXMAN NAIK (Goa): Mr. Deputy Chairman, Sir, I stand to \nsupport the Bill moved  by the hon. Law Minister on the subject of \nincreasing the salary of high court and the Supreme Court judges. I would \nsay that whenever such Bills have come before the House, Members have \nbeen kind-enough, gracious-enough and with all broadmindedness, they have \nnever, never opposed any such legislation. That is because the judiciary \nor judges deal with very diverse problems, including human problems, \nwhere fate of husband and wife is decided, fate of mother and child is \ndecided, matters worth crores and crores of rupees are decided by the \njudicial officers. Even the counsel who argues before them, his five \nminutes salary will be ten times more than the monthly salary of the \npresiding judge. This is the situation. We cannot compensate or bring \neverything at par. I would not say that it should be done because of the \nSixth Pay Commission or because of inflation; even otherwise, the salary \nof judicial officers must be on a very, very high pedestal. \nYou cannot even compare them with others. This is my point. Similarly, \nwe have to look after salaries and perks of the Lower Judiciary. Of \ncourse, fundamentally, the State Governments will have to deal with this. \nBut the status of the Lower Judiciary, the Civil Judges, District Judges \nand Additional District Judges, is not favourable. This is especially \ntrue in the case of Judicial Magistrate First- Class. The Judicial \nMagistrate First-Class may convict a person or may acquit a person. But, \nwhen a person is acquitted, he will travel in the same public transport \nused by the Judicial Magistrate. Perhaps the person, who is convicted and \nwhose sentence has been suspended, will also travel in the same public \ntransport. You can imagine the plight of the judicial officer travelling \nin a public transport together with them. Today, the powers of Civil \nJudges or Senior Judges are quite high in their respective jurisdictions. \nJudgements are passed with large consequences. Therefore, we should also \ntake care of their salaries and perks. Sir, judges stand on a high \npedestal, as I said, and the judges are held in high esteem. We, the \npoliticians, also hold the judges in high esteem. But, sadly speaking, it \n\n 378 \nis not true vice-versa. We are looked at with contempt. You might have \nseen the various observations and remarks made by the Judiciary outside. \nWe are not treated in a fair manner. I would like to bring this to the \nattention of the hon. Law Minister and request him to do whatever is \npossible on this matter. In fact, some time back, it was reported in \nreputed newspapers when the Judiciary seemed to have remarked, \"What was \nParliament doing? Were they sleeping?\" These were the words used, and we \ndid not do anything about it. Day in, day out, we hear such remarks \nagainst politicians, be it the MLAs or the MPs. If you recollect, Sir, \nthe railway pass of some ex- MP was  cancelled  by a judicial \npronouncement, and then Parliament had to bring in some legislation or,  \n\n 379 \nperhaps, they had to modify some rule to regularise it. This is the \ntreatment that we get. However, the year 2008 had not been a very \npleasant year as far as the Judiciary is concerned. Various allegations \nof corruption have been made. Of course, there is a procedure to go into \nit. I will not go into it in detail. But there is a demand from the Bar \nAssociation of India that it should have a statutory role, like the one \nin the U.S., while dealing with such matters. Somehow, some restraint or \nsome kind of a curtailment can be there in this Bill. I really do not \nknow how it can be done. But this is a thought- provoking statement that \nthere must be a statutory backing for regulating these things. \nThen, I fully agree with the views expressed regarding Collegium. \nEarlier, the power was with the Government. Then, the Chief Justice \nassumed powers. Now, we have the Collegium on the appointment of judges. \nBut let there not be an impression created that Collegium involves \nrotation. If this has to be avoided, then, a suitable legislation, laying \ndown criteria in the matter of appointment of judges, should be brought \nabout. This will bring transparency in the matter of appointments. Now, \nthe question whether the Collegium's word is final or, the Government's \nword is final, or, the word of the Chief Justice is final, has to be \ndecided. But a proper legislation will bring about transparency in the \nmatter. \nNow, the issue of encroachment is a sensitive one. The Prime Minister, \nDr. Manmohan Singh, once said, in a polite manner, \"Judiciary should not \nbreach the constitutionality of demarcated boundaries.\" \nBut the reply given by Judiciary was that they did not encroach upon \nany matter unless the Legislature failed to act. Now, Sir, if the \nLegislature fails, does any other organ of the Government have the power \nto pass or amend a law? Who has given such a power to any other organ of \nthe State? I would repeat the argument which I have been making and which \nhas been referred to by my colleague here; in case, tomorrow, the \nExecutive says that, because thousands of judgments are pending in the \nSupreme Court and in High Courts, the Prime Minister would pass the \norders, or, similarly, the Chief Ministers of States would say that since \njudgements were pending in the High Court and the Judiciary was not doing \nits duty, he would pass the order, what would happen? Can this logic \nsurvive? And if this logic cannot survive, the other logic also cannot \n\n 380 \nsurvive. Therefore, each organ has to have its own restraint; they have \nto understand what their respective powers are. Today, laws are created \nmore through interpretations. If you see the law that exists today in the \nform of judgments, it is much more than the laws that we have passed. It \nis ten or 20 times more. As a result, it is very difficult to comprehend \nwhat the law is in a given area. Therefore, this sort of increasing \nencroachment has to find some way. It is not an exaggeration, but I will \nnot be surprised if a day may come when courts of law will pass order to \nthe Presiding Officer of this House or the other House saying that the \ncourt restrains the Presiding Officer of this House or the other from \nadmitting a Calling Attention Motion or a Question, or from admitting a \ndiscussion.  \n\n 381 \nIn our rules, Sir, we have to amend or delete that rule which has \nexisted for all these years. That is an anomaly. We normally say that \nmatters pending in courts cannot be taken up in the form of a question, \netc. There are many public matters which have to be discussed. But there \nis this rule which says that matters pending in courts cannot be \ndiscussed. So, somebody can make use of this clause and can restrain us. \nSince most of the matters which are discussed today are pending in courts \nof law, this rule should be deleted from the Statute Book to avoid any \ncontroversy because ultimately we will have to because of Public Interest \nLitigations. Most of the matters that we discuss are subject- matters of \ncourts in some form or the other. Therefore, Sir, we have to delete that \nparticular clause. Now, Sir, in order to bring transparency in the \nfunctioning of the Government, the Government of India has graciously \nenacted the Right to Information Act. No Department can dare to refuse \ninformation. Whenever information is denied, they go in appeal and the \nappellate authority decides it. A very fine and transparent procedure is \nthere. Every Government department follows it. But, what about the \nJudiciary? It does not. When the Central Information Commission passed an \norder that this Act applied to Judiciary also, what has the Supreme Court \ndone? The Supreme Court has challenged the order of the CIC in the Delhi \nHigh Court. Tell me, Sir, what would be predicam ent of the Judge or the \nJudges who decide against the Supreme Court because it is the Supreme \nCourt which is the aggrieved party, which has brought it before the Delhi \nHigh Court saying that it is aggrieved and the CIC's order should be \ncancelled. \nNow, what is to be done by the Delhi High Court, I don't know. \nSupposing, this matter ultimately goes to the Supreme Court, either way; \nwho will be deciding? The Supreme Court will be deciding. Initially, who \nwas the applicant? It was the Supreme Court. Now, how is it to be \nresolved? Mr. Law Minister, Sir, you have to find some answer to it. You \nhave to protect that legislation, and see to it that judges themselves do \nnot decide this matter, or, you have to amend it and make a clarifying \nclause, as it is, by Parliament that it will apply, irrespective of this \nthing, to the Supreme Court also. Let it be said in clear terms, so that \neverything is resolved. Thereafter, if they will strike down the clause, \n\n 382 \nI don't know. That is possible. Even if you put a clause that it shall \napply to the Supreme Court, they may strike it down. That is possible. \nThey are the supreme. \nNow, Sir, secondly, I would like to say that nowadays judgements are \ngoing against legal literacy. What I am saying is, every citizen has to \nknow the law of the land, the fundamental laws. But, if you see the trend \nof the courts, they write long, long judgements, and to find out what is \ntheir pronouncement, it takes a lot of time. As a result, the task which \nour Law Ministry is doing today to spread legal literacy, these \njudgements go against that. The laws are to be made simple. As it is, the \nlegislations which pass, sometimes are complicated. If you interpret \njudgements of thousands of pages,  it  gets  further  complicated. \nTherefore, the common citizen does not have the access to the  \n\n 383 \nknowledge in regard to what a law is. Sometimes, Sir, I feel that in the \nage of computerisation, now, whether it is possible to do it. We have to \nexamine it, because if certain propositions of law are decided from time \nto time, there can be a software by which the facts of the case are put \nin the computer. The proposition of law, as decided earlier, can be put; \nand बटन दबाओ जजम�ट पाओ। In this way, the judgement can come out. Can we \ngo into such a world? I mean, that is not a bad idea, Sir, because human \ndiscussion is going astray these days. When human discussion going \nastray, let us resort to this, Sir, that a software can be developed by \nwhich facts and laws are fed in the computer. Thereafter, a judgement can \nbe sought from Mr. Computer on this occasion. \nThen, Sir, I am coming to a slightly different but a related matter. \nAs far as the Election Commission is concerned, we have got article 324. \nDuring the entire period of election, everybody has experienced it now \nthat there does not exist any Judiciary. Why I am saying this is because \nall the petitions related to it are to be decided after elections. There \nare so many orders which are passed by the Election Commission \npurportedly to be under article 324. They write letters day -in-day-out \npurportedly to be law, and if it violates any of my fundamental right or \nany other law, I have no remedy. The courts also give sanctity to such \nletters which are said to be judgements. They ask the Chief Electoral \nOfficer to follow them. Everyday they will be writing letters now \npurportedly under article 324. Can you find some solution to this, Sir? \nIt is very, very essential because those who contest elections or those \nwho work or assist them become very, very helpless to get these letters, \nwhich are written from the Election Commission, asking them to do this \nand do that. They will just forget about the Representation of People \nAct, they will forget about the constitutional provisions, and they will \nwrite letters, which, according to them, is the law of the land. During \nthose three weeks, nobody can do anything about it. Therefore, Sir, \nsomething has to be done in that context too. \nLastly, Sir, I would like to touch a small issue relating to my State \nfor the consideration of the hon. Law Minister. \nWe had in Goa what is called Judicial Commissioner's Court, it was a \nHigh Court. That court had full powers of a High Court. Absolutely, it \nwas not a bench of any court, but a full- fledged High Court. Before, we \nwere under the Portuguese regime, when we had a High Court and the \n\n 384 \njurisdiction of our Goa High Court during the Portuguese regime extended \noverseas to other countries, to other colonies of Portugal. This was the \nstatus of a High Court which we had. Ultimately, when we became a State, \nwe were given a bench of the Bombay High Court. We were entitled for a \nfull-fledged High Court. But, we were given a bench of the neighbouring \nHigh Court. \nNow, technically it is a fulfilment of a Constitutional requirement. \nThey club together several States and they give one. The State was \nentitled for a High Court, but a bench was given. But, the Government of \nIndia should now consider, after several years, the demand of Goa for a \nfull-fledged High Court. \n\n 385 \nSecondly, Sir, despite the fact that we had a strong judicia l \nbackground, a very good High Court, I do not know if there can be a \nState-wise representation in the Supreme Court, but I would say, Sir, Goa \njudicial officers, Goan lawyers must find a place on the bench of the \nSupreme Court of – India. It is essential,  Sir. Please consider these \ndemands. Thank you. \nSHRI P.R. RAJAN (Kerala): Thank you, Mr. Deputy Chairman, Sir. First \nof all, I welcome and support this Bill presented by the hon. Law \nMinister – The High Court and Supreme Court Judges (Salaries and \nConditions of Service) Amendment Bill, 2009. I welcome and support this \nBill because considering the hard work and the number of cases to be \ndealt with by the judges and the responsibilities that rest on them, it \nis highly and absolutely necessary to revise the salaries and allowances \nand conditions of service of the judges. It is reported that there are \nmore than 50,000 cases pending in the apex court itself. So, it is highly \nnecessary to appoint more judges to dispose of those cases. We must \nensure that all citizens who approach the apex courts must be delivered \nwith justice in a speedy manner. Justice delayed is justice denied. \nBut, Sir, I think, I must take this opportunity to share some thoughts \non the judicial system, especially the appointment of judges and to \ndecide their service conditions. My party, the CPI(M) strongly believes \nthat the Parliamentary democratic system of our country, as well as the \nexecutive, public and the judiciary must be given a role in appointing \njudges even in the higher courts. \nRecently, a healthy and open discussion has been initiated. It has \nbeen reported in the newspapers on the functioning of the judiciary. The \nChief Justice of the Supreme Court, the Prime Minister, the Lok Sabha \nSpeaker and many leading citizens of the country had taken part in this \ndiscussion. Sir, I would like to bring to the attention of the House that \nthe CPI(M), when raised certain criticisms against the shortcomings and \nweaknesses in the functioning of the judiciary and against the approach \nof certain judgments of the Supreme Court and the High Courts, a section \ncharacterised the criticisms as an attack on the judicial system. \nThere are reports that a section of the Judges is corrupt. The dispute \nis only about the percentage. There are allegations that some Judges are \nshowing favouritism and some are insufficient. \n\n 386 \nSir, I would like to bring to the notice of the House a recent letter \nof the Chief Justice of India, Justice Shri K.G. Balakrishnan's, \naddressed to the Chief Justices of High Courts, and it has been widely \nreported in our media, that those Judges who do not have the \nacceptability of the trust of the people, it will be appreciable if they \nthemselves leave the position as a Judge. \nSir, judgements ignore the interests of the poorer sections and \noppressed and restrict the efforts to ameliorate their conditions by \nsocial intervention. Some judgements ignore the interests of the common \npeople by their complete surrender to the globalisation philosophy. \n\n 387 \nSir, the method of appointment of Judges is also a matter to be \ndiscussed. In India it is not open to public scrutiny and lacks \naccountability and transparency. Even a reasonable criticism against a \njudgement can be branded as contempt of court as per the provisions of \nthe present law. Perhaps, it is only  in India that Judges are appointed \nby Judges alone. In India, till 1993, Executive had a role in the \nappointment of Judges. The nine- Judge Bench with the majority of the \nseven, by an innovative interpretation of the Constitution, took over the \npower of appointing Judges in its own hands. This came in the background \nof a weak Executive at the Centre. It held that the recommendation for \nappointment of a Judge should be made by the Chief Justice of India in \nconsultation with his two senior- most colleagues and that recommendation \nshould be given effect to by the hon. Present. As regards the method of \nappointing Judges by Judges itself it is a challenge to the four- pillar \ndemocratic system of our country. The collegium Judges who appoint the \nJudges may help in creating a coterie in future. It can also lead to the \nappointment of friends and relatives of the collegium Judges. \nThe 1993 decision was reaffirmed in a unanimous opinion rendered by a \nnine-Judge Bench of the Supreme Court in 1998 on a reference made by th e \nPresident under article 143 of the Constitution. The method of \nappointment of Judges by Judges themselves poses challenges to the \ndemocratic system. The present system is incapable to appropriately \naddress the diversities of India, and is not open to public scrutiny and \nlacks accountability and transparency. The system of appointment should \nbe transparent and subject to public scrutiny. The public should be given \nan opportunity to express their views on the names nominated for the post \nof a Judge. \nThe present law on contempt of court and the system of trial is \ninherited from the British feudal system. A common man has a right to \napproach the court against slanders and libels. In a democratic system it \nis not appropriate to give more powers to the Judges than what is open to \na common man. The system of insulating the judiciary from public scrutiny \nand opinion with the threat of contempt of court is unjust and dangerous \nin a democratic system particularly in our country. \nThe second Administrative Reforms Commission headed by Veerappa Moily \nhas recommended to the Government in January 2007 to constitute a \n\n 388 \nNational Judicial Council having the representation of the executive, \nlegislature and judiciary for recommending names of the judges for \nappointment and to decide their service conditions etc. of Supreme Court \nand High Court Judges for enquiry into the misconduct, for imposing \npenalties and recommending removal of judges also. My party has \nrecommended the constitution of a National Judicial Commission with \nrepresentatives from the three organs of the Government, -  Executive, \nLegislature and Judiciary -  representatives of the Bar Association and \nthe general public. The Commission should be vested with powers to make \nrecommendations for the appointment of judges, to conduct enquiries into \nthe misbehaviour of judges,  to impose minor punishments and to recommend \nto the President for the removal of judges  \n\n 389 \n3.00 P.M. \nfor misbehaviour and incapacity. Before concluding, Sir, I would request \nthe House and the Government that the basic nature of democracy should be \nmade applicable also to the appointment and to decide the service \nconditions and to fix the salaries of the judges also. Once again, Sir, I \nsupport the Bill. \nPROF P. J. KURIEN (Kerala): Sir, the independence of Judiciary is \nparamount for a healthy democracy. I support and I welcome this Bill \nwhich seeks to increase the salaries of judges. When the cost of living \nhas gone high and the salaries of the bureaucrats have been increased \nthere is adequate justification for asking such a Bill. Furthermore, as \nit has been already pointed out, there are very eminent advocates who are \nhaving lucrative practice and those advocates are not prepared to be \nelevated to the Bench because, for example, Mr. Ram Jethmalani is here, \nsuch a senior advocate, a man getting such a... \nSHRI RAM JETHMALANI (Nominated): No one has ever offered me the post \nof a judge. I have not been found respectable enough. \nPROF. P. J. KURIEN: You are a luminary in that field. Even Arun \nJaitley who was speaking here, see, so many good advocates are there but \nthey do not go to the Bench. The reason is very simple. If you go to the \nBench, the income and salary is less. You can get ten times or even \nhundred times more income by being a good advocate. So, the increase in \nsalary is nominal. (Interruptions) The hon. Minister who is also a good \nadvocate refused to become a judge. So, therefore, I welcome this Bill \nand I have no complaints even if judges are giving little more. Having \nsaid that, I have to respond to my previous speaker. I have to say that \nwe have an experience in Kerala where the judiciary has come to our help \nwhen there are mass struggles and agitations, especially States' \nsponsored bandhs and agitations. The previous speaker, my good friend \nsaid that judiciary should not give judgement against mass struggles and \nmass agitations. I did not want to bring this point but because he spoke, \nI have to speak. We have in our State experience where bandhs are \nsponsored by the State, where nobody will be allowed to move even if they \ncall it hartal. In effect, it will be bandhs. Pregnant mothers who want \nto go to hospitals for delivery are not allowed. \n\n 390 \nThose who are suffering from accident or if an accident took place, \nthey are not allowed. If a person is about to die of heart attack, he is \nnot allowed. In every bandh, 2, 3 or 4 people die in our State. What is \nthe remedy? Sir, the remedy is available only from judiciary. Our High \nCourt has given a judgment that there shall not be any bandhs, because \nbandhs, especially State-sponsored... \nSHRI P. R. RAJAN: Sir, the court has not said like that. \n...(Interruptions)... \nPROF. P. J. KURIEN: If bandh is allowed, it also curtails the \nFundamental Rights of citizens. It is my right to walk. It is my right to \nwork. In our country, a few people want to undertake strike. They can do \nso. Why should they oppose others doing work? Same is the case with \nParliament. Suppose,  \n\n 391 \n50 Members want to create problem, they can create problem by walking \nout of the House. Why should they block or interrupt the entire House? \nTherefore, judiciary has taken a welcome step by intervening and \nprotecting the rights of citizens by banning bandhs and giving a verdict \nagainst mass agitations. The important thing is that the law has been \nupheld. The judiciary is upholding the law. So, I welcome that. Sir, \nbandhs or hartals may be either State-sponsord or whatever it is, but, at \nthe same time, every citizen has a right and that right has to be \nprotected. If the Government do not protect it, it is the duty of the \njudiciary to protect it. That is what I wanted to say. \nThe second point is: Sir, my friend has said about the 'institutional \ncourtesy.' It is correct. My friend, Shantaram Naik has already said it. \nNo institution has to cross the Laxman Rekha. Neither Parliament should \nstep into the realm of judiciary nor should judiciary step into the realm \nof Parliament. But, unfortunately, in certain judicial pronouncements, \nthere are unnecessary interventions about the whole lot of politicians. \nIf one politician is found guilty by courts, it is only one individual. \nEveryone can be a politician. To some extent, every citizen is a \npolitician, because he will have something in politics. And, if a \njudicial pronouncement says, 'all politicians are barred', it amounts to \nsaying that all Indians are barred. So, this kind of crossing the \nLakshman Rekha is not correct. I don't know how it can be stopped. Let \nthe hon. Minister, who is an expert in these matter, give a remedy to it. \nSir, the next point is about judicial activism. Here also, I differ \nwith my friend. My experience from what I read from newspapers and from \nothers things, the judicial activism has helped the society in a number \nof cases. As has been said, 'how judicial activism helped?' I am not an \nadvocate. So, I have an objective view. And, advocates have some \nsubjective view. Since I am not an advocate, my view is a layman's view \ni.e., objective view. Sir, from where the judicial activism came in. No \ndoubt, when the State failed, judicial activism came in. Take for \nexample, Public Interest Litigation. If my memory is correct, it is \nJustice V.R. Krishna Iyer who permitted the PIL for the first time in \nKerala. And a number of PILs have helped to correct the anomalies in the \nsociety and to call the Government for action. I am not saying this way \nor that way or whether it is beneficial or not. So, judicial activism, \n\n 392 \nper se, cannot be blamed. If the Government fails, what is the remedy? \nNow, you say, judiciary can interfere and correct if the Government does \nsomething wrong. If the wrong doings of the Government can be corrected \nby judiciary, a question can be put to them that a failure of judiciary \ncan also be corrected by the Government, because wrong doing is also a \nfailure. According to my personal view, the judicial activism has helped. \nThere may be a few cases in which judiciary might have overstepped. But, \nby and large, judicial activism has helped and that is what I would like \nto say. \nThen, Sir, what has been said about the process of appointment of \njudges is very correct. Judges appoint themselves! This is not good for \ndemocracy; this is not good even for Judiciary itself.  \n\n 393 \nTherefore, as recommended by the Law Commission, certain suggestions are \npending here. So, I would like to request the hon. Law Minister that this \npresent process of 'judges appointing themselves' should be corrected and \nnecessary law should be enacted in this regard. I have even spoken to \nsome of the judges, who are personal friends of mine. They also \npersonally told me that they also feel that this should be corrected. A \nvery senior retired judge, from Kerala, personally told me that this \nprocess of 'judges appointing themselves' should be corrected. So, what I \nam saying is that even judges will also agree with this. But the system \ntoday is allowing them; so, they are doing it. Whose duty is it to \ncorrect it? It is our duty. It is the duty of the Legislature to correct \nit. Therefore, I request the hon. Minister to take necessary action in \nthis regard. \nThen, my friend has mentioned about an RTI case that has been referred \nto the High Court by the Supreme Court. And, he has criticised that. I \nthink, by doing so, the Supreme Court has shown the best examples of \nconduct because the Supreme Court itself is a party in that. When this \nRTI case came before the Supreme Court – right or wrong, I don't want to \nsay anything in this regard because I am not a legal luminary – the Chief \nJustice of the Supreme Court had taken a view. There will certainly be \nsubjectivity in that. And, knowing that, the Chief Justice of India has \nleft it to the High Court for judicial scrutiny and a judicial verdict. I \nthink, this is one of the best examples of conduct one can show. How can \nit be criticised? Then, my friend doubts that if the Supreme Court has \nreferred a matter to the High Court, what the High Court judges will do. \nThey should take independent view that's why they are appointed there. If \nthat question is extended — if the President makes a reference to the \nSupreme Court, can you say that because the President has made a \nreference to the Supreme Court, the Supreme Court will say what the \nPresident wants? Not at all. Our Judges, right from the lowest rung to \nthe highest strata, are the men of character and integrity. Some of them \nmay not be having integrity, they may be corrupt. But they are few. But I \ncan say that most of our judges are men of character and integrity. There \nmay be some corruption for which we have to take some action. But it is \nwrong to think that because the Supreme Court has referred the matter to \nthe High Court the judges will be afraid of giving a judgement according \n\n 394 \nto law and as per their conscience. I think, the Chief Justice of India \nhas done a right thing by referring the matter, where he himself is a \nparty, for legal scrutiny. He could have easily not referred the matter \nto the High Court and would have said that it was his own duty. But he \ndid not do that. It is the highest example of democratic and judicial \nbehaviour that even the Chief Justice of India is submitting his views to \nthe High Court for legal scrutiny. This is what I feel. \nSHRI SHANTARAM LAXMAN NAIK: What if they give contrary judgement? \nPROF. P. J. KURIEN: As I said earlier, I am not a legal luminary. I am \nspeaking as a layman. My view is also subjective. \n\n 395 \nThen, I have to make a request to the hon. Law Minister. I have \nwritten to him as well as to the Chairman of the Parliamentary Standing \nCommittee also regarding the number of vacancies for judges that are \nlying there. Mr. Arun Jaitley says that these vacancies are always \npending. What is the meaning of retiring the High Court judges at the age \nof 62? \nWhat is the need? At 62 years, if they are incompetent, then, how can \nMinisters be there in office at 68- 70-75 years? Then, most of us will \nalso be incompetent. What is the justification for this? In fact, at 62 \nor 65 years of age, these judges will be more productive. When you \nappoint a judge, he is a new man. It will take 2 -3 or 4 years for him to \nunderstand and deliver better judgements. When the time comes for him to \ncontribute and deliver really good judgements, he is retired. Such \nexperienced and talented judges will be made to sit at home and you will \ngive him pension also. You will have to bring a new and incompetent \nperson to the Bench. What is the need for this? So, there is a strong \nneed for increasing the retirement age of judges. \nSecondly, what is the need for having a difference between the \nretirement age of High Court judges and the Supreme Court judges? I don't \nunderstand this because in the Supreme Court, law matters and \nconstitutional matters come, and interpretation of law also has to be \ndone. For this you need a more alert brain. There, the retirement age is \n65 years. And in High Courts, you ask them to retire at 62 years of age. \nThere is no need for having a disparity in retirement age of judges of \nHigh Courts and the Supreme Court. I request the hon. Minister to \nconsider this. This is not just my view. The Parliamentary Standing \nCommittee on Personnel, Public Grievances, Law and Justice has already \nrecommended that this disparity should be rectified and the same has been \nrecommended three times. I request the hon. Minister to seriously \nconsider, approve and accept the recommendations of the Standing \nCommittee. ...(Interruptions)... It should be increased and, in any case, \nboth should be equal. The age- limit can be increased up to 68 years. In \nAmerica, there are judges who are 70 and 75 years of age and they never \nretire. ...(Interruptions)... The Government has better wisdom. It can \nincrease the age up to 68 or 70. The Government should decide this. It \ncan increase the retirement age and, then, bring the retirement age of \n\n 396 \nthe High Court judges on a par with the Supreme Court judges. \nLastly, I would plead for the common man; the aam aadmi of our \ncountry. Sir, justice is very costly in our country. It is very costly. \nSir, ultimate justice is acquired from the Supreme Court. I don't think, \na Keralite, unless he is a millionaire, or, a Tamilian, unless he is a \nmillionaire, or, for that matter, anybody from your State, unless he is a \nmillionaire ...(Interruptions)... can come to the Supreme Court and file \na case. The expenses for coming here, meeting an advocate, fees paid to \nthe advocate, etc. are very high. There are some good advocates who will \ncharge less fees and argue your case. There are very few such advocates. \nA hefty fees is charged, but the common man does not,  ultimately,  get  \njustice.  It  is  a  fact.  Then, what is the way out? We also want \nultimate justice,  \n\n 397 \nwhatever may be the argument on the other side. Heavens will not fall if \na Bench of the Supreme Court is set up in the South. It could be in \nChennai or Mangalore. Heavens will not fall if a Bench of the Supreme \nCourt is set up in East Kolkata or Bhubaneswar. And, heavens will not \nfall if a Bench of the Supreme Court is set up on the Western side. This \nis needed for getting ultimate justice for the aam aadmi. Sir, I know of \nsome cases where the sentence is, 'hang till death', but, the accused \nsays that he is innocent. \nHe cannot approach them because he does not have money. Even such \ncases are there, I know. But I do not know why our Judiciary and our \nleadership are not thinking about this. For giving ultimate justice to \nthe common man, Judiciary should not be concentrated at one point. The \nSupreme Court should hold Benches in all these places. \nSir, one more point I would like to make. In Kerala, there is an \nagitation going on. It is my duty to bring that point to the notice of \nthe House. That agitation is by the lawyers of Trivandrum. They are \nasking for a bench of the High Court at Trivendrum. They are also saying \nthe same thing which I have said. It is my request to the hon. Minister \nto consider both these requests favourably and take appropriate action. \nWith these words, Sir, I fully support this Bill. Thank you very much. \nश्री वीरे न्द्र भा�टया (उत्तर प्रदे श): उपसभाप�त जी, म� इस �बल का समथर्न तो करता ह� हूं, हमारे \nप्रो. पी.जे. कु �रयन साहब ने अभी जो कु छ कहा है, उसका भी समथर्न करता हूं। अभी श्री अरुण \nजेटल� जी कह रहे थे �क एक ल�मण रे खा कायर् पा�लका और न्याय पा�लका के बीच म� होनी चा�हए। \nहम प्राय: सदन म� और हर \nजगह यह बात कहते ह� �क न्याय पा�लका कायर् पा�लका के अ�धकार �ेत्र \nम� अ�तक्रमण कर रह� है, उस ल�मण रे खा का उल्लंघन कर रह� है, overstepping in the \njurisdiction of the executives. म � एक बात जरूर कहूंगा �क न्याय पा�लका जो कर रह� \nहै, उस पर आरोप लगाने से पहले हम� अपना आत्मालोचन भी करना चा�हए, अपने बारे म� भी सोचना \nचा�हए और यह सोचना चा�हए �क �कन प�रिस्थ�तय� म� न्याय पा�लका ने यह अ�धकार अपने हाथ म� ले \n�लया। आज तमाम ऐसे मामले ह�, िजनम� अगर न्याय पा�लका ने हस्त�ेप नह�ं �कया होता तो आज \nप�रिस