डील करने के �लए कोई way-out �नकलना चा�हए, जैसा �क लॉ ऐंड जिस्टस \nकमेट� ने �रकम�ड �कया। ले�कन म� एक बात जरूर कहना चाहता हूं �क independence of \nJudiciary बहुत जरूर� भी है। �कसी प्रकार से प्रत्य� या अप्रत्य� त र � के से उस पर प्रहार नह�ं होना \n\n 404 \nचा�हए। जब हम Judicial enquiry क� बात कर रहे ह�, जजेज क� इन्क्वायर� क� बात कर रहे ह� \nतो उच्च न्यायालय और सुप्रीम कोटर् के जजेज को अगर �कसी प्रकार का चाबुक उन पर महसूस होगा तो \nवे  \n*Withdrawn by the hon. Members \n\n 405 \n�नष्प�ता और �नभ�कता से न्याय नह�ं कर पाएंगे। कृ पा करक े जजेज को सीबीआई इन्क्वायर� से \nबचाइए। म� लॉ �म�नस्टर साहब को बधाई दे ता हूं �क इन्ह�ने खुले आम यह बात कह�। इसम� कु छ \nजजेज ऐसे फं स जाते ह� िजनका चेहरा �बल्कु ल पाक साफ है, िजनक े च�रत्र के बारे म� बीस साल से \nलोग जानते ह� �क बहुत ईमानदार जज ह�। आप एक बात कहकर, जो उसने अपनी प्र�तष्ठा बनायी थी \n- उस जज के पास उसक� प्र�तष्ठा ह� तो है - उसने जो अपना एंपायर बनाया था, जो उसक� पूंजी \nथी, आपने सीबीआई इन्क्वायर� कराकर उ स के चेहरे पर दाग लगा �दया। म� �सफ र् एक अं�तम वाक्य \nकहना चाहता हूं �क वैसे तो म� इस �बल का समथर्न करता हूं, ले�कन एक बात लॉ �म�नस्टर साहब से \nजरूर कहूंगा �क ज्यूडी�शयर� को तो आपने कु छ �दया, वक�ल� के �लए भी आप कु छ कर द�िजए, उनक े \n�लए भी इंश्योर�स होना चा�हए। आज वह िस्थ�त नह�ं है। या तो वक�ल बहुत ऊपर ह� जो दस लाख रुपए \nरोज लेते ह�। \nTHE VICE- CHAIRMAN (PROF. P. J. KURIEN): Advocates are getting very \ngood fees. \nSHRI VIRENDRA BHATIA: I don't agree with you. I know there is \ndisparity. अगर दस लाख रुपए चाजर् करने वाले वक�ल ह� तो ऐसे वक�ल भी ह� जो पांच रुपए भी \nनह�ं ले जाते। इस�लए म� कहूंगा �क उनक े �लए भी कु छ इंश्योर�स क� व्यवस्था क� जानी चा�हए। इन्ह�ं \nशब्द� के साथ म� एक बार �फर इस �बल का समथर्न करता हूं। \nSHRI RAM JETHMALANI: Sir, I hate to be autobiographical, but I do wish \nto share with this House that I took my legal education in the 30s and I \nstarted my practice in the 1941. I took my legal education in those \ncolleges and under those teachers who, day in and day out, impressed upon \nus that judges discharge divine functions. \nIt is the function of God to meet out justice to his creation, but, by \nthe compulsion of society, we have to appoint some human beings to meet \nout justice. But, the functions, which they are essentially discharging, \nare the functions of God. Sir, I am not exaggerating, but, practically, \nall my professional life, I have devoted to supporting the dignity and \nthe independence of Judges. And, Sir, look at the Constituent Assembly. \nWhat awesome powers did they confer upon our Judges - matters of life and \nliberty on criminal side; complete powers on the civil side, whatever be \nthe property involved, whatever be the rights involved, whether it is \nordinary property rights, or, intellectual property rights, complete sway \nover the avocations of people. But, Sir, the other power, which they \nconferred upon them was that Judges were made the guardians of the common \nman in this country. We are not merely a democracy. Our Constitution says \n\n 406 \nthat we are a Republic and a democracy. A republic is different from a \ndemocracy. In a democracy, the majority governs almost in every field. \nBut, in a Republic, it is the individual citizen who is paramount because \nhe is clothed with some inalienable rights which even the two Houses of \nParliament acting unanimously cannot take away or abridge.  If  they  do  \nabridge those rights and liberties, the abridgement has to be justified \nin the eyes \n\n 407 \n of our Judges who have to decide whether the restrictions are reasonable \nor not. And, Sir, what is more, the Parliament represents the will of the \nelectors of India. You make a law here in both the Houses; the President \nsanctions it. But, five Judges of the Supreme Court have the right to \nturn around and tell the President and the two Houses of Parliament that \nyou have passed a law, but you have passed a law without understanding \nthe basic law which you enacted some 60- 70 years ago; we understand it \nbetter than you do, and they have the right to strike down that declared \nwill of both Houses of Parliament supported by the hon. President. These \nare the awesome powers which the Constitution- makers conferred upon our \nJudges. But, they did so because they too were of the same opinion as my \nteachers that these Judges are agents of the divine power and they are \ndischarging divine duties, and, Sir, look at the brilliant galaxy of \nJudges we had at that time either in the Federal Court of India or in the \nSupreme Court of India. Sir, one has to recall some of those names. I can \nsay that they were Judges who could hold a candle to the best Judges in \nany part of the world. Sir, Judge like Kania Saheb, who became the first \nChief Justice of India; Judge like Vivian Bose; Judge like Faisal Ali; in \nrecent times, Justice Krishna Iyer, Justice Venkatachaliah, Justice Verma \n- these are the names to be mentioned and you get a sense of pride, you \nhave a glow of pride when you think of these great Indian Judges. But, \nSir, I agree with every speaker who has spoken before me, particularly \nwith Mr. Bhatia. I do not know where he has gone now. It is unfortunate \nthat standards have fallen. Standards have fallen to such an extent that \nit is, perhaps, true that on the criminal side particularly, judgements \nwhich were delivered 30-40 years ago by Presidency Magistrates in Madras, \nor, in Bombay, or, in Calcutta were better than the judgements which are \nnow being produced in the Supreme Court. \nAnd, what is more, it is unfortunate that some incidents have taken \nplace and evidence has surfaced which has also cast a doubt upon the \nintegrity of some of the Judges, not merely at the subordinate level. \nChief Justice Bharucha publicly declared that twenty per cent of the \nJudges in the Higher Courts are corrupt. Sir, th e people of this country \nare very anxious to know what the remaining eighty per cent of pure \njudges have done about those Judges, whom Justice Bharucha, perhaps, \nrightly described as corrupt judges. \n\n 408 \nSir, look at the records. A Rajasthan High Court Judge and his \nRegistrar sought sexual favours from a female doctor. A committee \nappointed by the Supreme Court found them guilty. The Judge only \nresigned. The Judge should have been in jail. No further action was \ntaken. Some Judges in Mysore were involved in a sexual scandal. Nothing \nserious happened. A Judge of the Delhi High Court has been formally \ncharged for corrupt dealings with property developers and the trial has \nnot yet commenced for more than a decade. The case raises an interesting \nquestion. In receiving sexual favours, does a Judge receive illegal \ngratification? It is an interesting point, which, I hope, the Supreme \nCourt will have to decide. Sir, this is the state of affairs. Sir, to my \nmind, because of the very  nature  of the functions which Judges have to \ndischarge, they must be completely made above  \n\n 409 \nwant so that they don't have the slightest temptation, slightest envy \nwith the richest persons who appear before them or whose interests and \nwhose rights and obligations, they sit in judgement upon. \nTherefore, Sir, now you have trebled their salaries; for all practical \npurposes, I think, it is little less than triple. If you had made it \nquadruple or even five times, I would have still supported it. But, Sir, \nthe support should come from a realisation of the great dignity of that \njudicial office and the enormous value of the functions which these \nJudges perform. \nI have a grievance against the Statement of Objects and Reasons of \nthis Bill. What does the Statement of Objects and Reasons say? It does \nnot talk of the great role of the Judges in our body politic. They are \nimportant but they are more important than even the Legislature and the \nExecutive combined. They are the guardians of citizens against the \narrogance and the corruption and the venality of power. And, Sir, this \ntribute should have been made in the Statement of Objects and Reasons. \nSHRI H.R. BHARDWAJ: I will do it now. \nSHRI RAM JETHMALANI: I have no doubt. Why do you think I am saying \nthis? I know your views about Judges and I know what you really feel \nabout them. But, Sir, the Statement of Objects and Reasons says that the \nSixth Pay Commission has decided to give higher emoluments to the \nbureaucrats, and, therefore, this Bill is being brought. Now, this is \nunfair to our Judges in a sense. Of course, my friend's explanation will \nput things on the proper pedestal, and, I hope, the Judges will also have \nthe satisfaction that they are being recognized by the Parliament of this \ncountry as being superior to the bureaucrats in this country, and, \nsuperior to almost everybody else in this country. \nThen, Sir, what do you do about these falling standards? Let me tell \nyou one instance of about four years ago. I have forgotten the exact \ndate. I could not collect the exact date but about four years ago, \nTransparency International' and a Delhi-based 'Centre for Media Studies', \na research firm, undertook study of corruption in India. The result \npublished the same year said that Indians pay out more than Rs. 20,000 \n\n 410 \ncrores as bribes every year and scores of public servants at all levels \nare involved. The report made a number of very wise suggestions. At the \nsame time, it made scathing observations about corruption in the \njudiciary. Speaking of the system being highly dilatory and expensive, \nwhich every Member has spoken about, it said that it provided a strong \ntemptation to use some money to oil creating wheels. Lawyers, court \nofficials and even judges were found to be  \nrecipients. I am not talking of the lawyers' normal fees. These are the \nfees for extra professional services.  So,  Sir, if this is so, and we \nourselves have made a mockery of the only method of dealing  \n\n 411 \nwith the corrupt judges which the Constitution provides, namely, \nimpeachment. Sir, Members of Parliament were active. Impeachment Motion \nwas made. It was accepted. Three of the best judges of the country were \nappointed to inquire into the charges of corruption. The Committee found \nthe judge guilty of 9 charges out of 12 which were framed against him. \nThe Motion came here. Sir, by the sheer device of asking Members not to \nvote, the Motion failed because two-thirds majority could not be obtained \nwhich is required by the Constitution. But, Sir, let us look back upon \nit. What was Parliament doing? Let us do some introspection about this \nepisode in our life why did we help a judge who was found guilty by his \nthree brother judges and three brother judges of the highest reputation \nin the country. Sir, is it not a case of Parliamentary corruption coming \nto the rescue of judicial corruption? That is what has happened. \nTherefore, Sir, something more has now to be done. First of all, I don't \nbelieve that our selection process takes into account the merit of \nlawyers. Even income tax returns are not adequate proof of good character \nand good conduct or even professional competence. There are lawyers today \nwho, I regret to say, I am ashamed of it, only do work of fixing things \nat the police stations. They get more fees than the lawyers who charge \nRs. ten lakh in court because there the amounts are absolutely phenomenal \nand merely giving the income tax return to the Chief Justice and saying, \n\"Look, this is my practice\", is neither here nor there. There are various \nbranches of our law –  criminal law, labour law, income tax law, \nintellectual property law. Is there anyone who ma kes an analysis of the \nqualification of a person who wants to become a judge? No. Sir, let me \ntell you that I am facing this almost everyday. Fortunately, now I have \nat least decided to wind up my practice. But, Sir, literally, the life \nand liberty of people including sentences of death and life imprisonment \nare being inflicted upon citizens by judges who have never tried a simple \nhurt case in all their life. But, Sir, this can by remedied by judicial \ncharacter. A judge in England was transferred by the Lord Chancellor from \nthe Chancery court and put in the commercial court. He told the Lord \nChancellor that I am not a great expert in commercial law, don't put me \nin that court. The Lord Chancellor said, \"No, a judge should know every \nlaw\", which is a myth. The law is so vast that all judges combined cannot \nknow all the law. Law has to be learnt in every particular case with the \nassistance of the Bar. So, that judge went to the commercial court, where \n\n 412 \nhe was asked to go by the Lord Chancellor. The collected members of the \nBar who were present on the first day before him, he told them, \n\"Gentlemen, I must tell you that it is not fair that I should be a judge \nhere. It is not fair to you that I should be a judge here. Let me tell \nyou that I don't know anything of commercial law. I don't know why I am \nhere. But now it is your duty to teach me law for the next three months. \nI assure you that I am a good learner and I will deliver my first \njudgement after I have received training from you lawyers for the next \nthree months. The Press picked it up. The Lord Chancellor had to eat a \nhumble pie and get the judge reverted back to the Chancery court. Now, \nthis is how the things work, Ladies and Gentlemen. I assure you that my \ncomrades, my colleagues, we don't display that character today. This \nreport says that it is the duty of the Bar to expose corrupt judges. \n\n 413 \nSir, I agree. It is the duty of the Bar to expose corrupt Judges. But, \nhow many lawyers have the courage to do so? In Urdu, they say, एक �सतम का \nदौर, �सतम के दौर म� वह अहले �दल ह� काम आया, जुबां पे नाज था िजनको, वे बेजुबां �नकले। \nआज हमारे वक�ल भाइय� और हमार� वकालत का यह हाल है। They have become sycophants \nand flatterers of some Judges than persons who will expose the faults of \nJudges. This, therefore, is not the function of the Bar today. There are \nsome Judges and some lawyers who still speak their mind and speak \nbravely. But, they are few and far between and please don't expect all \nprogress to come from that quarter. Therefore, Sir, the only solution of \nthe problem of good appointments or removal of bad Judges is the creation \nof the National Judicial Commission. I regret to say that most political \nparties had got it in their manifesto once upon a time that we will \ncreate a National Judicial Commission. I, at least, remember that the BJP \nhad written it in their manifesto at one time. That party has also done \nit. A National Judicial Commission in which the Chief Justice of India \nmust be represented which means the judiciary should be represented, the \nGovernment must be repr esented, perhaps through its Law Minister, the \nLeader of Opposition must be represented, the academic world must be \nrepresented, etc. \nSir, I will pause here for a second. For a brief few months, I was the \nLaw Minister in this country. And, Sir, some of the District Judges used \nto come to me. I freely entertained them and heard them. They were all \nseeking promotions. So, I always told each one of them that please go \nback and send me four judgments which you have delivered – one in favour \nof the citizen, one in favour of the Government, one in favour of the \naccused and one in favour of the prosecution. Send me your four judgments \nand I will make up my mind and then, support you. Sir, who does that \nthese days? Nobody. Nothing is being done. And, Sir, as I said, you have \nto look into all these kinds of things. It can only be done by Members of \nthe Bar, the organised Bar being represented on the National Judicial \nCommission. The social scientists must be represented on the National \nJudicial Commission and the academic world must also be represented. \nToday, jurisprudence has gone. One of the Members said that nobody \nunderstands jurisprudence these days. Jurisprudence is a dead, dead \nsubject today. Sir, read one judgment. And, I don't mind telling you \nwhether you treat it as contempt or not, I have stopped reading judgments \n\n 414 \nbecause they are unreadable now. That's the trouble. My juniors in some \ncases come and brief me. They tell me that these are the judgments which \nare relevant in the case. I read those. Otherwise, Sir, I don't read them \nbecause it is a headache. My friend was right. He said that judgments \nhave really become unreadable. That's true. \nSir, the National Judicial Commission, therefore, is the order of the \nday. That is the imperative requirement of our legal system and somehow, \nSir, let us start it. You want a world of peace. Please stop building \nmonuments over war heroes. Build monuments for those who have delivered \njustice, who have done good justice and who have turned out to be great \nJudges. Find out the greatest Judges  and  for  God's sake, build \nmonuments in their memories. When the Emergency took place,  \n\n 415 \none Judge delivered a dissenting judgment, Justice Khanna. The New York \nTimes and the Washington Post said that the people of India will have to \nbuild a monument in the memory of this great Judge of India. Sir, did we \ndo that? Of course, Justice Khanna came to be honoured later on in his \nlife. But, Sir, we still have not built a good monument. Please do that \nand you will get good Judges. Sir, the Bar must be invited. You must find \nout that this lawyer specialises in a particular branch. We require that \nbranch to be administered from the Bench. Invite those lawyers and the \nlegal profession must consider it a moral, professional and ethical duty. \nIn England, when a counsel is invited by the Prime Minister or the Lord \nChancellor to become a Judge, he considers it his professional duty to \nsay yes. He gives up all his practice and goes and attends the Bench. \nThere, nobody thinks that he has given up his lucrative practice. That is \nan incident of professional life. I hope, lawyers will rise to that. \nThank you so much. \nSHRI D. RAJA (Tamil Nadu): Thank you very much. Many points which I \nwanted to raise, were raised by our esteemed colleague, Shri Ram \nJethmalani. However, I would like to make a few points. At the outset, I \nappreciate the intention behind the Bill. Our judges must have comforts, \ntheir material living conditions must improve and there must not be any \ncompromise on that score. Having said that, I must point out that the \nsystem in which we live today with the Constitution which is a republican \none, the Parliament, the Judiciary and the Executive have certain defined \nroles, and they are very important components of our democracy. But very \noften we find that there are conflicts from time to time between the \nParliament and the Judiciary, and I think, here, the Parliament which \nrepresents the will of the people, must be treated as the supreme since \nthe Parliament makes laws, the Parliament represents the will of the \npeople and the conflicts between the Parliament and the Judiciary must be \nsorted out appropriately, and in any way, the Judiciary should not \nencroach upon the rights and positions of the Parliament. The other \nproblem is that justice must be given to people on time. Justice delayed \nis justice denied. In a way, justice hurried is also justice buried. But \nwe find today in the Indian prisons, thousands of people who are kept in \nprolonged confinement, prolonged detention for petty crimes or for no \ncrimes at all because they are not being tried, they are not being \n\n 416 \nproduced before the court, they are not being released, and this \nprolonged confinement, prolonged detention leads them to further crimes \nor this prolonged confinement makes them criminals, and in a society like \nours, this is very disturbing, and the Judiciary will have to do \nsomething in disposing of these cases because these people are from the \npoorer sections of the society. They are not from the hi- fi section of \nthe society. If you go to any central prison, the number of prisoners who \nare kept there for prolonged time without any trial, they are all from \nthe poorer sections of the society. This is where the Judiciary must be \nmore humane and must be more concerned to deal with these people. \nThen, Sir, the Judiciary, I think, must be with tremendous integrity, \nand Shri Ram Jethmalani mentioned  some names.  We  have  glorious  \nlegacy  of  judges  in  our  country. It is not only Justice  \n\n 417 \n4.00 P.M. \nKrishna Iyer, but also Justice Chinnappa Reddy, Justice Bhagwati, Justice \nDesai, and we can go on giving a number of names who were known for their \nintegrity, who were known for their commitment towards the people, who \nwere known for their commitment to the poorer sections of our society. \nNow, this has become questionable. That is where even the former Justice \nBharucha quoted by Shri Ram Jethmalani, 20 per cent of judges in the \nhigher courts are found to be corrupt. If judges are found to be corrupt, \nhow to fight corruption, and corruption is so rampant, and corruption is \nlike cancer in our system? How to fight corruption if judges are proved \nto be corrupt? Here, I think, the Judiciary, as an institution, is one \nthing; judges are human beings, is another thing. Judges are not angels \nthat way. They come from the same society in which we all live, and they \ndo have bias. One can say, it is a class bias, it is a caste bias. That \nis why people question the judgments also whether these judgments are \nindependent, whether these judgments are objective or whether these \njudgments have some bias. \nAnd you had also mentioned, Sir, when you spoke from your seat, about \nthe judgments on certain workers' struggles or workers' movements. I \nthink, it is transgressing; the judiciary cannot curb the rights of \nworking people; the judiciary cannot curb the rights of trade unions. And \nin a democracy like ours, 'expression of dissent' is a constitutional \nright; that I understand. In a democracy like ours, people come to the \nstreets to protest, to resist certain policies of the Government, which \nthey do not agree with. You cannot take away that right in the name of \nmaintaining law and order. Whatever examples people are giving are all \nvery strange! Maybe, in very rare cases, once in a while, trade unions \ngive a call for strike. Sir, here, I speak for my party 'Left', when we \ngive call, we always make exceptions; we don't disturb pregnant women; we \ndon't stop any sick man from going to the hospital. We allow supply of \nall essential services, supply of milk, supply of vegetables, and \neverything else. So, don't try to denigrate the legitimate strike of the \nworking class. Don't try to denigrate the legitimate protest of the poor \npeople, the working people, of this country. They have a right. If they \ndo not fight, how can they raise their demands? In a democracy like ours, \npeople have got that right. Here comes the question of the attitude of \n\n 418 \njudiciary; how the judiciary understands these struggles; how the \njudiciary treats these struggles. They cannot be treated as undemocratic; \nthat cannot be allowed in a democracy like ours. This is the first point, \nSir. \nThe second point that I must say about the judiciary is this. I am not \na lawyer like Mr. Ram Jethmalani. I am talking about jurisprudence; I am \nnot talking about criminal and civil laws, the intricacies involved in \nthese cases and how to deal with that. But, I understand this as a \npolitical activist; our Constitution has an important chapter on The \nDirective Principles'. I do not know whether the judiciary takes note of \nthe existence of 'Directive Principles for State Policy', and if the \njudiciary does take note of it, judiciary should not have allowed many \nneo-liberal measures taken by  \n\n 419 \nthe Government this time. That is where I question the role of judiciary \nalso. Had the judiciary shown its small commitment to the Directive \nPrinciples, the Government could not have gone for neo- liberal policies \nand implementation of the atrocious, neo- liberal policies, which take \naway the rights of the common people, and which, in fact, allow the \ndisparities to grow in an unprecedented manner. The Directive Principles \ntalk about wealth that it should not be concentrated in the hands of a \nfew individuals. What is the judiciary doing about that? That is where I \nquestion the role of the judiciary. If, at all, it is an independent \njudiciary, this judiciary should go by what the Constitution says and \nwhat the Constitution gives to the people. It is our Constitution. This \nConstitution provides and it, at least, guarantees justice social \npolitical and economic. Our judiciary upholds this aspect of the \nConstitution, to guarantee justice – social, political and economic. That \nis why, I question the role of the judiciary. And, there, I think, the \njudiciary needs to be more accountable; the judiciary needs to be more \nobjective, and the judiciary must be above all partisan positions of our \nsociety. Here, I think, the judicial reforms become imperative. Now, it \nis high time that we went in for judicial reforms. There, I agree with \nmany of the previous speakers; we should seriously think of constituting \na National Judicial Commission. These judicial reforms must be \nimplemented and judicial accountability must be established. Here comes \nthe question of disclosure of assets by Judges. Can Judges disclose their \nassets or not? I do not know why it has become a debatable point. What is \nwrong in the Judges disclosing their assets? There was a seminar, held in \nNew Delhi, by the Bar Association. \nOur respected Ram Jethmalani was saying that the Bar must be involved \nin the constitution of the National Judicial Commission. There was a \nseminar. Many legal luminaries were sitting there. I said, \"I am a \nlayman. I am asking: What is wrong in the Judges declaring their assets?\" \nThen everybody said, \"What is wrong in the Judges declaring their \nassets?\" Why should it be an issue of debate? Why should it be an issue \nof controversy? Why shouldn't the Judges declare their assets? If a \n\n 420 \nMember of Parliament has to declare his assets, he has to file an \naffidavit. Then, why shouldn't a Judge? We are not asking for something \nwhich we should not ask for. If you increase the salaries of Judges, we \nhave no problem. Yes, they must be given enough salaries and their \ncomforts are very important. But why shouldn't they declare their assets? \nWhat is the way out? How are you going to do it, as a Government, as the \nLaw Minister? Are they above all these things? At least, that must be \nexplained to the common people because finally it is the common people \nwho are the guarantee for any justice. They are the guarantee for any \ndemocracy. They are the guarantee for any functions of the bureaucracy. \nIf people don't have confidence in the judiciary, then the judiciary \ncan't perform. You can say that they are performing the duties of divine \npower. But when people don't have confidence  in  the  judiciary, where \nis the question of discharging the duties of divine power? That is  \n\n 421 \nwhere, I think, the judiciary must rise above all pernicious, biased and \nparochial tendencies. Our country is diverse in nature. It is a very \ncomplex country. There is a unity of different cultures and social \nmilieus. If the judiciary does not reflect and understand this, we will \nget into troubles. That is why people demand that there must be proper \nrepresentation of different sections of the society in the judiciary. \nThere are also parties in India which demand reservation in the judiciary \nalso because they think judiciary is biased. So, it is time that we \nrestored the integrity of the judiciary. It is time that we restored the \nconfidence of the people in the judiciary for which the Government and \nwe, as Members of Parliament, will have to do certain things. I think, \nwhen a conflict between the judiciary and the Parliament arises, the \nParliament must have the supremacy to settle the issue. The Parliament \nhas the will of the people to make laws and the judiciary is there to \nuphold the laws and to see to it that the law is implemented. With these \nwords, I conclude. \nTHE VICE-CHAIRMAN (PROF. P. J. KURIEN): Shri Gnanadesikan. You have \nthree minutes. \nSHRI B. S. GNANADESIKAN (Tamil Nadu): I will finish within three \nminutes, Sir. Most of the points have been covered by my colleagues. But \non some points I have some reservation. On some points, I endorse their \nviews. Since I have no time to clarify or modify or express my dissent \nfrom their views, I confine myself to a few points which I want to \nexpress before this august House. \nMy good friend, Shri Shantaram Laxman Naik, mentioned about the order \nof the Central Information Commission which was challenged by the \nadministrative side of the Supreme Court in the Delhi High Court. A \nquestion was raised: How can a High Court decide on the views of the \nSupreme Court? There is the administrative side of the judiciary and \nthere is the judicial side of the judiciary. We know that case where the \nfull court on the administrative side compulsorily retiring a subordinate \nofficer and two Judges of the same court, sitting on the judicial side, \nsetting aside the decision of the full court of the judiciary on the \nadministrative side. Therefore, there may not be any problem. \n\n 422 \nComing to the points raised by my friend, Shri Raja, he spoke of the \nright to bandh, the right to stop others. He has expressed certain \nthings. \nTHE VICE-CHAIRMAN (PROF. P.J. KUREIN): He has got the protection of my \nbeing in the Chair, though he was opposing what I said. \nSHRI D. RAJA: That was my view. \nTHE VICE-CHAIRMAN (PROF. P. J. KURIEN): That is your view, I know. I \nappreciate that. \nSHRI B.S. GNANADESIKAN: There is a proverb by Prof. Bernard Shaw. It \nsays, \"The freedom of your walking stick stops where my nose begins\". The \nSupreme Court wanted to protect my nose  \n\n 423 \nonly. When you wanted to wave the stick, the Supreme Court and the high \ncourts interfered to protect my nose. Therefore, there is nothing wrong \nin that. In short, the judges must be respected. At the same time, the \nerring judges must be removed. Freedom and independence of the judiciary \nhas to be protected. These are the three principles on which the judicial \nfoundation lays. \nNow I come to PIL. There are many issues like minimum wages, \npollution, traffic, encroachment, slum improvement, slum clearance, etc. \nPeople were able to get protection only because of the interference of \nthe judiciary. When the legislature fails to act and the executive fails \nto implement, it is the judiciary which steps in and tries to protect the \nordinary citizens and slum dwellers. Recently, when the advocates were \nassaulted inside the High Court by the police, the High Court took suo \nmotu contempt proceedings, just two days back. \nSir, I would like to make two points so far as this Bill is concerned. \nFirstly, the salary of the Chief Justice of India has been fixed at Rs. \n1,00,000. The salary of the Governor is Rs. 1,10,000. The Presidents gets \nRs. 1,50,000 and the Vice- President gets Rs. 1,25,000. If the Chief \nJustice of India is given Rs. 10,000 more, he would be on par with the \nGovernor. I do not know why the salary of the Chief Justice of India has \nbeen fixed at Rs. 1,00,000, which is just Rs. 10,000 less than what a \nGovernor gets. His salary should be increased by Rs. 10,000 in order to \nbring the Chief Justice of India, the highest constitutional authority, \non par with the Governors. \nThe second point is in regard to the LTC. Sir, only three LTCs are \ngiven to the Supreme Court judges. Many of the judges hail from various \nStates, like Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh. They come from various States. \nIf they want to go to their native places, three LTCs are not sufficient. \nSo the number should be increased. I hope the Law Minister would take \nthese two points into consideration and if necessary, would try to \naccommodate them in future. \nSo far as the appointment of judges is concerned, my personal view is \nthat there is political interference in the appointment of judges. \nTherefore, there should be no political interference in the appointment \nof judges. They should be appointed independently. How it can be done, \n\n 424 \nthe Government has to decide. Thank you. \nSHRI H.R. BHARDWAJ : Mr. Vice Chairman, Sir, at the outset, I seek \nyour permission to thank all the hon. Members who have made illuminating \nspeeches. Two former Law Ministers have spoken. Senior advocate and a \ngreat fighter for human rights, Shri Ram Jethmalani, whom we all love and \nhave very high regard for him, made vast contribution to the field o f \nlaw. I hope he would continue to make such suggestions. My dear friend, \nShri Arun Jaitley, also spoke. He has made very useful speech. His speech \ncontained very constructive suggestions. He himself was the Law Minister. \nHe knows that there is hardly any scope for a Law Minister to \nstraightaway make improvements in any deficiency  in the system. My \nfriend Shri Virendra Bhatia, who himself is a senior advocate, and a very  \n\n 425 \nknowledgeable person also spoke. Shri Shantaram Laxman Naik, a very \nimportant Member of Parliament also spoke. I may not agree with his \nphilosophies because he goes to the extremes. Then Shri D. Raja, Prof. \nKurien and Shri Gnanadesikan also made their contribution. All these \nspeeches by themselves were a treat to listen because they were very \nobjective, focussed and with a view to help in improving the system of \njustice administration. I think the debate has been very fine. I have \nlistened to every point made by the hon. Members. But we must remember \nwhat our founding fathers envisaged in the Constitution because they were \nmen of outstanding vision. Sir, I agree with my senior friend, Shri Ram \nJethmalani, that administration of justice is a divine function because \nas you travel back, in the ancient times, justice was associated with \ndharma. Dharma is not simply a ritual. Dharma is righteousness, and \nrighteousness includes justice. If I remember a famous Jewish tradition \nin the Toha, they say, \"One hour of life spent in administration of \njustice is equal to heaven.\" This is the quality; this is the high place \ngiven to it in all religions, including Hinduism or Islam. This is a part \nof Quran Sharif; this is there even in the Bible. Every year, the English \nJudges and Barristers assemble in Westminster Abbey and in the Church and \ntake a pledge saying, \"God give us the strength to serve our people in \nthe administration of justice.\" So, just to remind, it is not to \ndiscriminate, but righteousness and to restore Dharma is the \nresponsibility of judges. That is why I agree that it is a job of high \nhonour, and people of a very high quality of wisdom and scholarship \nshould occupy this position. I wish this is the view which the hon. \nJudges themselves take, that they are at once the image of integrity and \nscholarship. With that, I submit, Sir, that the power of judicial review \nwas conferred in India in the Constitution. It was borrowed from various \nsources by our founding fathers. And, I myself am making a research these \ndays on the power of judicial review. Also, people frequently write in \nthe Press and sometimes talk about it in the debates. Sir, the power of \njudicial review fertilises democracy. Without this, there will be no \ndemocracy at all. There will be arbitrariness at large, discrimination at \nlarge. I remember the prophetic word of Nehru, while intervening in the \ndebate in the Constituent Assembly on the Appointment of Supreme Court \nJudges. He intervened to say, \"The House should ponder over rules by \n\n 426 \nwhich we can appoint Supreme Court and High Court Judges, who are men of \nhigh integrity and outstanding wisdom.\" And I add one line more of what \nhe said that people, that is, the Executive, who can even go against us \nif we do anything wrong. Dr. Ambedkar also intervened to say that the \npower of appointment of Judges should not exclusively be vested in the  \nPresident of India, and he explained that this power should be left with \nresponsible offices, high offices, say, the Chief Justice of India, a \nvery high office, and the President of India, the Head of the State. So, \nthis is the constitutional position, that the President shall appoint \njudges in consultation with the Chief Justice of India. This is precisely \nthe scheme that it is not left in one hand, but it has been left in \nvarious hands. In the High Court, the process starts with the  Chief  \nJustice  of  that  High Court, and the Chief Minister and the Governor \nare consulted. They  \n\n 427 \nalso obtain reports from their agencies about their integrity, and remit \nthe cases to the Law Minister, who also consults the I.B, Secret Service \nand all that. \nThereafter, files are sent to the Chief Justice of India. The Chief \nJustice makes discreet inquiries about the quality and integrity of the \npersons concerned. Very wide- ranging consultation goes into it. I am \nproud to say that in our country, very high class judiciary has served \nthe nation. I would mention the first Chief Justice, Shri H.J. Kania, \nwhose nephew later became a Chief Justice. Then, it was Shri Patanjali \nShastri, a great scholar and jurist. Thereafter, Shri P.B. Mukherjee. \nThen, Shri S.R. Das. One after the other, very eminent Chief Justices and \nJudges have occupied seats in the Supreme Court. Similarly, in High \nCourts too, there were Judges of great eminence, and they have served the \ncountry with great dignity and dedication. That is why, Sir, we are a \ngreat democracy and a great Republic; all the three limbs of the State \nwork very hard to maintain democracy and freedom. All this goes to the \ncredit of our founding fathers and their successors. Sir, keeping in mind \nthat this institution has served the country ve ry well, and there has \nbeen no criticism either about the Legislature or about the Judiciary \ntill few years back, and if now we see some cases where the quality or \nintegrity is being questioned, it becomes the responsibility of all of us \nbecause in the analysis of the whole constitutional scheme, it is \nultimately the Legislature which has to do things, which has to set \nthings right. In 1950 itself, when Jawaharlal Nehru started zamindari \nabolition measures, the court had intervened and stayed the operation of \nthe law. But, immediately, the Parliament took cognizance of it and \npassed the First Amendment to the Constitution and the Ninth Schedule was \nadded to the Constitution to protect the policies of the Government. – It \nis a 'dominant domain' – these are the words used in the first judgement \non zamindari abolition case, in Maharaja Darbhanga's case of the \nExecutive and the Legislature to make policies for the nation. Judiciary \ncannot make policies for the nation. It is a demarcated area. The \nLegislature had always been respecting this dominant domain, that only \nthe Cabinet, represented by the elected Members of the Parliament have \nthe right to make policies for the nation. There has been no difficulty \n\n 428 \nso far as this was concerned because there have always been two views on \nthis matter, right from the beginning of democracy all over the world. \nJudicial activism and judicial restraint are two debates going on from \nthe famous case of 1883 in the United States of America and, later on, in \nRoosevelt's time, when he had threatened to pack the court. But this is a \nhealthy debate. No institution should try to ingress upon the area \ndemarcated to another institution for the functioning of a healthy \ndemocracy. We also believe in the same philosophy. If I am correct, this \nissue was resolved in the famous UP case, in Keshav Singh's case, which \nwas also the first reference by the President of India on this issue. \nThere was a clash between the Speaker and the High Court of Allahabad. \nIt was the Keshav Singh Case, and, I think, it was Justice Gajendragadkar \nwho gave the opinion of the court, and this is  what  the  Government  \naccepted,  and proceeded further and the matter was resolved. So, this  \n\n 429 \ndebate about judicial activism and judicial restraint is very healthy \nbecause the philosophy of the Constitution is not static. See, how much \nwe have changed! Up to Nehru, there was one constitutional philosophy \nthat Parliament is supreme. Later on, it slowly changed in the \nKesavananda Bharati Case when limitations were put on the power of the \nParliament, that Parliament cannot amend so as to alter the basic \nfeatures of the Constitution, the interpretation of the Constitution. \nLater on, the Minerva Mills Case cemented it, that Parliament even cannot \nconvert itself into a Constituent Assembly. You have to go to referendum. \nHow? The referendum is not mentioned in our Constitution. What I am \nsubmitting is, because the Kesavananda Bharati Case is an interpretation \nof the Constitution, the Parliament and the Governments have followed it, \nunless it is changed either by a larger review, review by a bigger bench, \nor, by amending the Constitution itself. So, there is no difficulty in \ndebating these issues in the House. \nSir, I am also proud to say that the Legislature and the Judiciary \nhave maintained great respect for each other. We have never discussed the \nconduct of sitting judges in any House, Sir, and even today, nobody has \ndone it. So, these are mutual respects of institutions of democracy, and \nIndia is very proud to have such a democracy where the three limbs of the \nState are functioning, by and large, in coordination, unison and in a \ncooperative manner; otherwise, where would have been our democracy? We \nhave been over- indulgent in protecting the power of judicial review. I \nagree with Mr. Ram Jethmalani when he said that this is an institution \nwhich is the custodian of the Constitution, custodian of civil liberties, \ncustodian of human rights. We should not weaken it because it does not \nserve anybody else's purpose. It serves the purpose of the people of \nIndia. Against what? Why do we have the power of judicial review? I have \ngone into this very seriously and, I said, right from the early days of \nthe nineteenth century till today, the power of judicial review primarily \nmeans two things. One is that laws which we pass from the Parliament \nshould not be ultra vires of the Constitution. Suppose, we are majority \nand we decide, 'no, no; we will not care for the Constitution, we will \npass this law'. There is a power of judicial review which says, anything \nwhich is void of the Constitution, they will strike it down. So, it is \nwith a view to protect the Constitution and fundamental rights that power \n\n 430 \nof judicial review is used, and it is for the benefit of the people of \nIndia. It is not a privilege enjoyed by the judges. It is a privilege of \nthe people of India that arbitrary laws are not passed, that ultra vires \nlaws are not passed by the Parliament. Otherwise, I believe, always that \nthere are no factors on the power of the Parliament. The Parliament is \nsupreme in matters of legislation. There is an extreme suggestion that \nsuppose this happens, that happens, this is not permissible under the \nsystem. There is a philosophy of checks and balances within the \nConstitution, and this is the correctional House. The Parliament is the \nfinal arbiter of all philosophical and political policies' and the \nGovernment of the day, whoever is in the Government, has to take note of \nall this. But, Sir, as I said, when my learned friend, Shri Arun Jaitley \nspoke, he has very elaborately made many points, one after the other. \n\n 431 \nOn one point he said that he did not agree with the Law Minister's \nstatement – that is my statement-that the National Judicial Council, for \ntaking decisions on the misconduct and discipline of the judges, should  \ninclude not only their own peers, but they should include some public men \nalso, independent persons also. This is not a dispute between two \ncolleagues. It is a difference of opinion and the difference of opinion \nis also because, again, this power of judicial review is there; suppose \nyou include outsiders. Again, somebody will go to the court and he would \nsay that this is an infraction on judicial independence and that it \nshould be struck down. For the sake of protecting the good intention of \nthe law, one should always take care that you do not include such things \nas may face problems in the courts. Otherwise, whatever he spoke, there \nis no controversy on the salary and service condition of the judges. \nEvery hon. Member has supported it. There is no complaint mostly about \ntheir discipline also. \nBut, two problems have really come on which I need to put the records \nstraight. Sir, right from the enactment of the Constitution till the \nAdvocates on Record versus Union of India, 1993, Supreme Court, there has \nbeen no problem at all on appointment of judges; it was the same \nconsultation process and the President appointed. It was the brilliant \nlawyers who approached the court and said, 'We have no faith in the \npoliticians.' They said, 'It should be handled by the judiciary.' One of \nthem was the former Law Minister himself, and a great Constitutional \nphilosopher has argued this. I was the Law Minister at that time. I know \nunder what circumstances these arguments were made. But, you cannot run \nthe court on the whims of lawyers alone. It is the Parliament which \ndecide it. The law was changed and the Constitution was wrongly \ninterpreted. I am saying in full responsibility. How can you take away \nthe power of the Cabinet and give it in different hands? The Cabinet \nadvises the President. Right from Shamsher Singh's case, this is the law \nof the land. Justice Krishna Iyer, one of the most outstanding judges, it \nis his judgment. I can quote the paragraph also. Later on, the Supreme \nCourt got divided 4:3 in SP Gupta's case, 1982, Supreme Court. There was \none set of judges who gave primacy to the President of India and others \ndid not agree. \nThe limited question now is: How to correctly interpret the \n\n 432 \nConstitution, that President should remain the appointing authority? And, \nthe aid and advice of the Council of Ministers cannot be taken away by \nany interpretation of the Constitution because this is the philosophy of \ngovernance in the country. Everything is done in the name of the \nPresident. But, under the Allocation of Business Rules, the President \nallocates the portfolios to the Ministers and they carry out those \nfunctions on behalf of the President. \nThis is the Constitutional philosophy and, therefore, I submit that \nthere is unanimous body of opinion now to say that this system needs a  \nreview. Now, what do we do? We had rounds of consultations from Dinesh \nGoswami onwards, he moved one suggestion; my comrade friends always  \n\n 433 \nwere behind that, perhaps; then Shri Arun Jaitely brought another law; \nthen, the earlier NDA Government appointed Venkatachalaiah Committee for \nreviewing the Constitution. So, nobody has neglected this aspect. This is \nunder continuous study. \nSir, this is the study part of it. Why people are so conscious in \ndealing with it? Because, judiciary is a very delicate nerve in the \nConstitution. If the nerve does not function well, nothing will function \nwell. \nTherefore, we should not be careless about doing things. Every \nGovernment has applied its mind, so are we applying. I made a study about \njudicial discipline and extensively from all civilised societies. \nPresident Bill Clinton in the United States appointed a Commission on \nthis very problem and that report has come now, it is available. Then I \ncollected material from Canada, they have exactly the same provision as \ncontained in the National Judicial Council, which I have put in for the \napproval of the Parliament. So, judicial conduct and misconduct, we are \nprepared very well and if there are any changes, and there is unanimity \nof views, I think, very shortly we will have in position a statutory \nprovision to deal with judicial misconduct and corruption. We are not \nsitting idle. Sir, on the question of appointment also, this is a very \nimportant area and what Justice Venkatachalaiah suggested I am tending to \ngo by that. It is replacing the present position by a National Judicial \nCommission consisting of certain persons. Now, there is a difference of \nopinion about how to man that Commission of judicial appointments. My \nlearned friend had some suggestions, Arunji had some suggestions, others \nmay have some suggestions, we will have to bring a consensus because in \nthis country, I am happy to inform, there has always been consensus on \njudiciary, which is absolutely independent of Executive. And the \nExecutive has to be very careful, I must make it very clear, executive \nactions must be backed by laws and they should also conform to the public \ninterest and they should not be arbitrary. Sometimes we feel as Ministers \nthat the courts have not done properly. But, Sir, when citizens go with \npetitions that this is happening and this is not being done by the \nExecutive and citizens' grievances are there, the access to justice \ncompelled them to pass orders. The famous case of removing the diesel and \n\n 434 \nother buses for CNG, the Government accepted it and action was obtained \nunder the environment law and all this. So, it is a continuous evolution \nof Constitutional philosophy, which is very important for a successful \ndemocracy. So, we should not put fetter either on Executive or \nLegislature or Judiciary. Let them go on and do their work constructively \nin their own areas and in good faith. So, I have invented a new system. \nEvery year all Chief Ministers meet, Chief Justices meet, Prime Minister \npresides, the Chief Justice of India meets Supreme Court Judges and \ndiscusses the matter of national importance so that our country gets the \nbest justice administration and best justice to the people and the change \nis coming. By this consensus your courts' construction has improved, your \ncomputerisation of courts has taken place. Now  recently  we  passed  the \nGrameen  Nyayalaya Act and India is coming on the top nations in the  \n\n 435 \nmatter of justice administration. If you travel outside India, people are \nvery, very appreciative of our independent judiciary and the whole credit \ngoes to the Government and Parliament because it is they who have created \na vision of judiciary and about its independence. If you curtail it, it \nwill be our failure because George Washington said, The judiciary is the \nfinest pillar of the Government.\" They do this work for the Government, \ntherefore, I am very happy that hon. Members have spoken in the best \ntraditions of our founding fathers in giving independence and autonomy to \nthe judiciary. Sir, about quality, yes, the quality of the judges should \nbe first rate. This is what Nehru said, what Ambedkar said, what Mahatma \nGandhi himself said. He was a barrister from Court of Inns. All of them \nwere barristers. Sardar Patel was a barrister, Nehruji was a barrister, \nand so they had in their vision the finest quality of judges and I have \nsaid that we have had finest quality of lawyers and judges. If there is a \ndecline in standards in public life, it is bound to affect the other \nparts of the Government or the State. \nWe must restore the quality of public life, the probity of public life \nand quality of judiciary. Quality is important in all walks of life. \nThere is a suggestion about specialisation. Sir, I feel that criminal law \ndeals with life and liberty of our people and our founding father in \nrelation to the code of criminal procedure gave three most important \narticles in the Constitution, ariicle 20, article 21 and article 22 to \nsupplement the British law which we inherited and they are very \nimportant. Article 21 governs life and liberty in a very wide spectrum. \nTherefore, it is expanding. So, we must have judges in High Courts and \nSupreme Court who specialise. Now, there is a demand from the business \nworld that we should have commercial Benches, commercial litigation. \nThen, there is patent on intellectual property rights. So, all these \ndemands are coming as India is marching ahead, progressing and shining. \nBut you must find talent. Unless you create talent, from where will you \npluck it? The talent will come out of my National Law schools which have \nbeen created by the various States and they are producing fine boys and \ngirls who will be outstanding by all standards in legal education. So, \nslowly and steadily India is achieving its high place in the \ninternational community and everybody is praising. Let us not be \npessimistic at all. Our Government has computerised courts. We have \n\n 436 \nprovided money for fast track courts as was started by Vajpayeeji's \nGovernment. We have not said that this should be abolished. We have given \nmore money. We have given more facilities, full facilities on \ncomputerisation. So, our judiciary is going to be one of the finest in \nthe world. So, therefore, when we give to judges we should give with an \nopen mind. What Winston Churchill said regarding British judiciary, I \nthink, is relevant. He said, 'we should not measure the services of \njudges in pounds and shillings. We should give straightaway whatever is \npossible within the means of our nation.' So, this is what has happened. \nI am not very particularly very keen to discuss what has been given. \nWhatever Government has given is given. Let me tell yoli, we have made a \nvery, very attractive package for judges.  Earlier  when  I  was a \nMinister in 1986 I amended the law in all aspects. Earlier, there was no  \n\n 437 \nchauffeur-driven cars for judges. Now, they have chauffeur- driven cars \nwith a limited quantity of 200 litres of petrol. Then, later on, we gave \nthem two LTCs. They are not using it on working days. We will see that \nsome guidelines are issued to the Chief Justices. Then, we increased \ntheir sumptuary allowance. Even now, Sir, I will inform the hon. House \nthat with Constitutional difficulty the Governor was already receiving \nhigher salary than CJI. But I have added so much perks. We have given Rs. \n10,000 tax-free sumptuary allowance to the Chief Justice of India. Now, \nit is Rs. 20,000 as sumptuary allowance. So, it is added to the perks. \nSo, every care has been taken that we should not deny any benefit that is \npossible within our national wings. So, I am very grateful, Sir, I \nexpress gratitude to the Chair as well as to the hon. Members for giving \nme such vital support for this great institution. Thank you very much. \nHands up, the debate goes up. \nTHE VICE CHAIRMAN (PROF. P. J. KURIEN): Now, I put the motion to vote. \nThe question is: \nThat the Bill further to amend the High Court Judges (Salaries and \nConditions of Service) Act, 1954 and the Supreme Court Judges (Salaries \nand Conditions of Service) Act, 1958, as passed by Lok Sabha be taken \ninto consideration. \nThe motion was adopted. \nTHE VICE-CHAIRMAN (PROF. P. J. KURIEN): We shall now take up clause-\nby-clause consideration of the Bill. \nClauses 2 to 15 were added to the Bill. \nClause 1, the Enacting Formula and the Title were added to the Bill. \nSHRI H. R. BHARDWAJ: Sir, I move: That the Bill be returned. \nThe question was put and the motion was adopted. \n_____ \nThe National Capital Territory of Delhi Laws (Special Provisions) Bill, \n2009 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT (SHRI AJAY \nMAKEN): Sir, I beg to move: \n\"That the Bill to make special provisions for the National Capital \nTerritory of Delhi  for a further  \n period up to the 31st day of December, 2009, and for matters connected \n\n 438 \ntherewith or incidental  \n thereto, as passed by Lok Sabha, be taken into consideration.\" \nSir, six forms of unauthorised developments are given protection \nthrough this Bill. The first one is the slum dwellers and Jhugi Jhonpri \nclusters, schools, dispensaries, religious institutions, cultural \ninstitutions, storages, warehouses and godowns used for agriculture \ninputs of produce, including diary and poultry in rural areas built on \nagricultural land, unauthorised colonies, including village abadi and its \nextension, urban villages, in addition to village abadi and its \nextension, and existing farm houses involving construction beyond \npermissible building limits. \n\n 439 \nSir, I would request the House to pass this Bill. \nThe question was proposed \nश्री एस. एस. अहलुवा�लया (झारखंड): उपसभाध्य� महोदय, म� �दल्ल� राष्ट्र�य राजधानी राज्य \n�ेत्र �व�ध (�वशेष उपबंध) �वधेयक, 2009 पर बोलने के �लए खड़ा हुआ हूं। महोदय, वैसे तो यह �बल \nस्ट��ड ंग कमेट� को जाना चा�हए था, ले�कन आज सुबह जब मंत्री महोदय ने आकर कहा �क यह �बल \nबहुत जरूर� है और स्टै �ड ंग कमेट� म� भेजने से इसम� दे र� होगी। अगर यह स्ट��ड ंग कमेट� म� चला गया, \nतो लोक सभा क� अव�ध समाप्त हो जाएगी और शायद यह �बल पास नह�ं हो स के ग ा । हमार� �वप� क� \nउपनेता श्रीमती सुषमा स्वराज जी ने कहा, चूं�क यह �दल्ल� का �बल है और �दल्ल� के लोग� को राहत \nदे ने वाला �बल है, इस�लए इसको हम बहस 